{"name":"Snow Leopard","page":"Snow_leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"High mountains of Central and South Asia","diet":"Wild sheep and goats, marmots, hares","facts":["Its long, thick tail is almost as long as its body; it wraps it around its face like a scarf to sleep.","It chases prey down steep mountainsides, using the push of its first leap to keep running for up to 300 meters (980 feet).","Unlike other big cats, it can't roar: it chuffs, mews, hisses and wails."],"lesson":"Strength doesn't need to be loud. The snow leopard can't roar, yet it rules the highest mountains on Earth through patience and quiet skill.","about":"The snow leopard is a felid species in the genus Panthera. It has a thick whitish to grey fur with black spots on the head and neck, with larger rosettes on the back, flanks and bushy tail. Males are larger than the females, and both sexes have long canine teeth.","description":"Species of large felid","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a5/Irbis4.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Irbis1983","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irbis4.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow_leopard","sci":"Panthera uncia","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":1},
{"name":"Axolotl","page":"Axolotl","group":"Amphibian","where":"Only Lake Xochimilco, by Mexico City","diet":"Worms, insects, small fish","facts":["It stays a \"teenager\" its whole life, keeping its feathery gills and living underwater.","It can regrow legs, its tail, parts of its heart and even parts of its brain.","There are far more axolotls in aquariums and labs than left in the wild."],"lesson":"Healing is possible. The axolotl regrows what it loses, a reminder that setbacks don't have to be permanent if you give yourself time to rebuild.","about":"The axolotl is a species of mole salamander. It is neotenic, reaching sexual maturity without undergoing metamorphosis, and the adults remain fully aquatic with obvious external gills. Axolotls may be difficult to distinguish from the larval stage of other neotenic adult mole salamanders, in particular the tiger salamander, or other species such as mudpuppies.","description":"Species of salamander","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Axolotl_ganz.jpg/1920px-Axolotl_ganz.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"LoKiLeCh","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axolotl_ganz.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axolotl","sci":"Ambystoma mexicanum","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":0.457,"life":null,"n":2},
{"name":"Peacock Mantis Shrimp","page":"Peacock_mantis_shrimp","group":"Crustacean","where":"Warm reefs of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Crabs, snails and clams","facts":["Its punch is so fast it boils the water for an instant and makes a flash of light.","It can break aquarium glass.","Its eyes have 12 kinds of light receptors and can even see polarized light; people have just three kinds for color."],"lesson":"Size isn't power. A creature smaller than your hand strikes hard enough to crack crab shells and even aquarium glass: what you lack in size, focus can make up.","about":"Odontodactylus scyllarus, commonly known as the peacock mantis shrimp, harlequin mantis shrimp, painted mantis shrimp, clown mantis shrimp, rainbow mantis shrimp, peacock odontodactyl, thumb splitter, or simply mantis shrimp, is a large odontodactylid stomatopod native to the epipelagic seabed across the Indo-Pacific, ranging from the Marianas to East Africa, and as far South as Northern KwaZulu Natal in South Africa.","description":"Species of mantis shrimp","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg/1920px-Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Cédric Péneau","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odontodactylus_scyllarus_R%C3%A9union.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odontodactylus_scyllarus","sci":"Odontodactylus scyllarus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":3},
{"name":"Common Octopus","page":"Common_octopus","group":"Mollusc","where":"The eastern Atlantic and the Mediterranean, from tide pools to deep water","diet":"Crabs, shrimp, clams, fish","facts":["It has three hearts and blue blood.","Most of its neurons are in its arms, which can taste what they touch.","It can change its color and skin texture in a fraction of a second, even though it's colorblind."],"lesson":"Adapt instead of resisting. The octopus survives with no shell at all, by changing color, shape and plan the moment things change.","about":"The common octopus is a mollusk belonging to the class Cephalopoda. Octopus vulgaris is one of the most studied of all the octopus species, and also one of the most intelligent. It ranges from the eastern Atlantic, extends from the Mediterranean Sea, Black sea and the southern coast of England, to the southern coast of South Africa.","description":"Species of cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Octopus2.jpg/1920px-Octopus2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octopus2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_octopus","sci":"Octopus vulgaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":1.5,"n":4},
{"name":"Red Panda","page":"Red_panda","group":"Mammal","where":"Bamboo-filled mountain forests of the eastern Himalayas and southwestern China","diet":"Mostly bamboo leaves, plus fruit and eggs","facts":["It was named \"panda\" first; the giant panda got the name later.","It has a false thumb, a wrist bone that helps it grip bamboo.","When it's cold it sleeps curled up in a tree with a back leg over its face; when it's hot it stretches out on a branch with its legs dangling."],"lesson":"Gentleness is its own kind of survival. The red panda thrives on a diet others ignore, by being patient, careful and content with little.","about":"The red panda, also known as the lesser panda, is a small mammal native to the eastern Himalayas and southwestern China. It has dense reddish-brown fur with a black belly and legs, white-lined ears, a mostly white muzzle and a ringed tail. Its head-to-body length is 51–63.5 cm (20.1–25.0 in) with a 28–48.5 cm (11.0–19.1 in) tail, and it weighs between 3.2 and 15 kg.","description":"Species of mammal in Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Red_Panda%2C_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg/1920px-Red_Panda%2C_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sunuwargr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Panda,_Gentle_Tree-Dweller_of_the_Himalayas.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_panda","sci":"Ailurus fulgens","status":"EN","mass":4.95,"length":null,"life":null,"n":5},
{"name":"Peregrine Falcon","page":"Peregrine_falcon","group":"Bird","where":"Every continent except Antarctica","diet":"Other birds, caught in flight","facts":["Diving for prey, it's the fastest animal on Earth: over 320 km/h (200 mph).","Special bony cones in its nostrils help it breathe at top speed.","In big cities it nests on tall buildings and bridges and lives mostly on pigeons."],"lesson":"Comebacks are real. Peregrines nearly vanished, and now they nest on skyscrapers; the right change at the right time can turn everything around.","about":"The peregrine falcon, sometimes referred to simply as the peregrine, is a bird of prey (raptor) in the family Falconidae known for its speed. A large, crow-sized falcon, it has a blue-grey back, barred white underparts and a black head. As is typical for bird-eating (avivore) raptors, peregrine falcons are sexually dimorphic, with females being considerably larger than males.","description":"Fastest known animal and common bird of prey","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Falco_peregrinus_m_Humber_Bay_Park_Toronto.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Mykola Swarnyk","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Falco_peregrinus_m_Humber_Bay_Park_Toronto.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peregrine_falcon","sci":"Falco peregrinus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":6},
{"name":"West Indian Manatee","page":"West_Indian_manatee","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm coasts and rivers from Florida through the Caribbean to Brazil","diet":"Seagrass and water plants","facts":["Its closest living relatives are elephants.","It replaces its teeth all its life: new molars move forward like a conveyor belt.","It can eat about a tenth of its own weight in plants every day."],"lesson":"Slow is not weak. Manatees move gently, eat plants and have no real predators besides us: calm can be a life well lived.","about":"The West Indian manatee, also known as the North American manatee, is a large, aquatic mammal native to warm coastal areas of the Caribbean, from the Eastern United States to northern Brazil. Living alone or in herds, it feeds on underwater plants and uses its whiskers to navigate.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg/1920px-Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Galen Rathbun","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manatee_with_calf.PD_-_colour_corrected.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indian_manatee","sci":"Trichechus manatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":7},
{"name":"Ground Pangolin","page":"Ground_pangolin","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["It's one of the only mammals covered in scales, made of keratin like your fingernails.","It walks on its back legs, holding its front feet and tail off the ground for balance.","When threatened, it rolls up into a tight ball of scales to protect its soft belly."],"lesson":"Protecting yourself is not the same as attacking. The pangolin's armor lets it curl up and wait out danger without hurting anyone.","about":"The ground pangolin, also known as Temminck's pangolin, Cape pangolin or steppe pangolin is a species of pangolin from genus Smutsia of subfamily Smutsiinae the within family Manidae. It is one of four species of pangolins which can be found in Africa, and the only one in southern and eastern Africa. The animal was named for the Dutch zoologist Coenraad Jacob Temminck.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Manis_temminckii_%2829645803646%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manis_temminckii_(29645803646).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ground_pangolin","sci":"Smutsia temminckii","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":8},
{"name":"Leafy Seadragon","page":"Leafy_seadragon","group":"Fish","where":"Cool seas off southern Australia","diet":"Tiny shrimp-like animals","facts":["Its leafy flaps are for camouflage only; it swims with nearly invisible fins.","Like seahorses, the father carries the eggs.","It sucks up tiny shrimp, plankton and baby fish through its long, pipe-like snout."],"lesson":"Blending in can be wisdom. The seadragon survives by looking like it belongs to its surroundings, not by fighting them.","about":"The leafy seadragon or Glauert's seadragon, is a marine fish. It is the only member of the genus Phycodurus in the family Syngnathidae, which includes seadragons, pipefish, and seahorses.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg/1920px-Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"James Rosindell","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leafy_Seadragon_on_Kangaroo_Island.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leafy_seadragon","sci":"Phycodurus eques","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":9},
{"name":"Komodo Dragon","page":"Komodo_dragon","group":"Reptile","where":"A few islands in Indonesia","diet":"Deer, pigs, water buffalo, carrion","facts":["It's the largest living lizard, up to 3 meters (10 feet) long.","Its bite delivers venom that keeps blood from clotting.","Females can have babies without a male."],"lesson":"Patience wins long games. The Komodo dragon waits quietly at its ambush spot until prey comes close, then charges at high speed.","about":"The Komodo dragon, also known as the Komodo monitor, is a large reptile of the monitor lizard family Varanidae that is endemic to the Indonesian islands of Komodo, Rinca, Flores, Gili Dasami, and Gili Motang. The largest extant population lives within the Komodo National Park in Eastern Indonesia.","description":"Largest living species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg/1920px-202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"James Jolokia (james1203)","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:202306_Varanus_komodoensis.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Komodo_dragon","sci":"Varanus komodoensis","status":"EN","mass":70.0,"length":1.5,"life":30.0,"n":10},
{"name":"Milnesium tardigradum","page":"Milnesium_tardigradum","group":"Micro-animal","where":"Moss, lichen and damp soil around the world","diet":"Algae and tiny animals: rotifers, roundworms, even smaller tardigrades","facts":["It's about half a millimeter long and walks on eight stubby legs.","In 2007 some were sent into space in a European Space Agency experiment, and a few survived the near-vacuum, radiation and deep cold.","When conditions get too harsh it goes into a deathlike pause called cryptobiosis, and while paused it doesn't age."],"lesson":"Resilience is quiet. The tardigrade survives what would destroy anything else by pausing, conserving, and coming back when conditions improve.","about":"Milnesium tardigradum is a cosmopolitan species of tardigrade that can be found in a diverse range of environments. It has also been found in the sea around Antarctica. M. tardigradum was described by Louis Michel François Doyère in 1840.","description":"Species of tardigrade","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/SEM_image_of_Milnesium_tardigradum_in_active_state_-_journal.pone.0045682.g001-2.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Schokraie E, Warnken U, Hotz-Wagenblatt A, Grohme MA, Hengherr S, et al. (2012)","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SEM_image_of_Milnesium_tardigradum_in_active_state_-_journal.pone.0045682.g001-2.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milnesium_tardigradum","sci":"Milnesium tardigradum","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":11},
{"name":"Platypus","page":"Platypus","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers and streams of eastern Australia","diet":"Insect larvae, shrimp, worms","facts":["It's a mammal that lays eggs.","Males have a venomous spur on their back legs.","It hunts with its eyes, ears and nose shut, sensing prey's electric fields with its bill."],"lesson":"Being one of a kind is fine. The platypus fits no category, an egg-laying mammal with a duck's bill, and it thrives exactly as it is.","about":"The platypus, sometimes referred to as the duck-billed platypus, is a semiaquatic, egg-laying mammal endemic to eastern Australia, including Tasmania. The platypus is the sole living representative of the family Ornithorhynchidae and genus Ornithorhynchus, though a number of related species appear in the fossil record.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Duck-billed_platypus_%28Ornithorhynchus_anatinus%29_Scottsdale.jpg/1920px-Duck-billed_platypus_%28Ornithorhynchus_anatinus%29_Scottsdale.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duck-billed_platypus_(Ornithorhynchus_anatinus)_Scottsdale.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platypus","sci":"Ornithorhynchus anatinus","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":12},
{"name":"Monarch Butterfly","page":"Monarch_butterfly","group":"Insect","where":"North America, wintering in Mexico and California; also South America, Australia, New Zealand, Hawaii and Spain","diet":"Milkweed (caterpillars), flower nectar (adults)","facts":["It flies about 1,900 to 4,500 km (1,200 to 2,800 miles) or more to mountain forests in Mexico it has never seen.","The trip takes several generations; the great-grandchildren find the way back.","Milkweed makes it taste bad, and its orange warns birds."],"lesson":"Some journeys are bigger than one life. No single monarch makes the whole trip; each generation carries it forward for the next.","about":"The monarch butterfly or simply monarch is a milkweed butterfly in the family Nymphalidae. Other common names, depending on region, include milkweed, common tiger, wanderer, and black-veined brown. It is among the most familiar of North American butterflies and an iconic pollinator, although it is not an especially effective pollinator of milkweeds.","description":"Milkweed butterfly in the family Nymphalidae","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg/1920px-Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Photo by and (c)2007 Derek Ramsey (Ram-Man)","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monarch_Butterfly_Danaus_plexippus_Male_2664px.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarch_butterfly","sci":"Danaus plexippus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":13},
{"name":"Bald Eagle","page":"Bald_eagle","group":"Bird","where":"Across North America, near water","diet":"Fish, waterbirds, carrion","facts":["It builds the biggest nest of any North American bird, up to 4 meters (13 feet) deep and 1 metric ton (1.1 tons) in weight.","It isn't bald: \"bald\" used to mean white-headed.","It nearly vanished from the lower 48 US states, then came back after DDT was banned there in 1972."],"lesson":"Build things that last. Eagles add to the same nest year after year until it weighs as much as a car: small efforts add up.","about":"The bald eagle is a bird of prey found in North America. A sea eagle, it has two known subspecies and forms a species pair with the white-tailed eagle, which occupies the same niche as the bald eagle in the Palearctic. Its range includes most of Canada and Alaska, all of the contiguous United States, and northern Mexico.","description":"Bird of prey species of North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_%282016%29.jpg/1920px-Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_%282016%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andy Morffew from Itchen Abbas, Hampshire, UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bald_eagle_about_to_fly_in_Alaska_(2016).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bald_eagle","sci":"Haliaeetus leucocephalus","status":"LC","mass":5.35,"length":null,"life":null,"n":14},
{"name":"Blue Whale","page":"Blue_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"Oceans worldwide, except most of the Arctic","diet":"Krill, up to about 4 tonnes a day","facts":["It's the largest animal that has ever lived, bigger than any dinosaur.","Its heart is the biggest known in any animal: one weighed 180 kg (400 lb).","It makes some of the loudest and lowest sounds of any animal, up to 188 decibels."],"lesson":"The biggest things are built from the smallest. The largest animal ever lives on tiny krill, eaten one gulp at a time.","about":"The blue whale is a species of baleen whale and the largest marine mammal in the rorqual family Balaenopteridae. Reaching a maximum confirmed length of 29.9–30.5 m (98–100 ft) and weighing up to 190–200 t, it is the largest animal known to have ever existed. The blue whale's long and slender body can be of various shades of greyish-blue on its upper surface and somewhat lighter underneath.","description":"Species of whale; largest animal known","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1920px-Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"NOAA Photo Library","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anim1754_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_whale","sci":"Balaenoptera musculus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":27.1,"life":null,"n":15},
{"name":"Capybara","page":"Capybara","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and rivers of South America","diet":"Grasses and water plants","facts":["It's the largest living rodent, usually weighing 35 to 66 kg (77 to 146 lb), and a few have topped 90 kg (200 lb).","Birds often ride on its back, swooping down on insects it stirs up and picking parasites off its skin.","Its eyes, ears and nostrils sit high on its head so it can hide underwater."],"lesson":"Calm is contagious. Birds happily ride on the capybara's back, because it doesn't make anything harder than it is.","about":"The capybara or greater capybara is the largest living rodent, native to all countries in South America except Chile. It is a semiaquatic herbivore that inhabits savannas and dense forests, living near and in bodies of freshwater and feeding mainly on grasses and aquatic plants.","description":"Largest species of rodents","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Capybaracropped.jpg/1920px-Capybaracropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capybaracropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capybara","sci":"Hydrochoerus hydrochaeris","status":"LC","mass":48.9,"length":null,"life":null,"n":16},
{"name":"Emperor Penguin","page":"Emperor_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Antarctica","diet":"Fish, krill, squid","facts":["It's the tallest and heaviest penguin, about 1.2 meters (4 feet) tall.","The fathers keep the egg warm on their feet for about two months in winter, without eating.","Up to several hundred huddle together against the cold, taking turns in the warm middle."],"lesson":"We survive the cold together. Penguins take turns at the freezing edge of the huddle, so nobody has to carry the worst alone.","about":"The emperor penguin is the tallest and heaviest of all living penguin species and is endemic to Antarctica. The male and female are similar in plumage and size, reaching 100 cm (39 in) in length and weighing from 22 to 45 kg. Feathers of the head and back are black and sharply delineated from the white belly, pale-yellow breast and bright-yellow ear patches.","description":"Large flightless seabird endemic to Antarctica","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Aptenodytes_forsteri_-Snow_Hill_Island%2C_Antarctica_-adults_and_juvenile-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ian Duffy from UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aptenodytes_forsteri_-Snow_Hill_Island,_Antarctica_-adults_and_juvenile-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_penguin","sci":"Aptenodytes forsteri","status":"NT","mass":0.425,"length":null,"life":null,"n":17},
{"name":"Narwhal","page":"Narwhal","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic waters around Greenland, Canada and Russia","diet":"Fish, squid, shrimp","facts":["Its \"horn\" is a tooth that can grow over 2.5 meters (8 feet) long.","The tusk has millions of nerve endings and may sense the water around it.","It can dive about 1,500 meters (5,000 feet) deep."],"lesson":"What makes you different can be your strength. The narwhal's odd tusk is a sense organ, not just a strange tooth.","about":"The narwhal is a species of toothed whale native to the Arctic. It is the only member of the genus Monodon and one of two living representatives of the family Monodontidae. The narwhal is a stocky cetacean with a relatively blunt snout, a large melon, and a shallow ridge in place of a dorsal fin.","description":"Medium-sized toothed whale species","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/%D0%9D%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%B2_%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"пресс-служба ПАО \"Газпром нефть\"","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9D%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%B2_%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%B9%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%B5.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narwhal","sci":"Monodon monoceros","status":"LC","mass":80.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":18},
{"name":"Ruby-throated Hummingbird","page":"Ruby-throated_hummingbird","group":"Bird","where":"Eastern North America in summer; Mexico, Central America and South Florida in winter","diet":"Flower nectar and tiny insects","facts":["It's the most common hummingbird in eastern North America, with about 35 million birds.","On migration, some fly nonstop across the Gulf of Mexico, a trip of up to about 1,450 km (900 miles).","Its heart can beat over 1,200 times a minute."],"lesson":"Small things can be fierce. Ruby-throats defend their feeding spots, chasing off other hummingbirds with energy that never quits.","about":"The ruby-throated hummingbird is a species of hummingbird that generally spends the winter in Central America, Mexico, and Florida, and migrates to Canada and other parts of Eastern North America for the summer to breed. It is the most common hummingbird in eastern North America, having population estimates of about 35 million in 2021.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Archilochus_colubris_-flying_-male-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"jeffreyw","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archilochus_colubris_-flying_-male-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby-throated_hummingbird","sci":"Archilochus colubris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":19},
{"name":"Saltwater Crocodile","page":"Saltwater_crocodile","group":"Reptile","where":"Coasts and rivers from India to northern Australia","diet":"Fish, birds, mammals, almost anything","facts":["It's the largest living reptile, up to about 6 meters (20 feet).","It has the strongest bite ever measured in a living animal.","It can swim far out to sea, riding ocean currents between islands."],"lesson":"Stillness is a skill. Crocodiles spend most of their time waiting, and only move when it counts.","about":"The saltwater crocodile is a crocodilian native to saltwater habitats, brackish wetlands and freshwater rivers from India's east coast across Southeast Asia and the Sundaland to northern Australia and Micronesia. It has been listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List since 1996. It was hunted for its skin throughout its range up to the 1970s, and is threatened by illegal killing and habitat loss.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/SaltwaterCrocodile%28%27Maximo%27%29.jpg/1920px-SaltwaterCrocodile%28%27Maximo%27%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Obtained from Molly Ebersold of the St. Augustine Alligator Farm","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SaltwaterCrocodile(%27Maximo%27).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saltwater_crocodile","sci":"Crocodylus porosus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":20},
{"name":"Fennec Fox","page":"Fennec_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Sahara Desert and North Africa","diet":"Insects, small rodents, plants","facts":["It's the smallest fox in the world, with unusually large ears about 9 cm (3.5 in) long.","Its ears help it hear prey underground and release heat.","Fur on the soles of its feet protects them from hot sand."],"lesson":"Turn hardship into an advantage. In the hottest desert, the fennec's huge ears both keep it cool and help it find food.","about":"The fennec fox is a small fox native to the deserts of North Africa, ranging from Western Sahara and Mauritania to the Sinai Peninsula. Its most distinctive feature is its unusually large ears, which serve to dissipate heat and listen for underground prey. The fennec is the smallest fox species.","description":"Species of fox","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg/1920px-Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Drew Avery","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fennec_Fox_Vulpes_zerda.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fennec_fox","sci":"Vulpes zerda","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":21},
{"name":"Panther Chameleon","page":"Panther_chameleon","group":"Reptile","where":"Northern and eastern Madagascar","diet":"Insects","facts":["Its tongue can shoot out more than its body length in a fraction of a second.","Its eyes move separately, so it can look two ways at once.","Males from different parts of Madagascar wear different colors: red, orange, green or blue."],"lesson":"Your colors can say how you feel. Chameleons change color mostly to communicate: honesty about your state helps everyone around you.","about":"The panther chameleon is a species of chameleon found in the eastern and northern parts of Madagascar in a tropical forest biome. Additionally, it has been introduced to Réunion and Mauritius, as well as the state of Florida within the United States.","description":"Species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Panther_chameleon_%28Furcifer_pardalis%29_male_Nosy_Be.jpg/1920px-Panther_chameleon_%28Furcifer_pardalis%29_male_Nosy_Be.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panther_chameleon_(Furcifer_pardalis)_male_Nosy_Be.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panther_chameleon","sci":"Furcifer pardalis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":22},
{"name":"Western Honey Bee","page":"Western_honey_bee","group":"Insect","where":"Worldwide, with beekeepers","diet":"Flower nectar and pollen","facts":["It tells other bees where flowers are with a \"waggle dance.\"","Workers turn nectar into honey by drying it until it can't go moldy, then seal it in wax, where it keeps indefinitely.","Bees keep the nursery part of the nest at about 35 °C (95 °F) so the young can grow."],"lesson":"Small work matters. Each bee does a small share, and together they store enough honey to feed the hive through winter.","about":"The western honey bee is the most common of the 7–12 species of honey bees worldwide. The genus name Apis is Latin for 'bee', and mellifera is the Latin for 'honey-bearing' or 'honey-carrying', referring to the species' production of honey.","description":"Species of honey bee","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg/1920px-Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apis_mellifera_Western_honey_bee.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_honey_bee","sci":"Apis mellifera","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":23},
{"name":"Great White Shark","page":"Great_white_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Temperate and tropical seas worldwide, near coasts and in the open ocean","diet":"Seals, fish, dolphins","facts":["It's partly warm-blooded: it warms parts of its body so it can stay active and hunt in cold water.","It has about 300 jagged teeth in rows, and new ones keep moving forward like a conveyor belt to replace old ones.","It can live for over 70 years."],"lesson":"Be curious, not just fearsome. White sharks often investigate with a careful bite, then let go: they test before they commit.","about":"The great white shark, also known as the white shark, white pointer, or great white, is a large shark. It is closely related to the mako sharks, the porbeagle, and the salmon shark. It is a robustly built species with a grayish upperside and a white underside.","description":"Species of large lamniform shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/White_shark.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Pterantula (Terry Goss) at en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_shark.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_white_shark","sci":"Carcharodon carcharias","status":"VU","mass":1088.621,"length":null,"life":73.0,"n":24},
{"name":"Masai Giraffe","page":"Masai_giraffe","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of central and southern Kenya and Tanzania, plus Uganda and Zambia's Luangwa Valley","diet":"Leaves, especially acacia","facts":["It has the same number of neck bones as you: seven.","Its tongue is about 50 cm (20 in) long and dark, maybe to prevent sunburn.","Its mother gives birth standing up, so the calf falls to the ground, and within a few hours it can run around."],"lesson":"Rise above and see further. The giraffe's height lets it spot danger early and reach what others can't.","about":"The Masai giraffe, also spelled Maasai giraffe, and sometimes called the Kilimanjaro giraffe, is a species of giraffe. It is native to East Africa. The Masai giraffe can be found in central and southern Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, and the Luangwa Valley region of Zambia.","description":"Species of giraffe","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Masai_giraffes_%28Giraffa_tippelskirchi%29_Arusha.jpg/1920px-Masai_giraffes_%28Giraffa_tippelskirchi%29_Arusha.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masai_giraffes_(Giraffa_tippelskirchi)_Arusha.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masai_giraffe","sci":"Giraffa tippelskirchi","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":25},
{"name":"Common Raccoon","page":"Raccoon","group":"Mammal","where":"North America, and now Europe and Japan","diet":"Almost anything: fruit, nuts, insects, trash","facts":["Its front paws are so sensitive it \"sees\" with them, especially when wet.","It can remember how to solve a puzzle for at least three years.","Its black mask may cut glare and help it see at night."],"lesson":"Use your hands and your mind. Raccoons solve problems by touch and memory: curiosity, kept up, becomes skill.","about":"The raccoon, sometimes called the North American, northern or common raccoon to distinguish it from other species of raccoon, is a mammal native to North America. It is the largest of the procyonid family, having a body length of 40 to 70 cm, and a body weight of 5 to 26 kg. Its grayish coat mostly consists of dense underfur, which insulates it against cold weather.","description":"Medium-sized mammal native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg/1920px-Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raccoon_in_Central_Park_(35264).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raccoon","sci":"Procyon lotor","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":20.0,"n":26},
{"name":"Sea Otter","page":"Sea_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Coasts of the North Pacific","diet":"Sea urchins, clams, crabs","facts":["To keep from drifting out to sea while it rests, it wraps itself in kelp.","It has the thickest fur of any animal: about a million hairs per square inch.","It uses rocks as tools to crack open shells on its belly."],"lesson":"Hold on to the ones you love. A sea otter mother wraps her pup in kelp so it won't float away while she dives for food.","about":"The sea otter is a marine mammal native to the coasts of the northern and eastern North Pacific Ocean. Adult sea otters typically weigh between 14 and 45 kg (30–100 lb), making them the heaviest members of the weasel family, but among the smallest marine mammals. Unlike most marine mammals, the sea otter's primary form of insulation is an exceptionally thick coat of fur, the densest in the animal kingdom.","description":"Species of marine mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Sea_Otter_%28Enhydra_lutris%29_%2825169790524%29_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Marshal Hedin from San Diego","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sea_Otter_(Enhydra_lutris)_(25169790524)_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_otter","sci":"Enhydra lutris","status":"EN","mass":45.0,"length":1.48,"life":null,"n":27},
{"name":"African Bush Elephant","page":"African_bush_elephant","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas, bush and forests of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Grass, leaves, bark, fruit","facts":["It's the largest land animal.","Its long trunk ends in two finger-like tips that it uses to grab things.","Its deep rumbles, mostly too low for people to hear, can travel vast distances to other elephants."],"lesson":"Remember, and grieve, and care. Elephants mourn their dead and protect their young as a family: deep bonds are a strength.","about":"The African bush elephant, also known as the African savanna elephant, is a species of elephant native to sub-Saharan Africa and the largest living terrestrial animal, with fully grown bulls reaching an average shoulder height of 3.04–3.36 m (10.0–11.0 ft) and a body mass of 5.2–6.9 t; the largest recorded specimen had a shoulder height of 3.96 m (13.0 ft) and an estimated body mass of 10.4 t.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:178_Male_African_bush_elephant_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_bush_elephant","sci":"Loxodonta africana","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":28},
{"name":"Cheetah","page":"Cheetah","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of Africa, and a few in Iran","diet":"Gazelles, impalas, hares","facts":["It's the fastest land animal: about 100 km/h (60 mph) in short bursts.","It goes from 0 to 100 km/h in about three seconds.","It can't roar, but it purrs."],"lesson":"Know your strengths and use them wisely. The cheetah's speed lasts only seconds, so it plans the chase before it starts.","about":"The cheetah is a species of large feline and the only living member of the genus Acinonyx. The cheetah is a pursuit predator and the fastest land animal, capable of running at 93–104 km/h (58–65 mph) it has evolved for speed, having powerful hindlimb muscles and a long lumbar spine. The cheetah has a tawny to creamy white or pale buff fur that is marked with evenly spaced, solid black spots.","description":"Large feline of the genus Acinonyx","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg/1920px-Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"AfricanConservation","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_cheetah_facing_left_in_South_Africa.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheetah","sci":"Acinonyx jubatus","status":"VU","mass":40.2,"length":null,"life":null,"n":29},
{"name":"American Barn Owl","page":"American_barn_owl","group":"Bird","where":"Open country such as farmland and grassland across North and South America, plus Hawaii, where people brought it","diet":"Mice, voles, shrews","facts":["It can catch prey in total darkness by sound alone.","One ear sits higher than the other, to pinpoint sounds up and down.","Soft-edged feathers make its flight almost silent."],"lesson":"Listen more than you look. The barn owl hunts in total darkness by sound alone.","about":"The American barn owl is usually considered a subspecies group and together with the western barn owl group, the eastern barn owl group, and sometimes the Andaman masked owl, make up the barn owl, cosmopolitan in range. The barn owl is recognized by most taxonomic authorities. A few separate them into distinct species, as is done here.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/American_Barn_Owl%2C_Bear_River%2C_Utah_%289637780911%29.jpg/1920px-American_Barn_Owl%2C_Bear_River%2C_Utah_%289637780911%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"USFWS Mountain-Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Barn_Owl,_Bear_River,_Utah_(9637780911).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_barn_owl","sci":"Tyto furcata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":30},
{"name":"Strawberry Poison Frog","page":"Strawberry_poison-dart_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"Rainforests of Nicaragua, Costa Rica and Panama","diet":"Ants and mites","facts":["Its bright red warns: \"don't eat me.\"","Its poison comes from the ants and mites it eats; frogs raised on other food aren't poisonous.","The mother carries her tadpoles one by one to tiny pools of water in plants, then feeds each one her own unfertilized eggs."],"lesson":"Bright can mean bold. The frog's colors are an honest warning, not a disguise: telling the truth about your boundaries keeps you safe.","about":"The strawberry poison frog, strawberry poison dart frog or blue jeans poison frog is a species of small poison dart frog found in Central America. It is common throughout its range, which extends from eastern central Nicaragua through Costa Rica and northwestern Panama. The species is often found in humid lowlands and premontane forest, but large populations are also found in disturbed areas such as plantations.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_%2870539%29.jpg/1920px-Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_%2870539%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strawberry_poison_dart_frog_(70539).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strawberry_poison_dart_frog","sci":"Oophaga pumilio","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":31},
{"name":"Red Kangaroo","page":"Red_kangaroo","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry open lands of Australia","diet":"Grasses and shrubs","facts":["A mother can pause her next baby's growth until the joey before it has left the pouch.","It can hop about 9 meters (30 feet) in one leap.","A newborn is about the size of a jelly bean and crawls into the pouch."],"lesson":"You can't go backward easily, so keep moving forward. The kangaroo's body is built to go ahead, in big bounds.","about":"The red kangaroo is the largest of all kangaroos, the largest terrestrial mammal native to Australia, and the largest extant marsupial. It is found across mainland Australia, except for the more fertile areas, such as southern Western Australia, the eastern and southeastern coasts, and the rainforests along the northern coast.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Red_kangaroo_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"fir0002 flagstaffotos [at] gmail.com Canon 20D + Canon 70-200mm f/2.8 L","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_kangaroo_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_kangaroo","sci":"Osphranter rufus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":32},
{"name":"Common Eastern Firefly","page":"Photinus_pyralis","group":"Insect","where":"Lawns, fields and forest edges of the eastern United States","diet":"Larvae eat snails, slugs and worms; adults barely eat","facts":["Its glow comes from a chemical reaction in a lantern on its belly, run by an enzyme called luciferase.","Males flash while swooping upward in a J shape, which is why it's called the \"big dipper firefly.\"","A female answers from the grass with a flash of her own, timed just right, so the male can find her."],"lesson":"Shine without burning out. A firefly makes its glow with a gentle chemical reaction, not fire: real brightness doesn't have to cost you everything.","about":"Photinus pyralis, also known by the common names the common eastern firefly or big dipper firefly, and sometimes called a \"lightning bug\", is a species of flying beetle. An organ on its abdomen is responsible for its light production. It is the most common species of firefly in North America and is typically found east of the Rocky Mountains.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"art farmer from evansville indiana, usa","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photinus_pyralis_Firefly_3.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photinus_pyralis","sci":"Photinus pyralis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":33},
{"name":"Orca","page":"Orca","group":"Mammal","where":"Every ocean, from the poles to the tropics","diet":"Fish, seals, sharks, even whales","facts":["It's the largest member of the dolphin family, even though it's called the killer whale.","Each family group has its own dialect of calls.","Each family is built around its oldest female, and orca grandmothers can live for decades after they stop having calves."],"lesson":"Family and tradition carry wisdom. Orca pods have their own dialects, and each family is built around its oldest female.","about":"The orca, or killer whale, is a toothed whale and the largest member of the oceanic dolphin family. The only extant species in the genus Orcinus, it is recognizable by its distinct pigmentation; being mostly black on top, white on the bottom and having recognizable white eye patches.","description":"Largest living species of dolphin","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Killerwhales_jumping.jpg/1920px-Killerwhales_jumping.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Robert Pittman","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Killerwhales_jumping.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orca","sci":"Orcinus orca","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":34},
{"name":"Galápagos Tortoise","page":"Galápagos_tortoise","group":"Reptile","where":"The Galápagos Islands","diet":"Grasses, leaves, cactus","facts":["It can live over 100 years.","It can go a year without food or water.","Each island's tortoises have their own shell shape."],"lesson":"Slow and steady really can last a century. The tortoise survives by conserving, enduring, and not rushing.","about":"The Galápagos tortoise, also called the Galápagos giant tortoise, is a very large species of tortoise in the genus Chelonoidis. The species comprises 15 subspecies. It is the largest living species of tortoise, and can weigh up to 417 kg (919 lb).","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg/1920px-Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Mfield, Matthew Field, http://www.photography.mattfield.com","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galapagos_giant_tortoise_Geochelone_elephantopus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal%C3%A1pagos_tortoise","sci":null,"status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":35},
{"name":"Great Hammerhead","page":"Great_hammerhead","group":"Fish","where":"Warm coastal seas worldwide","diet":"Stingrays, fish, squid","facts":["Its hammer-shaped head spreads out its sensors, like a metal detector.","It pins stingrays to the seafloor with its head.","It's the largest hammerhead, reaching up to 6.2 m (20 ft) long."],"lesson":"See the whole picture. The hammerhead sweeps its wide head over the seafloor to sense stingrays hidden in the sand.","about":"The great hammerhead is the largest species of hammerhead shark, belonging to the family Sphyrnidae, attaining an average length of 4.6 m (15 ft) and reaching a maximum length of 6.2 m (20 ft). It is found around the globe in all the warmer parts of the oceans.","description":"Species of shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Great_hammerhead2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_hammerhead2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_hammerhead","sci":"Sphyrna mokarran","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":36},
{"name":"Snowy Owl","page":"Snowy_owl","group":"Bird","where":"The Arctic tundra","diet":"Lemmings, other small mammals, birds","facts":["Unlike most owls, it hunts in daylight: the Arctic summer has no night.","Males get whiter as they age.","A family may stash more than 80 lemmings around its nest."],"lesson":"Adapt to your seasons. In the Arctic summer with no night, the snowy owl simply hunts by day.","about":"The snowy owl, also known as the polar owl, the white owl and the Arctic owl, is a large, white owl of the true owl family. Snowy owls are native to the Arctic regions of both North America and the Palearctic, breeding mostly on the tundra. It has a number of unique adaptations to its habitat and lifestyle, which are quite distinct from other extant owls.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg/1920px-SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler d/b/a Focus On Wildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SnowyOwlAmericanBlackDuck.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowy_owl","sci":"Bubo scandiacus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":37},
{"name":"Mexican Long-nosed Armadillo","page":"Mexican_long-nosed_armadillo","group":"Mammal","where":"North and Central America, including Florida and the southern US","diet":"Beetles, grubs, ants, termites and other small animals","facts":["It almost always has identical quadruplets, all grown from one egg.","It can hold its breath for up to six minutes, and walk across a riverbed under water.","When frightened it can jump 91 to 120 cm (3 to 4 feet) straight up, which makes it a danger on roads."],"lesson":"A little protection goes a long way. The armadillo's shell lets it keep exploring a world full of dangers.","about":"The Mexican long-nosed armadillo (Dasypus mexicanus) or northern nine-banded armadillo is a species of armadillo native to North and Central America. Until 2024, the species was considered a subspecies of the nine-banded armadillo classified as (Dasypus novemcinctus mexicanus). A study published in Systematic Biology reclassified it as a genetically distinct species.","description":"Species of armadillo native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Armadillo_en_Montemorelos.jpg/1920px-Armadillo_en_Montemorelos.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Ereenegee","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armadillo_en_Montemorelos.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_long-nosed_armadillo","sci":"Dasypus mexicanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":38},
{"name":"Moon Jellyfish","page":"Moon_jellyfish","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Coastal waters of the northeastern Atlantic, including the Baltic Sea, plus a few introduced spots in the northeastern US and Argentina","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It has no brain, heart or bones.","It's about 95% water.","Jellyfish have drifted in the oceans for over 500 million years, before dinosaurs."],"lesson":"Go with the flow, and endure. Jellyfish have drifted through the oceans for over 500 million years without a brain.","about":"Aurelia aurita is a species of true jellyfish in the family Ulmaridae and the type species of its genus, Aurelia. Before the 2020s, the species was considered cosmopolitan, but the taxon has since been split into at least 28 species. The species now identified as A. aurita is only found in the north Atlantic and in Argentina and is not dangerous to humans.","description":"Species of jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Aurelia_aurita_%28Cnidaria%29_Luc_Viatour.jpg/1920px-Aurelia_aurita_%28Cnidaria%29_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Luc Viatour","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurelia_aurita_(Cnidaria)_Luc_Viatour.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurelia_aurita","sci":"Aurelia aurita","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":39},
{"name":"Little Brown Bat","page":"Little_brown_bat","group":"Mammal","where":"Across North America","diet":"Mosquitoes, moths, beetles","facts":["It finds food in the dark by echolocation: it shouts and listens for echoes.","Late in pregnancy, a female can eat about 5.5 g (0.19 oz) of insects in one night.","One wild little brown bat lived to 34 years old, remarkably long for so small an animal."],"lesson":"Find your way in the dark. Bats send out a call and listen for the echo: reach out, and the world answers back.","about":"The little brown bat or little brown myotis is an endangered species of mouse-eared microbat found in North America. It has a small body size and glossy brown fur. It is similar in appearance to several other mouse-eared bats, including the Indiana bat, northern long-eared bat, and Arizona myotis, to which it is closely related.","description":"Species of mammal found in North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Little_Brown_Myotis_%28cropped%29.JPG/1920px-Little_Brown_Myotis_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"SMBishop","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Little_Brown_Myotis_(cropped).JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_brown_bat","sci":"Myotis lucifugus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":40},
{"name":"Arctic Fox","page":"Arctic_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"The Arctic tundra","diet":"Lemmings, birds, eggs, leftovers","facts":["Its coat turns white in winter and brown in summer.","It can hear lemmings digging under 10 to 13 cm (4 to 5 in) of snow.","It follows polar bears to eat their leftovers."],"lesson":"Change with the season. The Arctic fox turns white for winter and brown for summer, ready for whatever comes.","about":"The Arctic fox, also known as the white fox, polar fox, or snow fox, is a small species of fox native to the Arctic regions of the Northern Hemisphere and common throughout the Arctic tundra. It is well adapted to living in cold environments, and is known for its thick, warm fur that can be used as camouflage against snow in the winter. It has a large and fluffy tail.","description":"Species of fox","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_%28cropped_3%29.jpg/1920px-Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_%28cropped_3%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jonatan Pie (unsplash.com/@r3dmax)","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vulpes_lagopus_in_Iceland_(cropped_3).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_fox","sci":"Vulpes lagopus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":41},
{"name":"Western Lowland Gorilla","page":"Western_lowland_gorilla","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests and swamps of central Africa","diet":"Leaves, stems, fruit","facts":["It's the most common gorilla, and the one you're most likely to see in a zoo.","It can make simple tools, picking a branch and stripping off its leaves to fit the job.","Koko, the gorilla who learned over 1,000 signs, was a western lowland gorilla."],"lesson":"True strength is gentle. Gorillas are enormously strong, yet they live calm, peaceful lives in close families.","about":"The western lowland gorilla is one of two critically endangered subspecies of the western gorilla that lives in montane, primary and secondary forest and lowland swampland in central Africa in Angola, Cameroon, Central African Republic, Republic of the Congo, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Equatorial Guinea and Gabon.","description":"Subspecies of ape","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg/1920px-WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WesternLowlandGorilla03.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_lowland_gorilla","sci":"Gorilla gorilla gorilla","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":42},
{"name":"Lion","page":"Lion","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas of Africa, and one forest in India","diet":"Zebras, wildebeest, buffalo","facts":["It's the most social wild cat, living in family groups called prides.","Its roar can be heard about 8 km (5 miles) away.","Lionesses do most of the hunting, as a team."],"lesson":"Lead as a team. Lionesses hunt together: no one has to do it all alone.","about":"The lion is a large cat of the genus Panthera, currently ranging only in Sub-Saharan Africa and India. It has a muscular, broad-chested body; a short, rounded head; round ears; and a dark, hairy tuft at the tip of its tail. It is sexually dimorphic; adult male lions are larger than females and have a prominent mane that extends from the head to the shoulders and chest.","description":"Large cat native to Africa and India","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion","sci":"Panthera leo","status":"VU","mass":188.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":43},
{"name":"Common Bottlenose Dolphin","page":"Common_bottlenose_dolphin","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm and temperate seas worldwide","diet":"Fish and squid","facts":["Each dolphin has a signature whistle, like a name.","It sleeps with half of its brain at a time.","Pods often work as a team to round up schools of fish."],"lesson":"Know who you are. Each dolphin has a whistle that works like a name: identity helps us connect.","about":"The common bottlenose dolphin or Atlantic bottlenose dolphin is one of three species of bottlenose dolphin in the genus Tursiops. While formerly known simply as the bottlenose dolphin, this term is now applied to the genus Tursiops as a whole.","description":"Species of dolphin","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Tursiops_truncatus_01-cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"NASA","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tursiops_truncatus_01-cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_bottlenose_dolphin","sci":"Tursiops truncatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":44},
{"name":"Polar Bear","page":"Polar_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"The Arctic sea ice","diet":"Seals","facts":["Its fur is see-through and its skin is black.","Its big nose is built for smelling prey across vast distances.","It's counted as a marine mammal, because it lives on sea ice."],"lesson":"Patience feeds you. Polar bears wait beside breathing holes in the ice for a seal to come up.","about":"The polar bear is a large bear native to the Arctic and nearby areas. It is closely related to the brown bear, and the two species can interbreed. The polar bear is the largest extant species of bear and land carnivore by body mass, with adult males weighing 300–800 kg (660–1,760 lb).","description":"Species of bear native to the Arctic","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Alan Wilson","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polar_Bear_-_Alaska_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polar_bear","sci":"Ursus maritimus","status":"VU","mass":450.0,"length":null,"life":50.0,"n":45},
{"name":"American Flamingo","page":"American_flamingo","group":"Bird","where":"The Caribbean islands, northern South America, Mexico's Yucatán and the Galápagos, with a few in Florida","diet":"Shrimp, algae, small water animals","facts":["It's pink because of pigments in the food it eats.","It eats with its head upside down.","It often stands on one leg, tucking the other against its body to keep warm."],"lesson":"You become what you take in. Flamingos are pink because of what they eat: choose what you feed your mind.","about":"The American flamingo is a large species of flamingo native to the West Indies, northern South America and the Yucatán Peninsula. It is closely related to the greater flamingo and Chilean flamingo, and was formerly considered conspecific with the greater flamingo, but that treatment is now widely viewed as incorrect due to a lack of evidence.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/American_Flamingo_JG.jpg/1920px-American_Flamingo_JG.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JeffreyGammon","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Flamingo_JG.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_flamingo","sci":"Phoenicopterus ruber","status":"LC","mass":3.579,"length":null,"life":null,"n":46},
{"name":"Common Ostrich","page":"Common_ostrich","group":"Bird","where":"Savannas and deserts of Africa","diet":"Plants, seeds, insects","facts":["It's the largest bird and lays the largest egg.","It can run about 70 km/h (43 mph).","Its eyes, 5 cm (2 in) across, are the largest of any land animal with a backbone."],"lesson":"You don't have to fly to go far. The ostrich can't fly, but it can outrun almost anything.","about":"The common ostrich, or simply ostrich, is a species of flightless bird native to certain areas of Africa and is the largest living species of bird. The common ostrich is one of two extant species of ostriches, the only living members of the genus Struthio in the ratite group of birds.","description":"Species of flightless bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Struthio_camelus_-_Etosha_2014_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Struthio_camelus_-_Etosha_2014_(3).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_ostrich","sci":"Struthio camelus","status":"LC","mass":115.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":47},
{"name":"Gray Wolf","page":"Wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"North America, Europe and Asia","diet":"Deer, elk, moose, smaller animals","facts":["Under the right conditions, a wolf's howl can be heard across an area of up to 130 square km (50 square miles).","Every dog is descended from wolves.","Packs are usually families: a mother, a father and their pups."],"lesson":"We go further together. A wolf pack is a family that hunts, raises pups and travels as one.","about":"The wolf, also known as the grey wolf or gray wolf, is a canine native to Eurasia and North America. More than thirty subspecies of Canis lupus have been recognized, including the dog and dingo, though grey wolves, as popularly understood, include only naturally occurring wild subspecies.","description":"Species of canine","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg/1920px-Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"User:Mas3cf","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurasian_wolf_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolf","sci":"Canis lupus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":48},
{"name":"Brown-throated Sloth","page":"Brown-throated_sloth","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Leaves","facts":["It comes down from its tree about once a week, to go to the bathroom.","Algae grows in its fur, giving it a green tint.","Though it's slow in the trees, it's a strong swimmer."],"lesson":"Slowing down can be a strategy. The sloth survives by moving so little that predators barely notice it.","about":"The brown-throated sloth is a species of three-toed sloth found in the Neotropical realm of Central and South America.","description":"Species of New World mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Bradypus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Stefan Laube (Tauchgurke)","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bradypus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown-throated_sloth","sci":"Bradypus variegatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":49},
{"name":"Ocellaris Clownfish","page":"Ocellaris_clownfish","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the eastern Indian Ocean and the western Pacific","diet":"Plankton and algae","facts":["It lives safely among stinging anemone tentacles, thanks to a slimy coat.","Each group is led by one big female; if she's lost, the top male turns into a female and takes her place.","It protects its anemone too, chasing off reef fish that would eat the tentacles."],"lesson":"Find your safe place and make it home. The clownfish lives among stinging tentacles that protect it, because it learned to belong there.","about":"The ocellaris clownfish, also known as the false percula clownfish or common clownfish, is a marine fish belonging to the family Pomacentridae, which includes clownfishes and damselfishes. A. ocellaris are found in different colors, depending on where they are located. For example, black A. ocellaris with white bands can be found near northern Hawaii, USA, North America, Australia, Southeast Asia, and Japan.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg/1920px-Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Ritiks","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clown_fish_in_the_Andaman_Coral_Reef.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocellaris_clownfish","sci":"Amphiprion ocellaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":50},
{"name":"Lined Seahorse","page":"Lined_seahorse","group":"Fish","where":"Western Atlantic coasts, from Cape Cod in the US down to the Caribbean, Mexico and Venezuela","diet":"Tiny shrimp","facts":["The father gets pregnant and gives birth.","It moves each eye on its own.","It's a weak swimmer, so it hides by blending into its surroundings and ambushes its food."],"lesson":"Care isn't one person's job. Seahorse fathers carry the babies: sharing the load is how families thrive.","about":"The lined seahorse, northern seahorse or spotted seahorse, is a species of fish that belongs to the family Syngnathidae. H. erectus is a diurnal species with an approximate length of 15 cm and lifespan of one to four years. The H. erectus species can be found in myriad colors, from greys and blacks to reds, greens, and oranges.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Lined_Seahorse-_Hippocampus_erectus_%286042886100%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"MattSullivan from Boston, MA, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lined_Seahorse-_Hippocampus_erectus_(6042886100).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lined_seahorse","sci":"Hippocampus erectus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":51},
{"name":"Tiger","page":"Tiger","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia, and Russia's Far East","diet":"Deer, wild pigs, buffalo","facts":["The biggest tigers are bigger than the biggest lions, though an average tiger may be smaller than an average lion.","Every tiger's stripes form a pattern all its own.","Unlike most cats, it likes water and swims well."],"lesson":"Be comfortable alone. Tigers live solitary lives with confidence: solitude can be strength, not loneliness.","about":"The tiger is a large cat and a member of the genus Panthera native to Asia. It has a powerful, muscular body with a large head and paws, a long tail and orange fur that fades to white in parts, with black, mostly vertical stripes. It is traditionally classified into nine subspecies, though some recognise only two subspecies, the mainland Asian tiger and the Sunda Islands tiger.","description":"Species of large cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg/1920px-Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_tiger_(Panthera_tigris_tigris)_female_3_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger","sci":"Panthera tigris","status":"EN","mass":180.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":52},
{"name":"Koala","page":"Koala","group":"Mammal","where":"Eucalyptus forests of eastern Australia","diet":"Eucalyptus leaves","facts":["It sleeps up to 20 hours a day.","Each front paw has two thumb-like fingers that help it grip small branches.","Eucalyptus is so low in energy and so toxic that few other animals can live on it."],"lesson":"Rest is part of the work. Koalas sleep most of the day so they have the energy their tough diet demands.","about":"The koala, sometimes inaccurately called the koala bear, is an arboreal herbivorous marsupial native to Australia. It is the only extant representative of the family Phascolarctidae. Its closest living relatives are the wombats.","description":"Arboreal herbivorous marsupial native to Australia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Koala_climbing_tree.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Diliff","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koala_climbing_tree.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koala","sci":"Phascolarctos cinereus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":10.0,"n":53},
{"name":"Jaguar","page":"Jaguar","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests and wetlands from Mexico to Argentina","diet":"Capybaras, caimans, deer, turtles, fish","facts":["Only the tiger and the lion bite harder among cats, and its bite can pierce a turtle's shell.","Unlike most cats, it loves water and swims well.","It often kills with a bite straight through the skull."],"lesson":"Power is best used precisely. The jaguar doesn't waste its strength; it puts it exactly where it counts.","about":"The jaguar is a large cat species and the only living member of the genus Panthera that is native to the Americas. Its coat features pale yellow to tan colored fur covered with spots that transition to rosettes on the sides; some individuals have a melanistic spotted coat.","description":"Large cat native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Standing_jaguar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"USFWS","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standing_jaguar.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaguar","sci":"Panthera onca","status":"NT","mass":0.8,"length":null,"life":null,"n":54},
{"name":"Ocelot","page":"Ocelot","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and thick brush from southern Texas to northern Argentina","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, birds, lizards","facts":["No two ocelots have the same pattern of spots and stripes.","It hunts mostly at night and rests in trees by day.","A few dozen still live wild in southern Texas."],"lesson":"Your pattern is yours alone. No two ocelots look alike, and none of them tries to.","about":"The ocelot is a medium-sized spotted wild cat that reaches 40–50 cm (16–20 in) at the shoulders and weighs between 7 and 15.5 kg on average. It is native to the southwestern United States, Mexico, Central and South America, and the Caribbean islands of Trinidad and Margarita. Carl Linnaeus scientifically described it in 1758.","description":"Species of wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:016_Ocelot_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocelot","sci":"Leopardus pardalis","status":"LC","mass":13.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":55},
{"name":"Caracal","page":"Caracal","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry savannas and scrub of Africa, the Middle East and India","diet":"Birds, hares, rodents, small antelope","facts":["It can leap about 3 meters (10 feet) straight up to knock birds out of the air.","It flicks its long black ear tufts by shaking its head, to signal to other caracals.","Its name comes from the Turkish for \"black ear.\""],"lesson":"Reach higher than people expect. The caracal snatches birds out of the sky that never thought a cat could reach them.","about":"The caracal is a wild cat native to Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, and arid areas of Pakistan and northwestern India. It is characterised by a robust build, long legs, a short face, long tufted ears, relatively short tail, and long canine teeth. Its coat is uniformly reddish tan or sandy, while the ventral parts are lighter with small reddish markings.","description":"Medium-sized wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Caracal_on_the_road%2C_early_morning_in_Kgalagadi_%2836173878220%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Derek Keats","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caracal_on_the_road,_early_morning_in_Kgalagadi_(36173878220)_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caracal","sci":"Caracal caracal","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":56},
{"name":"Serval","page":"Serval","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and wetlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Rodents, birds, frogs, insects","facts":["It has the longest legs for its size of any cat.","Its big ears can each swivel up to 180 degrees on their own, to pinpoint prey by sound.","In one study in Tanzania, about half of its hunting attempts ended in a catch."],"lesson":"Listen before you leap. The serval stands still and listens first, which is why so many of its pounces land.","about":"The serval is a wild small cat native to Africa. It is widespread in sub-Saharan countries, where it inhabits grasslands, wetlands, moorlands and bamboo thickets. Across its range, it occurs in protected areas, and hunting it is either prohibited or regulated in range countries.","description":"Medium-sized wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Servalcropped.png/1920px-Servalcropped.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Diego Delso","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servalcropped.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serval","sci":"Leptailurus serval","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":57},
{"name":"Clouded Leopard","page":"Clouded_leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Southeast Asia and the Himalayan foothills","diet":"Monkeys, deer, porcupines, pangolins, birds","facts":["For its size, it has the longest canine teeth of any living cat.","It can climb down trees head first and hang upside down by its back feet.","Its cloud-shaped markings make it hard to see in the shadows of the forest."],"lesson":"Flexibility is a kind of strength. The clouded leopard goes where other cats can't, by bending in ways they don't.","about":"The clouded leopard, also called mainland clouded leopard, is a wild cat that has large dusky-grey blotches and irregular spots and stripes reminiscent of clouds. Its head-and-body length ranges from 68.6 to 108 cm with a 61 to 91 cm long tail, which it uses for balancing when moving in trees.","description":"Species of wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Neofelis_nebulosa%2C_Clouded_leopard.jpg/1920px-Neofelis_nebulosa%2C_Clouded_leopard.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rushenb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neofelis_nebulosa,_Clouded_leopard.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clouded_leopard","sci":"Neofelis nebulosa","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":58},
{"name":"Eurasian Lynx","page":"Eurasian_lynx","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Europe, Siberia and Central Asia","diet":"Roe deer, hares, rodents, birds","facts":["It's the largest lynx, up to about 30 kg (66 lb).","Its huge furry paws work like snowshoes.","It was brought back to forests in Switzerland, Germany and France after vanishing there."],"lesson":"Second chances can work. The lynx came back to forests that had lost it, once people made room for it again.","about":"The Eurasian lynx is one of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. It is widely distributed from Northern, Central and Eastern Europe to Central Asia and Siberia, the Tibetan Plateau and the Himalayas. It inhabits temperate and boreal forests up to an elevation of 5,500 m (18,000 ft).","description":"Species of medium-sized cat native to Eurasia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg/1920px-Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Aconcagua (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lynx_Nationalpark_Bayerischer_Wald_01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurasian_lynx","sci":"Lynx lynx","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":59},
{"name":"Bobcat","page":"Bobcat","group":"Mammal","where":"Most of North America, from southern Canada to Mexico, including Florida","diet":"Rabbits, rodents, birds","facts":["It's named for its short, \"bobbed\" tail.","It lives near people more often than they know, even in the suburbs.","Males weigh about 9.6 kg (21 lb) on average, and the biggest bobcats live in eastern Canada and northern New England."],"lesson":"You can thrive close to others without being seen. The bobcat shares the suburbs with millions of people, quietly.","about":"The bobcat, also known as the wildcat, bay lynx, or red lynx, is one of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. Native to North America, it ranges from southern Canada through most of the contiguous United States to Oaxaca in Mexico. It is listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List since 2002, due to its wide distribution and large population.","description":"Medium-sized North American wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg/1920px-Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Becker1999 (Paul and Cathy)","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bobcat_at_Columbus_Zoo_Boo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobcat","sci":"Lynx rufus","status":"LC","mass":0.31,"length":null,"life":null,"n":60},
{"name":"Cougar","page":"Cougar","group":"Mammal","where":"The Americas, from Canada to the tip of South America, including the Florida panther","diet":"Deer, elk, smaller mammals","facts":["It has more names than any other animal: puma, mountain lion, panther, catamount and dozens more.","It can't roar; it purrs, hisses and makes an eerie scream.","Florida's panthers are cougars, the last ones left in eastern North America."],"lesson":"Be adaptable. The cougar lives from Canadian mountains to Florida swamps, more places than almost any other wild mammal in the Americas.","about":"The cougar, also called puma, mountain lion, catamount, and panther, is a large feline native to the Americas. It inhabits North, Central and South America, making it the most widely distributed wild, terrestrial mammal in the Western Hemisphere, and one of the most widespread in the world. Its range spans Yukon, British Columbia and Alberta in Canada, the Rocky Mountains and areas in the western United States.","description":"Wild cat species native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Mountain_Lion_in_Glacier_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"National Park Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Lion_in_Glacier_National_Park.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cougar","sci":"Puma concolor","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":61},
{"name":"Pallas's Cat","page":"Pallas's_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Cold, rocky grasslands of Central Asia","diet":"Pikas, voles, small birds","facts":["Its soft, dense fur has up to 9,000 hairs per square centimeter (58,000 per square inch), for winters that drop to -50 C (-58 F).","Its round pupils (not slits) and flat face make it look permanently grumpy.","It's about the size of a house cat but looks bigger because of its fur."],"lesson":"Don't judge by the face. Pallas's cat looks grumpy, but it's just built for a hard place.","about":"Pallas's cat, also called manul, is a small wild cat with long and dense light grey fur, and rounded ears set low on the sides of the head. Its head-and-body length ranges from 46 to 65 cm with a 21–31 cm (8.3–12.2 in) long bushy tail. It is well camouflaged and adapted to the cold continental climate in its native range, which receives little rainfall and experiences a wide range of temperatures.","description":"Species of small wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Manoel.jpg/1920px-Manoel.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sander van der Wel","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manoel.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pallas's_cat","sci":"Otocolobus manul","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":62},
{"name":"Sand Cat","page":"Sand_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Sandy deserts of North Africa, Arabia and Central Asia","diet":"Rodents, birds, reptiles, insects","facts":["Thick fur on the soles of its feet protects them from hot sand and hides its tracks.","It can get all the water it needs from its prey, though it drinks when it finds water.","It even hunts venomous desert vipers."],"lesson":"Small and tough beats big and fragile. The sand cat thrives where almost nothing else can live.","about":"The sand cat is a small wild cat that inhabits sandy and stony deserts far from water sources. With its sandy to light grey fur, it is well camouflaged in a desert environment. Its head-and-body length ranges from 39–52 cm (15–20 in) with a 23–31 cm (9.1–12.2 in) long tail.","description":"Small wild cat species (Felis margarita)","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Persian_sand_CAT.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Payman sazesh","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persian_sand_CAT.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sand_cat","sci":"Felis margarita","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":63},
{"name":"Fishing Cat","page":"Fishing_cat","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and mangroves of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Fish, frogs, crabs, birds","facts":["Its front toes are partly webbed, for swimming.","It spends about half its hunting time lying in wait at the water's edge, then grabs fish right out of the water.","It even dives in after fish."],"lesson":"Use what's in front of you. The fishing cat made a living from water, which most cats avoid.","about":"The fishing cat is a medium-sized wild cat of South and Southeast Asia. It has a deep yellowish-grey fur with black lines and spots. Adults have a head-to-body length of 57 to 78 cm, with a 20 to 30 cm long tail.","description":"Small wild cat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Prionailurus_viverrinus_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Kelinahandbasket","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prionailurus_viverrinus_01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fishing_cat","sci":"Prionailurus viverrinus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":64},
{"name":"Leopard","page":"Leopard","group":"Mammal","where":"Africa south of the Sahara and parts of Asia","diet":"Antelope, deer, monkeys, almost anything","facts":["It can haul prey heavier than itself up into a tree.","Its spots are rosettes: rings around a lighter center.","It lives in more kinds of places than any other big cat: rainforest, desert, mountains, even the edges of cities."],"lesson":"Keep what you've earned safe. The leopard carries its meal into a tree, out of reach of lions and hyenas.","about":"The leopard is one of the five extant cat species in the genus Panthera. It has a pale yellowish to dark golden fur with dark spots grouped in rosettes. Its body is slender and muscular, reaching a length of 92–183 cm (36–72 in) with a 66–102 cm (26–40 in) long tail and a shoulder height of 60–70 cm (24–28 in).","description":"Species of cat native to Africa and Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/African_leopard_male_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-African_leopard_male_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sumeet Moghe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African_leopard_male_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopard","sci":"Panthera pardus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":65},
{"name":"Maned Wolf","page":"Maned_wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and savannas of central South America","diet":"Fruit (especially the \"wolf apple\"), rodents, birds","facts":["It isn't a wolf or a fox; it's the only species in its genus.","Its very long legs let it see over tall grass.","Its urine has a strong smell, a lot like hops or cannabis, which earned it the nickname \"skunk wolf.\""],"lesson":"It's fine to be your own thing. The maned wolf looks like a fox on stilts, and it's the only species in its genus.","about":"The maned wolf is a large canine of South America. It is found in Argentina, Brazil, Bolivia, Peru, and Paraguay, and is almost locally extinct in Uruguay. Its markings resemble those of a red fox but it is neither a fox nor a wolf.","description":"Species of carnivore","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg/1920px-Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jonathan Wilkins","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobo_Guar%C3%A1_andando.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maned_wolf","sci":"Chrysocyon brachyurus","status":"NT","mass":23.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":66},
{"name":"African Wild Dog","page":"African_wild_dog","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of southern and eastern Africa","diet":"Impalas, gazelles, wildebeest calves","facts":["Its chases often end in a kill more than 60% of the time, though one big review found an average of about 31%.","Pack members \"vote\" on when to go hunting by sneezing.","Each dog's patchy coat pattern is unique."],"lesson":"A team that shares beats a team of stars. Wild dogs let their pups eat first at a kill, and bring food back up to share with grown-ups in the pack too.","about":"The African wild dog, also called painted dog and Cape hunting dog, is a wild canine native to sub-Saharan Africa. It is the largest wild canine in Africa and the only extant member of the genus Lycaon, which is distinguished from Canis by dentition highly specialised for a hypercarnivorous diet and by a lack of dewclaws.","description":"Species of canine","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg/1920px-African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"AfricanConservation","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:African_Wild_Dog_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_wild_dog","sci":"Lycaon pictus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":67},
{"name":"Dhole","page":"Dhole","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Deer, wild pigs","facts":["It whistles to keep its pack together in thick forest.","A pack can take down prey ten times the size of one dhole.","It's sometimes called the \"whistling dog.\""],"lesson":"Stay in touch. Dholes whistle to each other so the pack never loses anyone in the forest.","about":"The dhole is a canid native to South, East and Southeast Asia. The sole member of the genus Cuon, it is anatomically distinguished from members of the genus Canis in several aspects: its skull is convex rather than concave in profile, it lacks a third lower molar, and the upper molars possess only a single cusp as opposed to between two and four.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Dhole_%28Asiatic_wild_dog%29_cropped.jpg/1920px-Dhole_%28Asiatic_wild_dog%29_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Davidvraju","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhole_(Asiatic_wild_dog)_cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhole","sci":"Cuon alpinus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":68},
{"name":"Red Fox","page":"Red_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Across the Northern Hemisphere and Australia","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, birds, fruit, scraps","facts":["It's the most widespread wild carnivore on Earth.","It may use the Earth's magnetic field to aim its pounce.","Its hearing is so sharp it can hear a mouse squeak about 100 meters (330 feet) away."],"lesson":"Curiosity takes you far. The red fox's willingness to try new places made it the most widespread carnivore in the world.","about":"The red fox is the largest of the true foxes, and the most widely distributed member of the order Carnivora. It is present across the entire Northern Hemisphere, including most of North America, Europe and Asia, as well as parts of North Africa.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"ClaudiaTen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_red_fox_in_Rautas_fj%C3%A4llurskog_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_fox","sci":"Vulpes vulpes","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":0.425,"life":5.0,"n":69},
{"name":"Bat-eared Fox","page":"Bat-eared_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry grasslands of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Termites, beetles, other insects","facts":["Its huge ears, 11 to 13 cm (4.3 to 5.1 in) long, help it find insects by sound and also help keep it cool.","It has more teeth than almost any other mammal with a placenta: 46 to 50.","Termites make up most of its food."],"lesson":"Small things add up. The bat-eared fox lives almost entirely on termites, eaten by the thousand.","about":"The bat-eared fox is a species of fox found on the African savanna. It is the only extant species of the genus Otocyon, and is the sister species to all other members of the tribe Vulpini, not genetically being a true fox. Fossil records indicate this canid first appeared during the middle Pleistocene.","description":"Species of carnivorous mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg/1920px-Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otocyon_megalotis_-_Etosha_2014.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bat-eared_fox","sci":"Otocyon megalotis","status":"LC","mass":4.15,"length":null,"life":null,"n":70},
{"name":"Ethiopian Wolf","page":"Ethiopian_wolf","group":"Mammal","where":"High mountain meadows of Ethiopia","diet":"Giant mole rats and other rodents","facts":["It's the rarest wild dog in Africa: fewer than 500 are left.","It hunts alone, but lives and patrols in a family pack.","It lives above about 3,000 meters (10,000 feet)."],"lesson":"You can be independent and still belong. Ethiopian wolves hunt alone but come home to their pack every day.","about":"The Ethiopian wolf, also called the red jackal, the Simien jackal or Simien fox, is a canine native to the Ethiopian Highlands, including the Simien Mountains. In southeastern Ethiopia, it is also known as the horse jackal. It is similar to the coyote in size and build, and is distinguished by its long and narrow skull, and its red and white fur.","description":"Canine native to Ethiopian Highlands","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Ethiopian_wolf_%28Canis_simensis_citernii%29.jpg/1920px-Ethiopian_wolf_%28Canis_simensis_citernii%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethiopian_wolf_(Canis_simensis_citernii).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopian_wolf","sci":"Canis simensis","status":"EN","mass":19.0,"length":1.01,"life":null,"n":71},
{"name":"Coyote","page":"Coyote","group":"Mammal","where":"All of North America and Central America, including cities","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, fruit, insects, scraps","facts":["It spread across North America as wolves were wiped out, and urban coyotes are now common in many cities.","Its scientific name, Canis latrans, means \"barking dog.\"","When pack members get back together, they greet each other with a \"group yip howl.\""],"lesson":"Adapt, don't retreat. Coyotes didn't shrink back as cities grew; they learned to live in them.","about":"The coyote is a species of canine also known as the American jackal, prairie wolf, and brush wolf. It is native to North America, and it is smaller than its close relative, the gray wolf, and slightly smaller than the closely related eastern wolf and red wolf. It fills much of the same ecological niche as the golden jackal does in Eurasia, but the coyote is generally larger.","description":"Species of canine native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote","sci":"Canis latrans","status":"LC","mass":0.263,"length":null,"life":null,"n":72},
{"name":"Gray Fox","page":"Gray_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"North and Central America, including Florida","diet":"Rodents, rabbits, fruit, insects","facts":["It's one of the only dogs that can climb trees, scrambling up bare trunks as high as 18 meters (59 feet) with its hooked claws.","It's an ancient kind of dog: the oldest gray fox fossils are about 3.6 million years old.","Its den can be in a hollow tree as high as 9 meters (30 feet) off the ground."],"lesson":"Know your escape route. The gray fox climbs out of trouble instead of outrunning it.","about":"The gray fox, or grey fox, is an omnivorous mammal of the family Canidae, widespread throughout North America and Central America. This species and its only congener, the diminutive island fox of the California Channel Islands, are the only living members of the genus Urocyon, which is considered to be genetically sister to all other living canids. Its species name cinereoargenteus means \"ashen silver\".","description":"Species of canid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Gray_fox.jpg/1920px-Gray_fox.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"VJAnderson","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray_fox.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_fox","sci":"Urocyon cinereoargenteus","status":"LC","mass":0.086,"length":null,"life":null,"n":73},
{"name":"Giant Panda","page":"Giant_panda","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountain bamboo forests of central China","diet":"Bamboo, about 99% of its diet","facts":["It eats about 9 to 14 kg (20 to 31 lb) of bamboo shoots a day, because bamboo gives it so little energy.","It has a \"false thumb,\" a wrist bone that helps it grip bamboo.","It was moved from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" in 2016, thanks to protection."],"lesson":"Protection works. The giant panda's numbers grew once its forests were saved.","about":"The giant panda, also known as the panda bear or simply panda, is a bear species endemic to China. It is characterised by its white coat with black patches around the eyes, ears, legs and shoulders. Its body is rotund; adult individuals weigh 100 to 115 kg and are typically 1.2 to 1.9 m long.","description":"Species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Grosser_Panda.JPG/1920px-Grosser_Panda.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"J. Patrick Fischer","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grosser_Panda.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_panda","sci":"Ailuropoda melanoleuca","status":"VU","mass":117.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":74},
{"name":"Sun Bear","page":"Sun_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Tropical forests of Southeast Asia","diet":"Insects, honey, fruit","facts":["It's the smallest bear.","It sticks out its extra-long tongue to lick honey and insects out of trees.","The patch on its chest varies from bear to bear, from orange to cream or white, and a few bears have none."],"lesson":"Small can be mighty. The sun bear is the smallest bear, and still climbs higher and digs harder than most.","about":"The sun bear is a bear species in the family Ursidae found in the tropical forests of Southeast Asia. It is the only species in the genus Helarctos and the smallest bear species, standing nearly 70 cm (28 in) at the shoulder and weighing 25–65 kg (55–143 lb). It is stockily built, with large paws, strongly curved claws, small, rounded ears and a short snout.","description":"Tropical species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Sun-bear.jpg/1920px-Sun-bear.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rushenb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sun-bear.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_bear","sci":"Helarctos malayanus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":75},
{"name":"Spectacled Bear","page":"Spectacled_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Cloud forests of the Andes, from Venezuela to northwestern Argentina","diet":"Bromeliads, fruit, cactus, sometimes meat","facts":["It's the only bear in South America.","The pale rings around its eyes look like glasses, and each bear's are different.","It builds platforms of branches in trees to rest and eat in."],"lesson":"Make yourself at home wherever you are. The spectacled bear builds its own resting spots high in the trees.","about":"The spectacled bear, also known as the Andean bear, is a species of bear native to the Andes Mountains in northern and western South America. It is the only living species of bear native to South America, and the last remaining short-faced bear. Unlike other omnivorous bears, the diet of the spectacled bear is mostly herbivorous.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg/1920px-Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Wilfredor","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremarctos_ornatus_in_Jardim_Zool%C3%B3gico_de_Curitiba.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectacled_bear","sci":"Tremarctos ornatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":76},
{"name":"Grizzly Bear","page":"Grizzly_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountains and forests of western North America","diet":"Roots, berries, salmon, moths, carrion","facts":["Its shoulder hump is pure muscle, for digging.","It uses its keen sense of smell to track down carcasses to eat.","Before winter it can put on about 180 kg (400 lb) in a feeding binge."],"lesson":"Prepare in the good times. Grizzlies eat all autumn so they can rest all winter.","about":"The grizzly bear, also known as the North American brown bear or simply grizzly, is a population or subspecies of the brown bear inhabiting North America.","description":"Subspecies of brown bear","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jean Beaufort","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GrizzlyBearJeanBeaufort.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grizzly_bear","sci":"Ursus arctos horribilis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":77},
{"name":"American Black Bear","page":"American_black_bear","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests across North America, including the Florida black bear","diet":"Berries, nuts, insects, plants","facts":["Despite the name, it can be black, brown, cinnamon, blond, or even white.","It's an expert tree climber.","Most of what it eats is plants."],"lesson":"Don't judge by a name. Black bears come in half a dozen colors.","about":"The American black bear, or simply black bear, is a species of medium-sized bear which is endemic to North America. It is the continent's smallest and most widely distributed bear species. It is an omnivore, with a diet varying greatly depending on season and location.","description":"Species of bear","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg/1920px-Vancouver_Island_black_bear_%28Ursus_americanus_vancouveri%29_Tofino_4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_Island_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_vancouveri)_Tofino_4.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_black_bear","sci":"Ursus americanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":78},
{"name":"Honey Badger","page":"Honey_badger","group":"Mammal","where":"Africa, the Middle East and India","diet":"Almost anything: rodents, snakes, insects, honey","facts":["Its loose, thick skin lets it twist around and bite even when grabbed.","It kills and eats venomous snakes, even black mambas and cobras.","When cornered it fights fiercely, and has reportedly even driven off lions and hyenas."],"lesson":"Courage beats size. The honey badger stands its ground against animals many times bigger.","about":"The honey badger, also known as the ratel, is a mammal widely distributed across Africa, Southwest Asia, and the Indian subcontinent. It is the sole living representative of the Mellivorinae subfamily of the Mustelidae.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Honey_Badger.jpg/1920px-Honey_Badger.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sumeet Moghe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Honey_Badger.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_badger","sci":"Mellivora capensis","status":"LC","mass":12.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":79},
{"name":"Wolverine","page":"Wolverine","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern forests and tundra of North America, Europe and Asia","diet":"Carrion, small mammals, roots, berries","facts":["It's the largest land member of the weasel family.","One young male tagged in Wyoming wandered more than 1,300 km (800 miles), all the way to North Dakota.","It can crunch frozen meat and bone with its powerful jaws."],"lesson":"Persistence goes a long way. The wolverine keeps moving through snow other animals give up on.","about":"The wolverine, also called the carcajou or quickhatch, is the largest land-dwelling member of the family Mustelidae. It is a muscular carnivore and a solitary animal. The wolverine has a reputation for ferocity and strength out of proportion to its size, with the documented ability to kill prey many times larger than itself.","description":"Species of the family Mustelidae","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/J%C3%A4rv1.jpg/1920px-J%C3%A4rv1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Esquilo","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A4rv1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolverine","sci":"Gulo gulo","status":"LC","mass":14.8,"length":null,"life":null,"n":80},
{"name":"Giant Otter","page":"Giant_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers and lakes of the Amazon and Pantanal","diet":"Fish, especially piranhas and catfish","facts":["It's the longest otter, and the longest member of the weasel family, reaching up to 1.8 meters (5.9 feet).","Each has a unique cream-colored throat patch.","Families defend their territory together, and team up to tackle bigger prey like young caimans."],"lesson":"A loud family is a strong family. Giant otters call to each other constantly, and stick together against danger.","about":"The giant otter or giant river otter is a South American carnivorous mammal. It is the longest member of the weasel family, Mustelidae, reaching up to 1.8 m. Atypical of mustelids, the giant otter is a social species, with family groups typically supporting three to eight members.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Giant_otters_%28Pteronura_brasiliensis%29.jpg/1920px-Giant_otters_%28Pteronura_brasiliensis%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_otters_(Pteronura_brasiliensis).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_otter","sci":"Pteronura brasiliensis","status":"EN","mass":29.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":81},
{"name":"North American River Otter","page":"North_American_river_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers, lakes and coasts of North America, including Florida","diet":"Fish, crayfish, frogs","facts":["Its long, thin whiskers help it find prey in dark water.","It slides down muddy and snowy banks, apparently for fun.","A see-through inner eyelid protects its eyes while it swims."],"lesson":"Play is part of a good life. River otters slide down banks again and again, just because.","about":"The North American river otter, also known as the northern river otter and river otter, is a semiaquatic mammal that is endemic to the North American continent throughout most of Canada and along the coasts of the United States and its inland waterways. An adult North American river otter can weigh between 5 and 14 kilograms (11–31 lb).","description":"Species of semi-aquatic mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_%2822802102984%29.jpg/1920px-Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_%2822802102984%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"USFWS Mountain-Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_River_Otter_on_Seedskadee_NWR_(22802102984).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_American_river_otter","sci":"Lontra canadensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":82},
{"name":"Black-footed Ferret","page":"Black-footed_ferret","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands of the western United States","diet":"Prairie dogs, about 90% of its diet","facts":["It was declared extinct in 1979, until a small wild group turned up near Meeteetse, Wyoming, in 1981.","Every black-footed ferret alive today descends from just seven of the 18 that were rescued then.","In 2020, a clone called Elizabeth Ann was born, the first North American endangered species ever cloned."],"lesson":"It's never too late to try. Black-footed ferrets came back from 18 survivors.","about":"The black-footed ferret, also known as the American polecat or prairie dog hunter, is a mustelid species native to central North America. It is roughly the size of a mink and is similar in appearance to the European polecat and the Asian steppe polecat. It is largely nocturnal and solitary, except when breeding or raising litters.","description":"Species of carnivore","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Mustela_nigripes_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"USFWS Mountain Prairie","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mustela_nigripes_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-footed_ferret","sci":"Mustela nigripes","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":83},
{"name":"Stoat","page":"Stoat","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern Europe, Asia and North America","diet":"Rabbits, voles, mice","facts":["In the far north its coat turns white in winter, when it's called an ermine.","It \"dances\" in leaps and twists, which may hypnotize rabbits.","It can take on prey much bigger than itself."],"lesson":"Confidence is half the battle. The stoat takes on rabbits far bigger than itself.","about":"The stoat, also known as the Eurasian ermine or ermine, is a species of mustelid native to Eurasia and the northern regions of North America. Because of its wide circumpolar distribution, it is listed as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List. The name ermine is used especially in its pure white winter coat of the stoat or its fur.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/R%C3%B8yskatt_%28Mustela_erminea_erminea%29%2C_Lista%2C_Norway.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Marton Berntsen, crop by User:MPF","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B8yskatt_(Mustela_erminea_erminea),_Lista,_Norway.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoat","sci":"Mustela erminea","status":"LC","mass":0.219,"length":0.247,"life":5.0,"n":84},
{"name":"Spotted Hyena","page":"Spotted_hyena","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and woodlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Wildebeest, zebras, antelope, carrion","facts":["It hunts most of its own food; it isn't just a scavenger.","Females are larger and lead the clan.","Its loud \"who-oop\" call and its \"laugh\" are among the best-known sounds of Africa, and each hyena's whoop is its own."],"lesson":"Don't believe your reputation. Hyenas are known as scavengers, yet they hunt most of what they eat.","about":"The spotted hyena, also known as the laughing hyena, is a hyena species, currently classed as the sole extant member of the genus Crocuta, native to sub-Saharan Africa. It is listed as being of least concern by the IUCN due to its widespread range and large numbers estimated between 27,000 and 47,000 individuals.","description":"Species of hyena","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Spotted_hyena_%28Crocuta_crocuta%29.jpg/1920px-Spotted_hyena_%28Crocuta_crocuta%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spotted_hyena_(Crocuta_crocuta).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spotted_hyena","sci":"Crocuta crocuta","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":85},
{"name":"Meerkat","page":"Meerkat","group":"Mammal","where":"Deserts and dry grasslands of southern Africa","diet":"Insects, scorpions, lizards","facts":["One meerkat stands guard while the others forage, and calls out a warning.","It's immune to some scorpion venom.","When digging a burrow, meerkats line up head to tail and toss the soil back between their legs."],"lesson":"Take turns looking out for each other. Meerkats eat in peace because one of them always keeps watch.","about":"The meerkat or suricate is a small mongoose found in southern Africa. It is characterised by a broad head, large eyes, a pointed snout, long legs, a thin tapering tail, and a brindled coat pattern. The head-and-body length is around 24–35 cm (9.4–13.8 in), and the weight is typically between 0.62 and 0.97 kg.","description":"Species of mongoose from Southern Africa","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Meerkat_%28Suricata_suricatta%29_Tswalu.jpg/1920px-Meerkat_%28Suricata_suricatta%29_Tswalu.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meerkat_(Suricata_suricatta)_Tswalu.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerkat","sci":"Suricata suricatta","status":"LC","mass":0.725,"length":null,"life":null,"n":86},
{"name":"Binturong","page":"Binturong","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Fruit, especially figs; small animals","facts":["It smells like buttered popcorn.","Its long, bushy tail can grip branches, and it uses it to climb and keep its balance.","It plants fig trees by spreading their seeds."],"lesson":"Leave things better than you found them. The binturong plants forests just by eating its lunch.","about":"The binturong, also known as the bearcat, is an arboreal viverrid native to South and Southeast Asia. It is the only species in the genus Arctictis, and it is divided into nine subspecies. It has long, thick hair, and is primarily dark in appearance, but can also have a whitish speckled pelage.","description":"Asian mammal also known as a bearcat","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Binturong_in_Overloon.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Tassilo Rau","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Binturong_in_Overloon.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binturong","sci":"Arctictis binturong","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":87},
{"name":"Fossa","page":"Fossa_(animal)","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Madagascar","diet":"Lemurs, rodents, birds","facts":["It's Madagascar's largest predator.","It looks like a cat but is related to mongooses.","It can climb down trees head first, and even leap from tree to tree, thanks to its very flexible ankles."],"lesson":"You don't have to be what you look like. The fossa looks like a cat, but it's something else entirely.","about":"The fossa is a slender, long-tailed, cat-like mammal that is endemic to Madagascar. It is a member of the carnivoran family Eupleridae.","description":"Cat-like species of mammal from Madagascar","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Cryptoprocta_Ferox.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ran Kirlian","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cryptoprocta_Ferox.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossa_(animal)","sci":"Cryptoprocta ferox","status":"VU","mass":9.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":88},
{"name":"White-nosed Coati","page":"White-nosed_coati","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests from Arizona to Colombia","diet":"Insects, fruit, eggs, small animals","facts":["It holds its long, ringed tail straight up as it walks.","Females and their young live together in groups called bands, while grown males mostly live alone.","Its long, flexible snout sniffs out insects under leaves."],"lesson":"Follow your nose. The coati finds its food by sniffing out what others walk past.","about":"The white-nosed coati, also known as the coatimundi, is a species of coati and a member of the family Procyonidae. Local Spanish names for the species include antoon, gato solo, pizote, and tejón, depending upon the region. It weighs about 4–6 kg (8.8–13.2 lb), and the nose-to-tail length of the species is about 110 cm (3.6 ft) with about half of that being the tail length.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png/1920px-WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler, Focus On Wildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WNCoati_Alajuela_CR.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White-nosed_coati","sci":"Nasua narica","status":"LC","mass":0.18,"length":null,"life":null,"n":89},
{"name":"Kinkajou","page":"Kinkajou","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Fruit (especially figs), flowers, nectar","facts":["Its slender tongue can stretch about 13 cm (5 in) to lick nectar from flowers and scoop out soft fruit.","Its tail can grip branches like a hand.","It can turn its back feet backward to run either way along a branch."],"lesson":"Sweetness has its place. The kinkajou lives on fruit and nectar, and pollinates flowers as it goes.","about":"The kinkajou is a mammal of the family Procyonidae, a relative to olingos, coatis, raccoons, and the ringtail and cacomistle. It is the only member of the genus Potos and is also known as the honey bear. Though kinkajous are arboreal, they are not closely related to any other tree-dwelling mammal group.","description":"South American mammal (Potos flavus)","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Potos_flavus_%288973438737%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Dick Culbert from Gibsons, B.C., Canada","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potos_flavus_(8973438737).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinkajou","sci":"Potos flavus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":90},
{"name":"Striped Skunk","page":"Striped_skunk","group":"Mammal","where":"Most of North America","diet":"Insects, grubs, small animals, fruit","facts":["It can spray its oily, yellow musk several meters, and the smell has been compared to garlic, burning sulfur and sewer gas.","It warns first: it stamps its feet and raises its tail before spraying.","It tears apart rotting logs with its sharp claws to find grubs."],"lesson":"Give a warning before you strike back. The skunk always stamps and hisses first.","about":"The striped skunk is a skunk of the genus Mephitis that occurs across much of North America, including southern Canada, the United States, and northern Mexico. It is currently listed as least concern by the IUCN on account of its wide range and ability to adapt to human-modified environments.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg/1920px-Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Wallace Keck","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skunk_about_to_spray.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Striped_skunk","sci":"Mephitis mephitis","status":"LC","mass":0.034,"length":null,"life":null,"n":91},
{"name":"European Badger","page":"European_badger","group":"Mammal","where":"Woodlands and fields across Europe","diet":"Earthworms, insects, fruit, roots","facts":["Families live in \"setts,\" underground tunnels used for decades and passed down from generation to generation.","Earthworms are its most important food, ahead of insects, fruit and small animals.","It changes its bedding regularly, dragging in fresh grass and leaves."],"lesson":"Build something that lasts. Badger setts are handed down for generations.","about":"The European badger, also known as the Eurasian badger, is a species of badger in the family Mustelidae native to Europe and West Asia and parts of Central Asia. It is classified as least concern on the IUCN Red List due to its wide range and large, stable population size which is thought to be increasing in some regions. Several subspecies are recognised, with the nominate subspecies predominating in most of Europe.","description":"Species of mustelid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/European_badger_%28Meles_meles_taxus%29_Drenthe.jpg/1920px-European_badger_%28Meles_meles_taxus%29_Drenthe.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_badger_(Meles_meles_taxus)_Drenthe.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_badger","sci":"Meles meles","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":92},
{"name":"Asian Small-clawed Otter","page":"Asian_small-clawed_otter","group":"Mammal","where":"Wetlands and rivers of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Crabs, shellfish, fish","facts":["It's the smallest otter.","Its claws are so short that they don't reach past the pads of its webbed fingers.","Families are close and chatty, with at least a dozen different calls."],"lesson":"Use the tools you have. Small-clawed otters in zoos leave shellfish in the sun until the heat makes them open.","about":"The Asian small-clawed otter, also called oriental small-clawed otter and small-clawed otter, is an otter species native to South and Southeast Asia. It is the only species of the subgenus Amblonyx in the genus Lutra. It has short claws that do not extend beyond the pads of its webbed digits.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Otter_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"fir0002 flagstaffotos [at] gmail.com Canon 20D + Canon 70-200mm f/2.8 L","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otter_-_melbourne_zoo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asian_small-clawed_otter","sci":"Aonyx cinerea","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":93},
{"name":"Bonobo","page":"Bonobo","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests south of the Congo River, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","diet":"Mostly fruit, plus leaves, seeds and insects","facts":["It was long thought to be a kind of chimpanzee, but it's a species of its own.","Females team up with each other, so they, not the strongest male, lead the group.","Bonobos aren't good swimmers, and the Congo River keeps them apart from the chimpanzees living on its north side."],"lesson":"Peace is a skill. Bonobos are famous for calming tension in the group instead of fighting it out.","about":"The bonobo (Pan paniscus), also historically called the pygmy chimpanzee, is the smallest species of great ape and one of the two making up the genus Pan (the other being the chimpanzee, Pan troglodytes). While bonobos are today recognized as a distinct species, they were initially thought to be a subspecies of Pan troglodytes, because of the physical similarities between the two species.","description":"Species of great ape","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Apeldoorn_Apenheul_zoo_Bonobo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"natataek","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apeldoorn_Apenheul_zoo_Bonobo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonobo","sci":"Pan paniscus","status":"EN","mass":43.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":94},
{"name":"Chimpanzee","page":"Chimpanzee","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and wooded savannas of West, Central and East Africa","diet":"Fruit, leaves, insects, and sometimes monkeys","facts":["It makes tools, like stripped twigs for fishing termites out of their mounds.","Different chimp communities have their own tool habits, passed down like a culture.","Groups of males hunt together, often for red colobus monkeys."],"lesson":"Use what's around you. A chimpanzee turns a plain twig into the key to a meal.","about":"The chimpanzee (Pan troglodytes), commonly abbreviated to chimp, is a medium-sized great ape species found in the forests and savannas of equatorial Africa. It has four confirmed subspecies and a fifth proposed one. The chimpanzee shares the genus Pan with the bonobo.","description":"Species of great ape","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/015_Chimpanzee_at_Kibale_forest_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-015_Chimpanzee_at_Kibale_forest_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:015_Chimpanzee_at_Kibale_forest_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chimpanzee","sci":"Pan troglodytes","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":95},
{"name":"Bornean Orangutan","page":"Bornean_orangutan","group":"Mammal","where":"Lowland rainforests of Borneo","diet":"Fruit like figs and durian, plus leaves, bark and insects","facts":["It's the largest ape that truly lives in the trees.","It can live 35 to 45 years in the wild, and about 60 in a zoo.","It builds nests of branches high in the trees to sleep in."],"lesson":"Think it through. The clever orangutan solves problems with tools, not strength.","about":"The Bornean orangutan (Pongo pygmaeus) is an orangutan species endemic to the island of Borneo. It belongs to the only genus of great apes native to Asia and is the largest of the three Pongo species. It has a coarse, reddish coat and up to 1.5 m (4 ft 11 in) long arms.","description":"Species of ape","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Tanjung_Puting30477.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Nanosanchez","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanjung_Puting30477.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bornean_orangutan","sci":"Pongo pygmaeus","status":"CR","mass":1.73,"length":null,"life":null,"n":96},
{"name":"Ring-tailed Lemur","page":"Ring-tailed_lemur","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry forests and spiny scrub of southern Madagascar","diet":"Fruit, leaves and flowers, especially tamarind","facts":["Females are in charge of the troop.","In the morning it sunbathes sitting up, arms spread to the sun.","Males hold \"stink fights,\" wiping scent on their tails and waving them at rivals."],"lesson":"Warm up before you start. Ring-tailed lemurs greet every morning by soaking up the sun first.","about":"The ring-tailed lemur (Lemur catta) is a medium- to larger-sized strepsirrhine (wet-nosed) primate and the most internationally recognized lemur species, owing to its long, black-and-white, ringed tail. It belongs to Lemuridae, one of five lemur families, and is the only member of the Lemur genus. Like all lemurs, it is endemic to the island of Madagascar, where it is endangered.","description":"Species of mammal from Madagascar","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Ring-tailed_lemur_%28Lemur_catta%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ring-tailed_lemur_(Lemur_catta).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring-tailed_lemur","sci":"Lemur catta","status":"EN","mass":0.06,"length":null,"life":null,"n":97},
{"name":"Aye-aye","page":"Aye-aye","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Madagascar","diet":"Grubs in wood, nuts, fruit and nectar","facts":["It taps on wood with its long, thin middle finger and listens for hollow spots where grubs hide.","It gnaws a hole and hooks the grub out with that same finger.","It's the world's largest primate that's active at night."],"lesson":"Listen closely. The aye-aye finds its food by hearing what everyone else misses.","about":"The aye-aye (Daubentonia madagascariensis) is a long-fingered lemur, a strepsirrhine primate native to Madagascar with rodent-like teeth that perpetually grow and a special thin middle finger that they can use to catch grubs and larvae out of tree trunks. It is the world's largest nocturnal primate.","description":"Species of primate","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Wild_aye_aye.jpg/1920px-Wild_aye_aye.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"nomis-simon","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wild_aye_aye.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aye-aye","sci":"Daubentonia madagascariensis","status":"EN","mass":2.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":98},
{"name":"Proboscis Monkey","page":"Proboscis_monkey","group":"Mammal","where":"Mangroves, swamps and riverside forests of Borneo","diet":"Leaves, seeds and unripe fruit","facts":["The male's huge, drooping nose may make his calls louder, and females seem to prefer louder calls.","It may be the most water-loving primate, and can swim up to 20 meters (66 feet) underwater.","Many of its toes are webbed, which helps it swim across rivers."],"lesson":"Stand out without apology. The male proboscis monkey's big nose may be just what wins him a mate.","about":"The proboscis monkey or long-nosed monkey (Nasalis larvatus) is an arboreal Old World monkey with an unusually large nose (or proboscis), a reddish-brown skin color and a long tail. It is endemic to the Southeast Asian island of Borneo and is found mostly in mangrove forests and on the coastal areas of the island. This species co-exists with the Bornean orangutan and monkeys such as the silvery lutung.","description":"Primate species","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Proboscis_Monkey_in_Borneo.jpg/1920px-Proboscis_Monkey_in_Borneo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"David Dennis","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proboscis_Monkey_in_Borneo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proboscis_monkey","sci":"Nasalis larvatus","status":"EN","mass":0.455,"length":null,"life":null,"n":99},
{"name":"Golden Snub-nosed Monkey","page":"Golden_snub-nosed_monkey","group":"Mammal","where":"Snowy mountain forests of central and southwest China","diet":"Lichens, leaves, bark and seeds","facts":["It handles colder weather than any other monkey, in forests where snow is common.","In winter it lives mostly on lichen.","Its big groups, sometimes hundreds strong, are made of small families led by one male."],"lesson":"Huddle up in hard times. Golden snub-nosed monkeys get through snowy winters by sticking close.","about":"The golden snub-nosed monkey (Rhinopithecus roxellana) is an Old World monkey in the subfamily Colobinae. It is endemic to a small area in temperate, mountainous forests of central and Southwest China. They inhabit these mountainous forests of Southwestern China at elevations of 1,500–3,400 m (4,900–11,200 ft) above sea level.","description":"Species of Old World monkey","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Golden_Snub-nosed_Monkeys%2C_Qinling_Mountains_-_China.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Giovanni Mari","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Snub-nosed_Monkeys,_Qinling_Mountains_-_China.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_snub-nosed_monkey","sci":"Rhinopithecus roxellana","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":100},
{"name":"Japanese Macaque","page":"Japanese_macaque","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Japan, from the warm south to the snowy north","diet":"Fruit, seeds, leaves, bark and insects","facts":["It lives farther north than any other monkey.","In Jigokudani, troops warm up in hot springs in winter.","On Koshima Island, a female named Imo began washing sweet potatoes in the water, and her troop picked up the habit."],"lesson":"Good ideas spread. One young macaque started washing her food, and soon her whole troop did it.","about":"The Japanese macaque (Macaca fuscata), also known as the snow monkey, is a terrestrial Old World monkey species that is native to Japan. Colloquially, this macaque is referred to as the \"snow monkey\", because some live in areas where snow covers the ground for months each year. No other non-human primate lives farther north, nor in a colder climate.","description":"Species of Old World monkey","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Japanese_Snow_Monkey_%28Macaque%29_Mother_Grooms_Her_Young.jpg/1920px-Japanese_Snow_Monkey_%28Macaque%29_Mother_Grooms_Her_Young.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rocktendo & Adam Gman","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Snow_Monkey_(Macaque)_Mother_Grooms_Her_Young.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_macaque","sci":"Macaca fuscata","status":"LC","mass":14.0,"length":0.95,"life":null,"n":101},
{"name":"Mandrill","page":"Mandrill","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Gabon, Cameroon, Equatorial Guinea and the Congo","diet":"Fruit, seeds, insects and small animals","facts":["It's the largest monkey in the world.","The top males have the brightest red and blue faces.","It lives in huge groups called hordes, which can be hundreds strong."],"lesson":"Wear your colors with confidence. A mandrill's bright face tells the whole horde who's in charge.","about":"The mandrill (Mandrillus sphinx) is a large Old World monkey native to west central Africa. It is one of the most colorful mammals in the world, with red and blue skin on its face and posterior. The species is sexually dimorphic, as males have a larger body, longer canine teeth and brighter coloring.","description":"Species of Old World monkey from Africa","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Mandrill_Albert_September_2015_Zoo_Berlin_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Mandrill_Albert_September_2015_Zoo_Berlin_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Katma0601","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandrill_Albert_September_2015_Zoo_Berlin_(2).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandrill","sci":"Mandrillus sphinx","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":102},
{"name":"Gelada","page":"Gelada","group":"Mammal","where":"High grasslands and cliffs of the Ethiopian Highlands","diet":"Grass blades, seeds and roots","facts":["It's the only primate that lives mostly on grass.","The bare red patch on its chest earns it the nickname \"bleeding-heart monkey.\"","It sleeps on cliff ledges at night and grazes on the plateau by day."],"lesson":"Find your niche. The gelada thrives on grass, a food other monkeys pass right by.","about":"The gelada (Theropithecus gelada, Amharic: ጭላዳ, romanized: č̣əlada, Oromo: Jaldeessa daabee), sometimes called the bleeding-heart monkey or the gelada baboon, is a species of Old World monkey found only in the Ethiopian Highlands, living at elevations of 1,800–4,400 m (5,900–14,400 ft) above sea level.","description":"Species of Old World monkey","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Gelada_%28Theropithecus_gelada_gelada%29_male_2.jpg/1920px-Gelada_%28Theropithecus_gelada_gelada%29_male_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gelada_(Theropithecus_gelada_gelada)_male_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gelada","sci":"Theropithecus gelada","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":103},
{"name":"Philippine Tarsier","page":"Philippine_tarsier","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Bohol, Samar, Leyte and Mindanao, in the southern Philippines","diet":"Insects, spiders and small lizards","facts":["It has the biggest eyes for its body size of any mammal.","Its eyes can't move in their sockets, so it turns its head instead, 180 degrees.","Its body is only about 7 cm (2.8 inches) long, and its tail is longer than that."],"lesson":"Small doesn't mean simple. The tiny tarsier sees and hears in ways that would amaze us.","about":"The Philippine tarsier (Carlito syrichta) is a species of tarsier endemic to the Philippines. It is found in the southeastern part of the archipelago, particularly on the islands of Bohol, Samar and Leyte. It is a member of the approximately 45-million-year-old family Tarsiidae, whose name is derived from its elongated \"tarsus\" or ankle bone.","description":"Species of primate","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Philippine_tarsier_%28Carlito_syrichta_fraterculus%29_Bohol.jpg/1920px-Philippine_tarsier_%28Carlito_syrichta_fraterculus%29_Bohol.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippine_tarsier_(Carlito_syrichta_fraterculus)_Bohol.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_tarsier","sci":"Carlito syrichta","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":104},
{"name":"Golden Lion Tamarin","page":"Golden_lion_tamarin","group":"Mammal","where":"Coastal Atlantic Forest of Rio de Janeiro state, Brazil","diet":"Fruit, insects, frogs and small lizards","facts":["It nearly vanished in the 1970s, down to a few hundred in the wild.","Zoo-born tamarins released into the forest helped bring it back.","It's named for the long golden hair around its face, like a lion's mane."],"lesson":"Second chances are real. The golden lion tamarin was nearly gone, and came back because people kept trying.","about":"The golden lion tamarin (Leontopithecus rosalia; Portuguese: mico-leão-dourado), less commonly known as the golden lion marmoset, is a small New World monkey of the family Callitrichidae. Endemic to the Atlantic coastal forests of Brazil, the golden lion tamarin is an endangered species. Its geographic range is entirely within the state of Rio de Janeiro.","description":"Species of New World monkey","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Leontopithecus_rosalia_-_Copenhagen_Zoo_-_DSC09082.JPG/1920px-Leontopithecus_rosalia_-_Copenhagen_Zoo_-_DSC09082.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Daderot","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leontopithecus_rosalia_-_Copenhagen_Zoo_-_DSC09082.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_lion_tamarin","sci":"Leontopithecus rosalia","status":"EN","mass":0.061,"length":null,"life":null,"n":105},
{"name":"Mantled Howler","page":"Mantled_howler","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests from southern Mexico to Colombia and Ecuador","diet":"Leaves, fruit and flowers","facts":["Males roar at dawn and dusk, and the calls carry far through the forest.","It spends most of the day resting, to save energy on its leafy diet.","Its tail can grip a branch like a fifth hand."],"lesson":"Save your energy for what matters. The howler rests most of the day and saves its voice for the roar.","about":"The mantled howler (Alouatta palliata) is a species of howler monkey, a type of New World monkey, from Central and South America. It is one of the monkey species most often seen and heard in the wild in Central America. It takes its \"mantled\" name from the long guard hairs on its sides.","description":"Species of New World monkey","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Panamanian_Male_Adult_Howler_Monkey.jpg/1920px-Panamanian_Male_Adult_Howler_Monkey.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Dr. Ariel Rodriguez-Vargas","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panamanian_Male_Adult_Howler_Monkey.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mantled_howler","sci":"Alouatta palliata","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":106},
{"name":"Pygmy Marmoset","page":"Pygmy_marmoset","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of the western Amazon","diet":"Tree gum and sap, plus insects","facts":["It's the smallest true monkey, weighing about 100 grams (3.5 oz).","It gnaws holes in tree bark and comes back to lick up the gum.","It can turn its head 180 degrees to look backward."],"lesson":"Little by little adds up. The pygmy marmoset lives on drops of gum from holes it gnaws again and again.","about":"The pygmy marmoset (genus Cebuella) is a small New World monkey. The genus consists of two species, the Western pygmy marmoset (Cebuella pygmaea), and the Eastern pygmy marmoset, (Cebuella niveiventris). The smallest monkey in the world at just over 100 g (3.5 oz), it is native to rainforests of the western Amazon Basin in South America.","description":"Genus of monkey","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Dv%C3%A6rgsilkeabe_Callithrix_pygmaea.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Malene Thyssen (User Malene)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dv%C3%A6rgsilkeabe_Callithrix_pygmaea.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmy_marmoset","sci":"Cebuella","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":107},
{"name":"Naked Mole-rat","page":"Naked_mole-rat","group":"Mammal","where":"Underground, in dry grasslands of Kenya, Ethiopia and Somalia","diet":"Roots and tubers","facts":["It can live more than 30 years, far longer than any other rodent its size.","It lives in colonies with one breeding queen, like bees.","It can survive 18 minutes without oxygen."],"lesson":"Work together and you'll go far. Naked mole-rats run their underground colony as one team.","about":"The naked mole-rat (Heterocephalus glaber), also known as the sand puppy, is a burrowing rodent native to the Horn of Africa and parts of Kenya, notably in Somali regions. It is the only species in the genus Heterocephalus.","description":"Burrowing eusocial rodent","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Webs180130-rat.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Kein keen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Webs180130-rat.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naked_mole-rat","sci":"Heterocephalus glaber","status":"LC","mass":0.034,"length":null,"life":null,"n":108},
{"name":"North American Beaver","page":"North_American_beaver","group":"Mammal","where":"Streams, rivers and lakes from Canada through the US to northern Mexico","diet":"Bark, twigs and water plants","facts":["Its dams make ponds and wetlands that become homes for many other animals.","It's the largest rodent in North America.","The largest beaver dam ever found, spotted by satellite in 2007, is about 850 meters (2,790 feet) long, more than half a mile."],"lesson":"Build something that helps others. A beaver's dam creates a whole wetland that other animals call home.","about":"The North American beaver (Castor canadensis) is one of two extant beaver species, along with the Eurasian beaver (Castor fiber). It is native to North America and has been introduced in South America (Patagonia) and Europe (primarily Finland and Karelia). The North American beaver is one of the national symbols of Canada and the official state mammal of Oregon and New York.","description":"Species of dam-building rodent","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/American_Beaver.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Steve from Washington, DC, USA","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Beaver.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_American_beaver","sci":"Castor canadensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":15.0,"n":109},
{"name":"Long-tailed Chinchilla","page":"Long-tailed_chinchilla","group":"Mammal","where":"Rocky slopes of the Andes in northern Chile","diet":"Grasses, leaves and seeds","facts":["Its fur is so thick that dozens of hairs grow from each root.","It takes dust baths to keep its fur clean.","Hunting for its fur left only a few wild colonies, in Chile."],"lesson":"Take care of what makes you special. The chinchilla keeps its famous fur in shape with dust baths.","about":"The long-tailed chinchilla (Chinchilla laniger), also called the Chilean, coastal, common, or lesser chinchilla, is one of two species of rodent from the genus Chinchilla: the other species being C. chinchilla. Both species are endangered in the wild after historically being hunted for their soft hair coats. Domestic breeds of chinchilla are believed to descend from specimens of C. lanigera.","description":"Species of rodent","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Chinchilla_lanigera_%28Wroclaw_zoo%29-2.JPG/1920px-Chinchilla_lanigera_%28Wroclaw_zoo%29-2.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Guérin Nicolas (messages)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinchilla_lanigera_(Wroclaw_zoo)-2.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-tailed_chinchilla","sci":"Chinchilla lanigera","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":110},
{"name":"Crested Porcupine","page":"Crested_porcupine","group":"Mammal","where":"Italy, North Africa and Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Roots, bulbs and leaves, plus the odd insect","facts":["It can't shoot its quills, but it backs into attackers to stab them.","It rattles the hollow quills on its tail as a warning.","It's one of the largest rodents in the world."],"lesson":"Warn before you fight. The crested porcupine rattles its quills first, and most trouble backs off.","about":"The crested porcupine (Hystrix cristata), also known as the African crested porcupine, is a species of rodent in the family Hystricidae native to Italy, North Africa, West Africa, Central Africa and East Africa. Alongside the Cape porcupine, it is one of the largest porcupine species in the world. It is the only species of porcupine found in Europe.","description":"Species of rodent","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Hystrix_cristata_qtl1.jpg/1920px-Hystrix_cristata_qtl1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Quartl","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hystrix_cristata_qtl1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crested_porcupine","sci":"Hystrix cristata","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":111},
{"name":"Eurasian Red Squirrel","page":"Red_squirrel","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of Europe and northern Asia","diet":"Pine and spruce seeds, nuts, berries and fungi","facts":["It strips pine cones neatly to get at the seeds inside.","In most of Britain and Ireland, it has lost out to the gray squirrel brought over from North America.","Its ear tufts are longest in winter."],"lesson":"Plan for winter. The red squirrel stores food in good times to get through the lean ones.","about":"The red squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris), also called Eurasian red squirrel, is a species of tree squirrel in the genus Sciurus. It is an arboreal and primarily herbivorous rodent and common throughout Eurasia.","description":"Species of tree squirrel common in Europe and Asia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Squirrel_posing.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Peter Trimming","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Squirrel_posing.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_squirrel","sci":"Sciurus vulgaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":112},
{"name":"Southern Flying Squirrel","page":"Southern_flying_squirrel","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of the eastern US, including Florida, and parts of Mexico and Central America","diet":"Nuts, seeds, berries and insects","facts":["It glides from tree to tree on a furry flap of skin between its legs.","It's small: 21 to 26 cm (8 to 10 inches) long, tail included.","It comes out only at night, and can live unseen in wooded suburbs."],"lesson":"Trust what you've got. The flying squirrel can't really fly, but it goes far by spreading out.","about":"The southern flying squirrel (Glaucomys volans), sometimes called the assapan or polatouche, is a species of squirrel in the family Sciuridae. G. volans is one of three species of flying squirrels found in North America. It is found in deciduous and mixed woods in the eastern half of North America, from southeastern Canada to Florida.","description":"Species of rodent","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Southern_Flying_Squirrel_-_Glaucomys_volans%2C_Arlington%2C_Virginia%2C_December_22%2C_2020_%2853406816432%29.jpg/1920px-Southern_Flying_Squirrel_-_Glaucomys_volans%2C_Arlington%2C_Virginia%2C_December_22%2C_2020_%2853406816432%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Judy Gallagher","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Flying_Squirrel_-_Glaucomys_volans,_Arlington,_Virginia,_December_22,_2020_(53406816432).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_flying_squirrel","sci":"Glaucomys volans","status":"LC","mass":0.066,"length":null,"life":null,"n":113},
{"name":"Patagonian Mara","page":"Patagonian_mara","group":"Mammal","where":"Open grasslands and scrub of Argentina","diet":"Grasses and other plants","facts":["It's also called the Patagonian hare, but it's a rodent, a cousin of guinea pigs and capybaras.","Pairs stay together for life.","Many pairs leave their babies together in one shared den."],"lesson":"Stay loyal. Patagonian maras pair for life and stay close to each other out in the open.","about":"The Patagonian mara (Dolichotis patagonum) is a rodent in the mara genus Dolichotis. It is also known as the Patagonian cavy or Patagonian hare. This herbivorous, somewhat rabbit-like animal is found in open and semi-open habitats in Argentina, including large parts of Patagonia.","description":"Species of rodent","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Dolichotis_patagonum_99386074.jpg/1920px-Dolichotis_patagonum_99386074.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Hugo Hulsberg","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolichotis_patagonum_99386074.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patagonian_mara","sci":"Dolichotis patagonum","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":114},
{"name":"American Pika","page":"American_pika","group":"Mammal","where":"Rocky mountain slopes of western North America","diet":"Grasses and wildflowers","facts":["All summer it gathers plants into hay piles to eat in winter.","It doesn't hibernate.","It overheats easily: a few hours above 25.5 C (78 F) with no cool place to hide can kill it."],"lesson":"Make hay while the sun shines. The pika spends its summer stacking up the food it needs for winter.","about":"The American pika (Ochotona princeps) is a small, herbivorous mammal of the family Ochotonidae, closely related to rabbits and hares (order Lagomorpha). It is native to the mountainous regions of western North America, typically inhabiting rocky slopes and talus fields at or above the tree line higher elevation. Unlike most mammals of similar size, the American pika is diurnal, being active primarily during the day.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/Ochotona_princeps_rockies.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"sevenstar","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ochotona_princeps_rockies.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_pika","sci":"Ochotona princeps","status":"LC","mass":0.148,"length":null,"life":null,"n":115},
{"name":"Snowshoe Hare","page":"Snowshoe_hare","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern forests of Canada, Alaska and the northern US","diet":"Grasses, twigs, buds and bark","facts":["Its coat turns white in winter and brown in summer.","Its big back feet keep it on top of the snow, like snowshoes.","Fur trappers' records show its numbers and the Canada lynx's rising and falling together in a cycle."],"lesson":"Change with the season. The snowshoe hare's coat turns white right when the snow comes.","about":"The snowshoe hare (Lepus americanus), also called the varying hare or snowshoe rabbit, is a species of hare found in North America. The \"snowshoe\" moniker refers to the hare's large hind feet, which prevent it from sinking into soft snow, much like snowshoes. The hare has two seasonal coats which aid in camouflaging the animal.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Lepus_americanus_5459_cropped.jpg/1920px-Lepus_americanus_5459_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Lepus_americanus_5459.JPG: Walter Siegmund (talk) derivative work: Wsiegmund","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lepus_americanus_5459_cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowshoe_hare","sci":"Lepus americanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":116},
{"name":"Mexican Free-tailed Bat","page":"Mexican_free-tailed_bat","group":"Mammal","where":"The southern US (including Florida), Mexico, Central and South America","diet":"Moths and other flying insects","facts":["Bracken Cave in Texas holds the biggest bat colony in the world, millions strong.","It has been clocked flying level at a ground speed of 160 km/h (99 mph), though wind gusts may have helped.","It's the state flying mammal of Texas."],"lesson":"There's strength in numbers. Millions of free-tailed bats pour out of Bracken Cave together at dusk.","about":"The Mexican free-tailed bat or Brazilian free-tailed bat (Tadarida brasiliensis) is a medium-sized bat native to North, Central, and South America and the Caribbean, so named because its tail can be almost half its total length and is not attached to its uropatagium. It has been claimed to have the fastest horizontal speed of any animal, reaching level flight speed of up to 99 mph (160 km/h).","description":"Species of bat native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Mexican_free-tailed_bat_%288006856842%29.jpg/1920px-Mexican_free-tailed_bat_%288006856842%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexican_free-tailed_bat_(8006856842).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_free-tailed_bat","sci":"Tadarida brasiliensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":117},
{"name":"Common Vampire Bat","page":"Common_vampire_bat","group":"Mammal","where":"Mexico, Central and South America","diet":"The blood of mammals like cattle and horses","facts":["Its saliva keeps blood from clotting while it feeds.","It can run and hop on the ground, unusual for a bat.","Bats share food with hungry roostmates, and the ones that share tend to get help back."],"lesson":"Kindness comes back around. Vampire bats share meals with the roostmates who once shared with them.","about":"The common vampire bat (Desmodus rotundus) is a small, leaf-nosed bat native to the Americas. It is one of three extant species of vampire bats, the other two being the hairy-legged and the white-winged vampire bats. The common vampire bat practices hematophagy, mainly feeding on the blood of livestock.","description":"Species of bat native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Desmo-Flug-01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Uwe Schmidt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Desmo-Flug-01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_vampire_bat","sci":"Desmodus rotundus","status":"LC","mass":0.033,"length":null,"life":null,"n":118},
{"name":"Honduran White Bat","page":"Honduran_white_bat","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Honduras, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and Panama","diet":"Figs","facts":["It cuts the ribs of a big leaf so it folds into a tent, and roosts under it.","Light through the leaf makes its white fur look green, hiding it.","It's one of only a few white bats in the world."],"lesson":"Make your own shelter. The Honduran white bat turns one leaf into a tent.","about":"The Honduran white bat (Ectophylla alba), also called the Caribbean white tent-making bat, is a species of bat in the family Phyllostomidae. It is the only member of the genus Ectophylla. The genus and the species were both scientifically described for the first time in 1892.","description":"Species of bat that constructs tents","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Ectophylla_alba_Costa_Rica.jpg/1920px-Ectophylla_alba_Costa_Rica.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Leyo","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5 ch","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ectophylla_alba_Costa_Rica.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honduran_white_bat","sci":"Ectophylla alba","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":119},
{"name":"Large Flying Fox","page":"Large_flying_fox","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and mangroves of Southeast Asia","diet":"Fruit, nectar and flowers","facts":["Its wings can span up to 1.5 meters (almost 5 feet), among the widest of any bat.","It spreads the seeds and pollen of many rainforest trees.","Whole colonies fly out at dusk in a long, scattered stream."],"lesson":"Spread good things as you go. The flying fox plants forests by carrying seeds far and wide.","about":"The large flying fox (Pteropus vampyrus), also known as the greater flying fox, Malayan flying fox, Malaysian flying fox, large fruit bat, kalang, or kalong, is a southeast Asian species of megabat in the family Pteropodidae. Despite its scientific name, it feeds exclusively on fruits, nectar, and flowers, like the other flying foxes of the genus Pteropus. It is noted for being one of the largest bats.","description":"Species of fruit bat","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Wilhelma_Kalong-Flughund_Pteropus_vampyrus_0513.jpg/1920px-Wilhelma_Kalong-Flughund_Pteropus_vampyrus_0513.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"NobbiP","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelma_Kalong-Flughund_Pteropus_vampyrus_0513.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_flying_fox","sci":"Pteropus vampyrus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":120},
{"name":"Star-nosed Mole","page":"Star-nosed_mole","group":"Mammal","where":"Wet ground of eastern Canada and the eastern US","diet":"Worms, insects and small fish","facts":["Its star of 22 pink tentacles has more touch sensors than a human hand.","It's the fastest-eating mammal, finding and swallowing food in a quarter of a second.","It can smell underwater by blowing bubbles and sniffing them back in."],"lesson":"Feel your way forward. The star-nosed mole can barely see, but its star is about six times more sensitive than a human hand.","about":"The star-nosed mole (Condylura cristata) is a small semiaquatic mole found in moist, low elevation areas in the northeastern parts of North America. It is the only extant member of the tribe Condylurini and genus Condylura. It has more than 25,000 minute sensory receptors in touch organs, known as Eimer's organs, with which this hamster-sized mole feels its way around.","description":"Species of mole","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Condylura.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"US National Parks Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Condylura.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star-nosed_mole","sci":"Condylura cristata","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":121},
{"name":"European Hedgehog","page":"European_hedgehog","group":"Mammal","where":"Gardens, hedges and woods of western Europe, Britain and Scandinavia","diet":"Beetles, caterpillars, worms and slugs","facts":["Its back is covered in thousands of spines.","It hibernates through the winter.","When threatened it rolls into a tight, spiky ball."],"lesson":"Protect your soft side. The hedgehog rolls up when it needs to, and uncurls when it's safe.","about":"The European hedgehog (Erinaceus europaeus), also known as the West European hedgehog or common hedgehog, is a hedgehog species native to Europe from Iberia and Italy northwards into Scandinavia and westwards into Ireland. It is a generally common and widely distributed species that can survive across a wide range of habitat types.","description":"Species of small spiny mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Erinaceus_europaeus_LC0119.jpg/1920px-Erinaceus_europaeus_LC0119.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jörg Hempel","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erinaceus_europaeus_LC0119.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_hedgehog","sci":"Erinaceus europaeus","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":122},
{"name":"Etruscan Shrew","page":"Etruscan_shrew","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm, damp, shrubby places from southern Europe and North Africa to Malaysia","diet":"Insects, larvae and earthworms, plus the odd baby lizard or frog","facts":["It's the smallest mammal by weight, about 1.8 grams.","Its heart can beat up to 1,511 times a minute.","It eats 1.5 to 2 times its own weight in food every day."],"lesson":"Keep moving. The tiny Etruscan shrew is always on the go when it's awake, and its heart can race at 25 beats a second.","about":"The Etruscan shrew (Suncus etruscus), also known as the Etruscan pygmy shrew, white-toothed pygmy shrew and Savi's pygmy shrew, is the smallest known extant mammal by mass, weighing only about 1.8 g (0.063 oz) on average - as much as a paperclip. (The bumblebee bat is regarded as the smallest mammal by skull size and body length.)\nThe Etruscan shrew has a body length of about 4 cm (1.6 in) excluding the tail.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Suncus_etruscus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Trebol-a","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suncus_etruscus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etruscan_shrew","sci":"Suncus etruscus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":123},
{"name":"Aardvark","page":"Aardvark","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands, savannas and woodlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["Its name means \"earth pig\" in Afrikaans.","Its sticky tongue can be 30 cm (1 foot) long.","Its old burrows become homes for many other animals."],"lesson":"Dig deep. The aardvark's burrows end up sheltering animals it'll never meet.","about":"The aardvark (Orycteropus afer) is a medium-sized, burrowing, nocturnal mammal native to Africa. The aardvark is the only living member of the genus Orycteropus, the family Orycteropodidae and the order Tubulidentata. The aardvark is an afrotherian, a clade that also includes elephants, manatees, and hyraxes.","description":"Burrowing mammal native to Africa","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Orycteropus_afer_175359469.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Kelly Abram","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orycteropus_afer_175359469.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aardvark","sci":"Orycteropus afer","status":"LC","mass":1.9,"length":null,"life":null,"n":124},
{"name":"Rock Hyrax","page":"Rock_hyrax","group":"Mammal","where":"Rocky hills of Africa and the Middle East","diet":"All kinds of plants and leaves, plus the odd insect or grub","facts":["It's a distant relative of the elephant, with little tusk-like front teeth.","It can go for days without drinking, getting its water from its food.","Its hardened mix of dung and pee, called hyraceum, has been used in folk medicine and perfume."],"lesson":"Don't judge by looks. The little rock hyrax is a cousin of the elephant.","about":"The rock hyrax (Procavia capensis), also called dassie, Rickaz, Cape hyrax, rock rabbit, or coney (in early English Bible translations), is a medium-sized terrestrial hyrax native to Africa and the Middle East. Commonly called the dassie in South Africa (Afrikaans: klipdassie), it is one of the five living species of the order Hyracoidea, and the only one in the genus Procavia.","description":"Species of mammal native to Africa and the Middle East","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/190_Rock_hyrax_family_in_Damaraland_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-190_Rock_hyrax_family_in_Damaraland_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:190_Rock_hyrax_family_in_Damaraland_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_hyrax","sci":"Procavia capensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":125},
{"name":"Quokka","page":"Quokka","group":"Mammal","where":"Rottnest and Bald islands and the far southwest of Western Australia","diet":"Grasses, leaves and shrubs","facts":["Its \"smiling\" face made it famous for selfies.","Rottnest Island was named by a Dutch explorer who took quokkas for giant rats.","It looks like a very small kangaroo, but it can climb small trees and shrubs up to 1.5 m (5 feet) high."],"lesson":"A friendly face opens doors. The quokka's smile made a small island famous around the world.","about":"The quokka (Setonix brachyurus) is a macropod native to Australia. It is about the size of a domestic cat. It is the only member of the genus Setonix.","description":"Small marsupial from southwestern Australia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Setonix_brachyurus_on_Rottnest_Island_1.jpg/1920px-Setonix_brachyurus_on_Rottnest_Island_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Christophe95","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setonix_brachyurus_on_Rottnest_Island_1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quokka","sci":"Setonix brachyurus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":10.0,"n":126},
{"name":"Tasmanian Devil","page":"Tasmanian_devil","group":"Mammal","where":"Tasmania","diet":"Carrion and small animals","facts":["It has one of the strongest bites for its size of any living mammal.","Since the late 1990s, a contagious face cancer has drastically cut their numbers.","It's known for its extremely loud screech, its strong smell and its fierce feeding."],"lesson":"Don't let a reputation define you. The devil is loud and fierce, but mostly it cleans up what others leave.","about":"The Tasmanian devil (Sarcophilus harrisii; palawa kani: purinina) is a carnivorous marsupial of the family Dasyuridae. It was formerly present across mainland Australia, but became extinct there around 3,500 years ago; it is now confined to the island of Tasmania. The size of a small dog, the Tasmanian devil became the largest carnivorous marsupial in the world following the extinction of the thylacine in 1936.","description":"Australian carnivorous marsupial","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Sarcophilus_harrisii_taranna.jpg/1920px-Sarcophilus_harrisii_taranna.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophilus_harrisii_taranna.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tasmanian_devil","sci":"Sarcophilus harrisii","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":127},
{"name":"Common Wombat","page":"Common_wombat","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and hills of southeastern Australia and Tasmania","diet":"Grasses, snow tussocks and other plants","facts":["Its poop comes out in cubes.","It's mostly active at night and lives in burrows.","Its burrows turn over the soil and give shelter to many other animals."],"lesson":"Steady wins. The wombat plods along, tunnel after tunnel, and ends up reshaping the land around it.","about":"The common wombat (Vombatus ursinus), also known as the bare-nosed wombat, is a marsupial, one of three extant species of wombats and the sole extant member of the genus Vombatus.","description":"Species of marsupial","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Vombatus_ursinus_-Maria_Island_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"JJ Harrison (jjharrison89@facebook.com)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vombatus_ursinus_-Maria_Island_National_Park.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_wombat","sci":"Vombatus ursinus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":128},
{"name":"Sugar Glider","page":"Sugar_glider","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests of southeastern Australia, in southern Queensland and New South Wales","diet":"Tree sap, nectar and insects","facts":["It can glide 50 meters (55 yards) or more between trees.","It's named for its love of sweet sap and nectar.","Groups huddle together in tree hollows to keep warm."],"lesson":"Stay close to your people. Sugar gliders get through cold nights by huddling together.","about":"The sugar glider (Petaurus breviceps) is a small, omnivorous, arboreal, and nocturnal gliding possum. The common name refers to its predilection for sugary foods such as sap and nectar and its ability to glide through the air, much like a flying squirrel. They have very similar habits and appearance to the flying squirrel, despite not being closely related—an example of convergent evolution.","description":"Species of Australian marsupial","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Petaurus_breviceps_119464446.jpg/1920px-Petaurus_breviceps_119464446.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Tasney","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petaurus_breviceps_119464446.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugar_glider","sci":"Petaurus breviceps","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":129},
{"name":"Numbat","page":"Numbat","group":"Mammal","where":"Eucalyptus woodlands of southwestern Western Australia","diet":"Termites, up to 20,000 a day","facts":["Unlike most marsupials, it's active by day, when the termites are.","It eats up to 20,000 termites a day.","It's the animal emblem of Western Australia."],"lesson":"Time your efforts. The numbat hunts by day, when the termites are near the surface.","about":"The numbat (binomen: Myrmecobius fasciatus), also known as the noombat or walpurti, is an insectivorous marsupial. It is diurnal and its diet consists almost exclusively of termites. The species was once widespread across southern Australia, but is now restricted to several small colonies in Western Australia.","description":"Species of Australian marsupial","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Numbat%2C_Dryandra_Woodland%2C_Western_Australia.jpg/1920px-Numbat%2C_Dryandra_Woodland%2C_Western_Australia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numbat,_Dryandra_Woodland,_Western_Australia.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numbat","sci":"Myrmecobius fasciatus","status":"NT","mass":0.6,"length":null,"life":null,"n":130},
{"name":"Virginia Opossum","page":"Virginia_opossum","group":"Mammal","where":"Most of the eastern and central US, including Florida, plus Mexico and Central America","diet":"Insects, fruit, carrion and small animals","facts":["It's the only marsupial north of Mexico.","When threatened, it \"plays possum,\" going limp and smelling dead.","It has 50 teeth, more than any other North American land mammal."],"lesson":"Sometimes the best fight is no fight. The opossum gets through danger by playing dead.","about":"The Virginia opossum (Didelphis virginiana), also known as the North American opossum, is a member of the opossum family found from southern Canada to northern Costa Rica, making it the northernmost marsupial in the world. Commonly referred to simply as the possum, it is a solitary nocturnal animal about the size of a domestic cat, and a successful opportunist.","description":"Species of North American marsupial","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Opossum_2.jpg/1920px-Opossum_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Cody Pope","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opossum_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virginia_opossum","sci":"Didelphis virginiana","status":"LC","mass":0.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":131},
{"name":"Short-beaked Echidna","page":"Short-beaked_echidna","group":"Mammal","where":"All over Australia, and in New Guinea","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["It's one of the only mammals that lay eggs.","Its baby, called a puggle, hatches inside its mother's pouch.","It has no teeth, and grinds its food against hard pads in its mouth."],"lesson":"Be one of a kind. The echidna is a mammal that lays eggs, and doesn't mind breaking the rules.","about":"The short-beaked echidna (Tachyglossus aculeatus), also known as the common echidna, or short-nosed echidna, is one of four living species of echidna, and the only member of the genus Tachyglossus, from Ancient Greek ταχύς (takhús), meaning \"fast\", and γλῶσσα (glôssa), meaning \"tongue\".","description":"Spiny furred egg-laying mammal from Australia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Wild_shortbeak_echidna.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"User:Fir0002","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wild_shortbeak_echidna.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short-beaked_echidna","sci":"Tachyglossus aculeatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":132},
{"name":"Giant Anteater","page":"Giant_anteater","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and forests from Central America to northern Argentina","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["Its tongue reaches 45 cm (18 inches) and flicks in and out about 160 times a minute.","It has no teeth.","It raids up to 200 nests a day, for up to a minute each, and eats around 35,000 insects."],"lesson":"Take only what you need. The giant anteater spends no more than a minute at each nest before moving on.","about":"The giant anteater (Myrmecophaga tridactyla) is a large insectivorous mammal native to Central and South America. It is the largest of the four living species of anteaters, which are classified with sloths in the order Pilosa. The only extant member of the genus Myrmecophaga, the giant anteater is mostly terrestrial, in contrast to other living anteaters and sloths, which are arboreal or semiarboreal.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Myresluger2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Malene Thyssen","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myresluger2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_anteater","sci":"Myrmecophaga tridactyla","status":"VU","mass":1.35,"length":null,"life":null,"n":133},
{"name":"Pink Fairy Armadillo","page":"Pink_fairy_armadillo","group":"Mammal","where":"Sandy plains and grasslands of central Argentina","diet":"Ants, larvae, worms and roots","facts":["It's the smallest armadillo, only 9 to 11.5 cm (3.5 to 4.5 inches) long.","Its pink shell is attached only along its back.","It \"swims\" through sand with its big front claws."],"lesson":"Find comfort in your own space. The pink fairy armadillo spends nearly its whole life underground.","about":"The pink fairy armadillo (Chlamyphorus truncatus) is the smallest species of armadillo, first described by Richard Harlan in 1825. The pink fairy armadillo is 90–115 mm (3.5–4.5 in) long, and typically weighs about 120 g (4.2 oz). This solitary, desert-adapted animal is endemic to the deserts and scrub lands of central Argentina.","description":"Species of armadillo","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Pink_Fairy_Armadillo_%28Chlamyphorus_truncatus%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Cliff","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pink_Fairy_Armadillo_(Chlamyphorus_truncatus).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_fairy_armadillo","sci":"Chlamyphorus truncatus","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":134},
{"name":"Okapi","page":"Okapi","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of the northeastern Democratic Republic of the Congo","diet":"Leaves, buds, fruit and fungi","facts":["It has zebra-like stripes, but its closest living relative is the giraffe.","Western scientists didn't formally name it until 1901, after years of rumors about an African unicorn.","It uses its long, dark tongue to strip leaves from branches."],"lesson":"You aren't what people assume. The okapi has a zebra's stripes, and it's really a giraffe's cousin.","about":"The okapi (Okapia johnstoni), also known as forest giraffe and zebra giraffe, is an artiodactyl mammal that is endemic to the northeast Democratic Republic of the Congo. It is the only species in the genus Okapia. Although the okapi has striped markings reminiscent of zebras, it is most closely related to the giraffe.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Saint-Aignan_%28Loir-et-Cher%29._Okapi.jpg/1920px-Saint-Aignan_%28Loir-et-Cher%29._Okapi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Daniel Jolivet","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Aignan_(Loir-et-Cher)._Okapi.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okapi","sci":"Okapia johnstoni","status":"EN","mass":250.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":135},
{"name":"Moose","page":"Moose","group":"Mammal","where":"Northern forests of North America, Europe and Asia","diet":"Leaves, twigs, bark and water plants","facts":["It's the largest member of the deer family.","It's a strong swimmer: its hollow, air-filled hairs help it float.","Bulls shed their antlers every winter and grow a new set in spring."],"lesson":"Let go to grow. A bull moose drops his antlers every winter, and grows a new set.","about":"The moose or elk (Alces alces) is the world's tallest, largest and heaviest extant species of deer and the only extant species in the genus Alces. It is also the tallest, and the second-largest, land animal in North America, falling short only to the American bison in body mass.","description":"Largest species of deer","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Alaska_moose.jpg/1920px-Alaska_moose.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Paxson Woelber","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaska_moose.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moose","sci":"Alces alces","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":136},
{"name":"Reindeer","page":"Reindeer","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic tundra and northern forests; called caribou in North America","diet":"Lichens, grasses and sedges","facts":["Both males and females grow antlers.","Its eyes change from gold in summer to blue in winter, to see better in the long dark.","Some herds travel farther each year than any other land mammal."],"lesson":"Keep going. Reindeer herds walk thousands of kilometers a year to find their food.","about":"The reindeer or caribou (Rangifer tarandus) is a species of deer with circumpolar distribution, native to the Arctic, subarctic, tundra, boreal, and mountainous regions of Northern Europe, Siberia, and North America. It is the only representative of the genus Rangifer.","description":"Species of deer","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Reinbukken_p%C3%A5_frisk_gr%C3%B8nt_beite._-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Reinbukken_p%C3%A5_frisk_gr%C3%B8nt_beite._-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Are G Nilsen","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reinbukken_p%C3%A5_frisk_gr%C3%B8nt_beite._-_panoramio.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reindeer","sci":"Rangifer tarandus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":137},
{"name":"American Bison","page":"American_bison","group":"Mammal","where":"Prairies and parks of North America, like Yellowstone","diet":"Grasses and sedges","facts":["It's the heaviest land animal in North America.","Tens of millions once roamed; by the late 1800s only a few hundred were left.","It's the national mammal of the United States."],"lesson":"Comebacks are possible. The bison fell to just a few hundred animals, and now there are about half a million again.","about":"The American bison (Bison bison; pl.: bison), commonly known as the American buffalo, or simply buffalo (not to be confused with true buffalo), is a species of bison that is endemic to North America. It is one of two extant species of bison, along with the European bison.","description":"Species of bovid mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/American_bison_k5680-1.jpg/1920px-American_bison_k5680-1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jack Dykinga","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_bison_k5680-1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_bison","sci":"Bison bison","status":"NT","mass":1270.058,"length":null,"life":null,"n":138},
{"name":"Plains Zebra","page":"Plains_zebra","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and savannas of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Grasses","facts":["No two zebras have the same stripes.","Migrating herds seem to remember where the grazing was best months before.","It grazes the tall grass first, clearing the way for wildebeest and gazelles."],"lesson":"Stand out together. Lions do best when they catch a zebra on its own, so zebras stay close in their herds.","about":"The plains zebra (Equus quagga, formerly Equus burchellii) is the most common and geographically widespread species of zebra. Its range is fragmented, but spans much of southern and eastern Africa south of the Sahara. Six or seven subspecies have been recognised, including the quagga which was thought to be a separate species.","description":"Species of zebra","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Equus_quagga_burchellii_-_Etosha%2C_2014.jpg/1920px-Equus_quagga_burchellii_-_Etosha%2C_2014.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equus_quagga_burchellii_-_Etosha,_2014.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plains_zebra","sci":"Equus quagga","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":139},
{"name":"Grévy's Zebra","page":"Grévy's_zebra","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry grasslands of Kenya and Ethiopia","diet":"Grasses","facts":["It's the largest wild member of the horse family.","Its stripes are narrower and closer together than other zebras'.","It's named after Jules Grévy, a president of France."],"lesson":"Details matter. Grévy's zebra is told apart from all the others by its fine, narrow stripes.","about":"Grévy's zebra (Equus grevyi), also known commonly as the imperial zebra, is the largest living species of wild equid and the most threatened of the three species of zebras, the other two being the plains zebra and the mountain zebra. Named after French president Jules Grévy, it is found in parts of Kenya and Ethiopia.","description":"Species of zebra","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Grevy%27s_Zebra_Stallion.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Rainbirder","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grevy%27s_Zebra_Stallion.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gr%C3%A9vy%27s_zebra","sci":"Equus grevyi","status":"EN","mass":40.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":140},
{"name":"Hippopotamus","page":"Hippopotamus","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers and lakes of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Grass, grazed at night","facts":["It spends the day in the water and comes out at night to graze.","Its skin makes a reddish oily liquid that works like sunscreen.","Its closest living relatives are whales and dolphins."],"lesson":"Do your work when the time is right. The hippo waits out the heat in the water and grazes in the cool of night.","about":"The hippopotamus (Hippopotamus amphibius; pl.: hippopotamuses or hippopotami), often shortened to hippo (pl.: hippos), further qualified as the common hippopotamus, Nile hippopotamus and river hippopotamus, is a large semiaquatic mammal native to sub-Saharan Africa.","description":"Species of large semi-aquatic mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Portrait_Hippopotamus_in_the_water.jpg/1920px-Portrait_Hippopotamus_in_the_water.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Muhammad Mahdi Karim","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_Hippopotamus_in_the_water.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippopotamus","sci":"Hippopotamus amphibius","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":141},
{"name":"Pygmy Hippopotamus","page":"Pygmy_hippopotamus","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and swamps of Liberia, Sierra Leone, Guinea and Ivory Coast","diet":"Ferns, leaves and fallen fruit","facts":["It's about half as tall as a common hippo, and much lighter.","Unlike the herd-living common hippo, it lives alone or in twos, like a mated pair or a mother and calf.","It's shy and comes out at night, so little is known about it in the wild."],"lesson":"Quiet lives matter too. The shy pygmy hippo is rarely seen, and the forest needs it all the same.","about":"The pygmy hippopotamus or pygmy hippo (Choeropsis liberiensis) is a small hippopotamid which is native to the forests and swamps of West Africa, primarily in Liberia, with small populations in Sierra Leone, Guinea, and Ivory Coast. It has been extirpated from Nigeria and possibly Guinea-Bissau. The pygmy hippopotamus is reclusive and nocturnal.","description":"Species of semi-aquatic mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Hexaprotodon_liberiensis_in_Edinburgh_Zoo.JPG/1920px-Hexaprotodon_liberiensis_in_Edinburgh_Zoo.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bardrock","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexaprotodon_liberiensis_in_Edinburgh_Zoo.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pygmy_hippopotamus","sci":"Choeropsis liberiensis","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":142},
{"name":"White Rhinoceros","page":"White_rhinoceros","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands of southern Africa, mostly South Africa","diet":"Grass","facts":["It's the largest rhino.","Its wide mouth is made for grazing grass.","Only two northern white rhinos are left, both females."],"lesson":"Protect what's precious. The white rhino survives because people guard it day and night.","about":"The white rhinoceros, white rhino or square-lipped rhinoceros (Ceratotherium simum) is the largest extant species of rhinoceros. It has a wide mouth used for grazing and is the most social of all rhino species. The white rhinoceros consists of two subspecies: the southern white rhinoceros, with an estimated 17,464 individuals in the wild as of the end of 2023, and the much rarer northern white rhinoceros.","description":"Species of large land mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/109_Male_White_rhinoceros_walking_in_the_Kalahari_Desert_of_Namibia_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-109_Male_White_rhinoceros_walking_in_the_Kalahari_Desert_of_Namibia_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:109_Male_White_rhinoceros_walking_in_the_Kalahari_Desert_of_Namibia_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_rhinoceros","sci":"Ceratotherium simum","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":143},
{"name":"Black Rhinoceros","page":"Black_rhinoceros","group":"Mammal","where":"Bush and savannas of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Leaves, twigs and shrubs","facts":["Its pointed, hooked upper lip grabs leaves and twigs.","Despite its name, it's brown to gray.","Poaching for its horn has made it critically endangered."],"lesson":"Names can mislead. The black rhino isn't black, and the white one isn't white.","about":"The black rhinoceros (Diceros bicornis), also called the black rhino or the hooked-lip rhinoceros, is a species of rhinoceros native to East and Southern Africa, including Angola, Botswana, Eswatini, Kenya, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique, Namibia, South Africa, Tanzania, Zambia, and Zimbabwe. Although the species is referred to as black, its colours vary from brown to grey.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Black_Rhino_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg/1920px-Black_Rhino_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"AfricanConservation","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Rhino_at_Working_with_Wildlife.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_rhinoceros","sci":"Diceros bicornis","status":"CR","mass":36.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":144},
{"name":"Indian Rhinoceros","page":"Indian_rhinoceros","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands of northeastern India and Nepal","diet":"Grasses, leaves and water plants","facts":["It has just one horn.","Folds in its thick skin make it look like it's wearing armor plates.","Kaziranga National Park in India holds more of them than anywhere else."],"lesson":"Protection works. Guarded parks helped Indian rhinos grow from about 2,600 in 2006 to over 4,000 in 2022.","about":"The Indian rhinoceros (Rhinoceros unicornis), also known as the greater one-horned rhinoceros, great Indian rhinoceros or Indian rhino, is a rhinoceros species native to the Indian subcontinent. It is the second largest living rhinoceros and the largest in Asia, with adult males weighing 2.07–2.2 t (2.04–2.17 long tons; 2.28–2.43 short tons) and adult females 1.6 t (1.6 long tons; 1.8 short tons).","description":"Species of rhinoceros","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Great-Indian-one-horned-rhinoceros-at-Kaziranga-national-park-in-Assam-India.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Mayank1704","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great-Indian-one-horned-rhinoceros-at-Kaziranga-national-park-in-Assam-India.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_rhinoceros","sci":"Rhinoceros unicornis","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":145},
{"name":"Saiga Antelope","page":"Saiga_antelope","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry steppes of Kazakhstan, southern Russia and Mongolia","diet":"Grasses, herbs and shrubs","facts":["It has an odd, droopy, trunk-like nose.","In May 2015, a disease killed more than 120,000 in central Kazakhstan, over a third of all saigas.","It has bounced back since, to more than a million in Kazakhstan."],"lesson":"Even after a disaster, you can recover. The saiga lost most of its herds, and came roaring back.","about":"The saiga antelope (Saiga tatarica), or simply saiga, is a species of antelope which during antiquity inhabited a vast area of the Eurasian steppe, spanning the foothills of the Carpathian Mountains in the northwest and Caucasus in the southwest into Mongolia in the northeast and Dzungaria in the southeast. During the Pleistocene, it ranged across the mammoth steppe from the British Isles to Beringia.","description":"Species of antelope","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Saiga_antelope_at_the_Stepnoi_Sanctuary.jpg/1920px-Saiga_antelope_at_the_Stepnoi_Sanctuary.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andrey Giljov","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saiga_antelope_at_the_Stepnoi_Sanctuary.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saiga_antelope","sci":"Saiga tatarica","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":146},
{"name":"Markhor","page":"Markhor","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountains of Pakistan, Afghanistan, Tajikistan and Kashmir","diet":"Grasses, leaves and twigs","facts":["Males have huge corkscrew horns.","It's the national animal of Pakistan.","In winter it sometimes stands on its back legs to reach high branches."],"lesson":"Grow in your own shape. The markhor's horns twist up like corkscrews, which is why it's called the screw-horned goat.","about":"The markhor (Capra falconeri) is a large wild Capra species native to the mountain regions at the crossroads of Central and South Asia, including the Karakoram and Himalayas. It occurs in parts of Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Afghanistan, Pakistan, and India. It has been listed on the IUCN Red List as Near Threatened since 2015.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Markhor_Schraubenziege_Capra_falconeri_Zoo_Augsburg-02.jpg/1920px-Markhor_Schraubenziege_Capra_falconeri_Zoo_Augsburg-02.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rufus46","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Markhor_Schraubenziege_Capra_falconeri_Zoo_Augsburg-02.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markhor","sci":"Capra falconeri","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":147},
{"name":"Mountain Goat","page":"Mountain_goat","group":"Mammal","where":"Rocky peaks of western North America, from Alaska to the Rockies","diet":"Grasses, mosses, lichens and shrubs","facts":["Despite its name, it isn't a true goat.","Its hooves have rough pads that grip rock like climbing shoes.","Its woolly white double coat protects it from the mountain weather."],"lesson":"Grip and go. The mountain goat stays steady on cliffs where nothing else dares to stand.","about":"The mountain goat (Oreamnos americanus), also known as the Rocky Mountain goat, is a cloven-footed mammal that is endemic to the remote and rugged mountainous areas of western North America. A subalpine to truly alpine species, it is a sure-footed climber commonly seen on sheer rock faces, near-vertical cliffs and icy passages.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Mountain_Goat%2C_Enchantments_Basin.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Adam Schneider","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Goat,_Enchantments_Basin.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_goat","sci":"Oreamnos americanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":148},
{"name":"Bighorn Sheep","page":"Bighorn_sheep","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountains and deserts of western North America","diet":"Grasses and shrubs","facts":["A ram's horns can weigh as much as all the bones in its body.","Rams clash horns in fights, and their skulls are built to absorb the blows.","Its split hooves grip rock, so it can climb steep cliffs."],"lesson":"Stand your ground. A bighorn ram takes the hit, and keeps standing.","about":"The bighorn sheep (Ovis canadensis) or bighorn is a species of sheep native to North America. It is named for its large horns, a pair of them may weigh up to 14 kg (30 lb) the sheep altogether typically weigh up to 143 kg (315 lb). Recent genetic testing indicates three distinct subspecies of Ovis canadensis, one of which is endangered: O. c. sierrae.","description":"Species of sheep native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/New_Mexico_Bighorn_Sheep.JPG/1920px-New_Mexico_Bighorn_Sheep.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jwanamaker","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Mexico_Bighorn_Sheep.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bighorn_sheep","sci":"Ovis canadensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":149},
{"name":"Springbok","page":"Springbok","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry grasslands and scrub of southern Africa","diet":"Grasses, shrubs and succulents","facts":["It \"pronks,\" leaping high into the air with its legs held stiff.","It can go for long stretches without drinking, getting water from plants.","It's South Africa's national animal and the name of its rugby team."],"lesson":"Show your strength. The springbok's high leaps tell predators it's too fit to catch.","about":"The springbok or springbuck (Antidorcas marsupialis) is an antelope found mainly in south and southwest Africa. The sole living member of the genus Antidorcas, this bovid was first described by the German zoologist Eberhard August Wilhelm von Zimmermann in 1780. Three subspecies are identified.","description":"Antelope of southwest and south Africa","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/174_Male_Springbok_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-174_Male_Springbok_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:174_Male_Springbok_in_Etosha_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Springbok","sci":"Antidorcas marsupialis","status":"LC","mass":41.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":150},
{"name":"Gerenuk","page":"Gerenuk","group":"Mammal","where":"Dry bush of the Horn of Africa and East Africa","diet":"Leaves and shoots of thorny bushes","facts":["It stands up on its back legs to reach leaves over 2 meters (6.5 feet) high.","Its name comes from the Somali word for it, and it's also called the giraffe gazelle.","It doesn't need to drink water regularly."],"lesson":"Reach for it. The gerenuk stands up tall to get what other antelope can't.","about":"The gerenuk (Litocranius walleri), also known as the giraffe gazelle, is a long-necked, medium-sized antelope found mainly in the Somali region of East Africa. The sole member of the genus Litocranius, the gerenuk was first described by naturalist Victor Brooke in 1879. It is characterised by its long, slender neck and limbs.","description":"Long-necked species of antelope","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/San_diego_zoo_group_3_DSC_0908_%2835305473932%29.jpg/1920px-San_diego_zoo_group_3_DSC_0908_%2835305473932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Laura Wolf","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_diego_zoo_group_3_DSC_0908_(35305473932).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerenuk","sci":"Litocranius walleri","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":151},
{"name":"Pronghorn","page":"Pronghorn","group":"Mammal","where":"Grasslands and deserts of western North America","diet":"Grasses, sagebrush and wildflowers","facts":["It's the fastest land animal in the Americas.","Its closest relatives are giraffes and okapis, not antelope.","Its horns have a sheath that it sheds and regrows every year."],"lesson":"Keep your edge. The pronghorn is slower than a cheetah, but it can hold its top speed much longer.","about":"The pronghorn (Antilocapra americana) is a species of artiodactyl (even-toed, hoofed) mammal indigenous to interior western and central North America. Though not an antelope, it is known colloquially in North America as the American antelope, prong buck, pronghorn antelope, and prairie antelope, because it closely resembles the antelopes of the Old World and fills a similar ecological niche due to parallel evolution.","description":"Species of North American hoofed mammal","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Antilocapra_americana.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"www.naturespicsonline.com","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antilocapra_americana.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pronghorn","sci":"Antilocapra americana","status":"LC","mass":3.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":152},
{"name":"Dromedary","page":"Dromedary","group":"Mammal","where":"Deserts of North Africa and the Middle East, and wild in Australia","diet":"Desert plants, thorny shrubs and dry grass","facts":["Its hump stores fat, not water.","It can lose up to about 30 percent of its body water and survive.","It can close its nostrils against blowing sand."],"lesson":"Prepare for the dry times. The camel carries its own reserves, so it can keep going where others stop.","about":"The dromedary (Camelus dromedarius), also known as the dromedary camel, Arabian camel and one-humped camel, is a large camel of the genus Camelus with one hump on its back. It is the tallest of the three camel species; adult males stand 1.8–2.4 m (5.9–7.9 ft) at the shoulder, while females are 1.7–1.9 m (5.6–6.2 ft) tall.","description":"One-humped camel","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Camelus_dromedarius_in_Nuweiba.jpg/1920px-Camelus_dromedarius_in_Nuweiba.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Florian Prischl","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camelus_dromedarius_in_Nuweiba.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dromedary","sci":"Camelus dromedarius","status":null,"mass":500.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":153},
{"name":"Bactrian Camel","page":"Bactrian_camel","group":"Mammal","where":"Steppes and deserts of Central Asia, especially Mongolia","diet":"Grasses, shrubs and leaves","facts":["It has two humps.","It handles freezing winters and hot summers alike.","It can eat snow for water."],"lesson":"Be ready for anything. The Bactrian camel handles blistering summers and freezing winters in the same year.","about":"The Bactrian camel (Camelus bactrianus), also known as the Mongolian camel, domestic Bactrian camel, two-humped camel or double-humped camel, is a camel native to the steppes of Central Asia. It has two humps on its back, in contrast to the single-humped dromedary. Its population of 2 million exists mainly in the domesticated form.","description":"Species of mammal of Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Camelus_bactrianus_%2855026866188%29.jpg/1920px-Camelus_bactrianus_%2855026866188%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Christian Ursilva from Copenhagen, Denmark","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camelus_bactrianus_(55026866188).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bactrian_camel","sci":"Camelus bactrianus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":154},
{"name":"Vicuña","page":"Vicuña","group":"Mammal","where":"High grasslands of the Andes in Peru, Bolivia, Argentina and Chile","diet":"Grasses","facts":["Its wool is among the finest and most costly in the world.","It's the wild ancestor of the alpaca.","It's the national animal of Peru and appears on its coat of arms."],"lesson":"What's rare is precious. The vicuña's fine wool is worth so much that it's now protected by law.","about":"The vicuña (Lama vicugna) or vicuna (both, very rarely spelled vicugna, its Latin specific name) is a wild South American camelid which lives in high alpine areas of the Andes. It is a relative of the llama, the domesticated descendant of the guanaco, the only other still wild South American camelid. The vicuña is believed to be the ancestor of domesticated alpaca, which is raised for its coat.","description":"Wild South American camelid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Vicuna_%2889163948%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Vicuna_%2889163948%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Thomas Quine","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicuna_(89163948)_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicu%C3%B1a","sci":"Vicugna vicugna","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":155},
{"name":"Malayan Tapir","page":"Malayan_tapir","group":"Mammal","where":"Rainforests of Malaysia, southern Thailand, Myanmar and Sumatra","diet":"Leaves, shoots and fruit","facts":["Its black-and-white coat breaks up its outline, so a resting tapir can look like a big rock.","Its long, flexible snout works like a short trunk.","Its babies are brown with white stripes and spots, to hide in the dappled forest light."],"lesson":"You'll grow into yourself. The tapir starts out striped and spotted, and changes as it grows.","about":"The Malayan tapir (Tapirus indicus), also called Asian tapir, Asiatic tapir, oriental tapir, Indian tapir, piebald tapir, or black-and-white tapir, is the only living tapir species outside of the Americas. It is native to Southeast Asia from the Malay Peninsula to Sumatra. It has been listed as Endangered on the IUCN Red List since 2008, as the population is estimated to comprise fewer than 2,500 mature individuals.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Schabrackentapir_Tapirus_indicus_Tiergarten-Nuernberg-1.jpg/1920px-Schabrackentapir_Tapirus_indicus_Tiergarten-Nuernberg-1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rufus46","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schabrackentapir_Tapirus_indicus_Tiergarten-Nuernberg-1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malayan_tapir","sci":"Tapirus indicus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":156},
{"name":"North Sulawesi Babirusa","page":"North_Sulawesi_babirusa","group":"Mammal","where":"Tropical forests of Sulawesi and nearby islands, Indonesia","diet":"Fruit, leaves, roots and nuts","facts":["The male's upper tusks grow right through the top of its snout and curve toward its forehead.","Its name means \"pig-deer\" in Malay.","It has two pairs of big tusks, both grown from its canine teeth."],"lesson":"Be your own kind of strange. The babirusa's tusks grow where no other animal's would.","about":"The North Sulawesi babirusa (Babyrousa celebensis) is a species of suid native to Sulawesi and some nearby islands (Lembeh, Buton and Muna) in Indonesia. It has two pairs of large tusks composed of enlarged canine teeth. The upper canines penetrate the top of the snout, curving back toward the forehead.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Hirscheber1a.jpg/1920px-Hirscheber1a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Masteraah at German Wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hirscheber1a.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Sulawesi_babirusa","sci":"Babyrousa celebensis","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":157},
{"name":"Common Warthog","page":"Common_warthog","group":"Mammal","where":"Savannas and grasslands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Grasses, roots and bulbs","facts":["It kneels on padded front \"wrists\" to graze.","Its tusks curve so much that one won't lie flat on a table.","The tough pads on its wrists start forming before it's even born."],"lesson":"Get down to what you need. The warthog drops to its knees to reach the short grass it lives on.","about":"The common warthog (Phacochoerus africanus) is a wild member of the pig family (Suidae) found in grassland, savanna, and woodland in sub-Saharan Africa. In the past, it was commonly treated as a subspecies of P. aethiopicus, but today that scientific name is restricted to the desert warthog of northern Kenya, Somalia, and eastern Ethiopia.","description":"Wild member of the pig family","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Southern_warthog_%28Phacochoerus_africanus_sundevallii%29_male.jpg/1920px-Southern_warthog_%28Phacochoerus_africanus_sundevallii%29_male.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_warthog_(Phacochoerus_africanus_sundevallii)_male.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_warthog","sci":"Phacochoerus africanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":158},
{"name":"Key Deer","page":"Key_deer","group":"Mammal","where":"Only the Florida Keys","diet":"Mangroves, palm berries and other plants","facts":["It lives only in the Florida Keys, mostly on Big Pine Key.","It's the smallest kind of white-tailed deer: a buck stands only about 76 cm (30 inches) at the shoulder.","It swims between islands."],"lesson":"Small places need protecting too. The Key deer came back from as few as 25 after a national wildlife refuge was set up for it in 1957.","about":"The Key deer (Odocoileus virginianus clavium) is an endangered subspecies of the white-tailed deer that lives only in the Florida Keys. It is the smallest extant North American deer subspecies.","description":"Subspecies of deer endemic to the Florida Keys","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Key_Deer_JCB.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Joseph C Boone","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Key_Deer_JCB.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_deer","sci":"Odocoileus virginianus clavium","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":159},
{"name":"Asian Elephant","page":"Asian_elephant","group":"Mammal","where":"Forests and grasslands from India to Southeast Asia","diet":"Grasses, bark, leaves and fruit","facts":["It's the largest land animal in Asia.","Males usually have big tusks, while females have tiny ones or none at all.","Its trunk has one finger-like tip, where the African elephant has two."],"lesson":"Remember your friends. Asian elephants keep wide circles of companions and are known to greet one another.","about":"The Asian elephant (Elephas maximus), also known as the Asiatic elephant, is the only living species in the genus Elephas. It is the largest living land animal in Asia and the second largest living elephantid in the world.","description":"Second-largest species of elephant","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Elephas_maximus_%28Bandipur%29.jpg/1920px-Elephas_maximus_%28Bandipur%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Yathin S Krishnappa","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephas_maximus_(Bandipur).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asian_elephant","sci":"Elephas maximus","status":"EN","mass":95.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":160},
{"name":"Wild Yak","page":"Wild_yak","group":"Mammal","where":"High, cold grasslands of the Tibetan Plateau","diet":"Grasses, herbs and lichens","facts":["It's the wild ancestor of the domestic yak.","Its long, shaggy hair and woolly undercoat keep out the cold.","A bull can weigh over a ton and stand 2 meters (6.5 feet) tall at the shoulder."],"lesson":"Thrive where it's hard. The wild yak is at home where the air is thin and the winters are bitter.","about":"The wild yak (Bos mutus) is a large, wild bovine native to the Himalayas. It is the ancestor of the domestic yak (Bos grunniens).","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Wild_Yak_Stuffed.jpg/1920px-Wild_Yak_Stuffed.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jim, the Photographer","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wild_Yak_Stuffed.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_yak","sci":"Bos mutus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":161},
{"name":"Muskox","page":"Muskox","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic tundra of Canada and Greenland, and brought back to Alaska and Norway","diet":"Grasses, sedges, willows and mosses","facts":["Its soft underwool, called qiviut, is prized for being soft, long and warm.","A herd facing wolves forms a ring, horns out, with the young inside.","It lived alongside woolly mammoths in the Ice Age."],"lesson":"Stand together. Muskoxen face danger shoulder to shoulder, with the young safe inside.","about":"The muskox (Ovibos moschatus) is a hoofed mammal of the family Bovidae. Native to the Arctic, it is noted for its thick coat and for the strong odor emitted by males during the seasonal rut, from which its name derives. This musky odor has the effect of attracting females during mating season.","description":"Species of arctic land mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Muskox_%28Ovibos_moschatus%29_male_Dovrefjell_4.jpg/1920px-Muskox_%28Ovibos_moschatus%29_male_Dovrefjell_4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muskox_(Ovibos_moschatus)_male_Dovrefjell_4.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muskox","sci":"Ovibos moschatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":162},
{"name":"Takin","page":"Takin","group":"Mammal","where":"Mountain forests of the eastern Himalayas and China","diet":"Leaves, grasses and bamboo shoots","facts":["It's the national animal of Bhutan.","It gives off an oily, strong-smelling substance all over its body, which it uses to mark trees.","It looks like a cross between a cow and a moose, but its closest relatives are sheep."],"lesson":"Don't go by first looks. The takin looks like a cow but is a cousin of sheep.","about":"The takin (Budorcas taxicolor) is a large species of ungulate of the subfamily Caprinae found in the eastern Himalayas. It includes three subspecies, the Mishmi takin (B. t. taxicolor), the golden takin (B. t. bedfordi), and the Bhutan takin (B. t. whitei), and is closely related to the Tibetan (or Sichuan) takin (B. tibetana).","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Thimphu-Takin-Reservat-06-2015-gje.jpg/1920px-Thimphu-Takin-Reservat-06-2015-gje.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Gerd Eichmann","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thimphu-Takin-Reservat-06-2015-gje.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takin","sci":"Budorcas taxicolor","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":163},
{"name":"Greater Kudu","page":"Greater_kudu","group":"Mammal","where":"Woodlands and bush of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Leaves, shoots, flowers and fruit","facts":["Males have long, spiral horns with two and a half twists.","It escapes predators by leaping over shrubs and small trees.","Its horn is blown as a musical instrument, including as a shofar."],"lesson":"Grow slowly, stand tall. A kudu's spiral horns take years to make their full turns.","about":"The greater kudu (Tragelaphus strepsiceros) is a large woodland antelope, found throughout eastern and southern Africa. Despite occupying such widespread territory, they are sparsely populated in most areas due to declining habitat, deforestation, and poaching. The greater kudu is one of two species commonly known as kudu, the other being the lesser kudu, T. imberbis.","description":"Species of woodland antelope","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Male_greater_kudu.jpg/1920px-Male_greater_kudu.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"L0k1m0nk33 at English Wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_greater_kudu.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_kudu","sci":"Tragelaphus strepsiceros","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":164},
{"name":"Humpback Whale","page":"Humpback_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"Every ocean, from polar feeding grounds to tropical calving waters","diet":"Krill and small fish","facts":["Males sing complex songs and change them over time, and other whales copy the changes.","Groups blow \"bubble nets\" to trap fish.","It migrates thousands of kilometers between feeding and breeding waters."],"lesson":"Keep your song fresh. Humpback whales keep changing their songs, and the new tunes spread across the ocean.","about":"The humpback whale (Megaptera novaeangliae) is a species of baleen whale. It is a rorqual (a member of the family Balaenopteridae) and is the only species in the genus Megaptera. Adults range in length from 14–17 m (46–56 ft) and weigh up to 40 metric tons (44 short tons).","description":"Large baleen whale species","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Humpback_whale_breaching_off_Cabo_San_Lucas.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Juan Cruzado Cortés","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humpback_whale_breaching_off_Cabo_San_Lucas.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humpback_whale","sci":"Megaptera novaeangliae","status":"LC","mass":45000.0,"length":16.0,"life":47.5,"n":165},
{"name":"Sperm Whale","page":"Sperm_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"Deep oceans all over the world","diet":"Squid, including giant squid, and fish","facts":["It has the biggest brain of any animal.","It can dive as deep as 2,250 meters (7,380 feet) and stay down for up to two hours.","It's the largest predator with teeth."],"lesson":"Go deep. The sperm whale dives into the dark deep sea, for up to two hours at a time, to find its food.","about":"The sperm whale or cachalot (Physeter macrocephalus) is the largest of the toothed whales and the largest toothed predator. It is the only living member of the genus Physeter and one of three extant species in the sperm whale superfamily Physeteroidea, along with the pygmy sperm whale and dwarf sperm whale of the genus Kogia.","description":"Largest species of toothed whale","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Mother_and_baby_sperm_whale.jpg/1920px-Mother_and_baby_sperm_whale.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Gabriel Barathieu","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mother_and_baby_sperm_whale.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sperm_whale","sci":"Physeter macrocephalus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":166},
{"name":"Beluga Whale","page":"Beluga_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic and sub-Arctic seas","diet":"Fish, squid, shrimp and crabs","facts":["It's called the \"sea canary\" for its chirps and whistles.","Unlike most whales, it can turn its head.","It's white, which hides it among the ice."],"lesson":"Speak up. The beluga's chirps and whistles keep its pod together under the ice.","about":"The beluga whale or the white whale (Delphinapterus leucas) is an Arctic and sub-Arctic cetacean. It is one of two living members of the family Monodontidae, along with the narwhal, and the only member of the genus Delphinapterus. It is also known as the sea canary, due to its high-pitched calls; and the melonhead, though that more commonly refers to the melon-headed whale, which is an oceanic dolphin.","description":"Species of whale","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Oceanogr%C3%A0fic_29102004.jpg/1920px-Oceanogr%C3%A0fic_29102004.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Javier Yaya Tur / CIUDAD DE LAS ARTES Y LAS CIENCIAS from Valencia, España","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oceanogr%C3%A0fic_29102004.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beluga_whale","sci":"Delphinapterus leucas","status":"LC","mass":60.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":167},
{"name":"Gray Whale","page":"Gray_whale","group":"Mammal","where":"The North Pacific, from the Arctic to Baja California","diet":"Tiny animals it sucks from the mud of the seafloor","facts":["It rolls onto its side on the seafloor and sucks up mud, straining out the small animals in it.","It makes one of the longest migrations of any mammal.","Its calves are born in shallow lagoons in Baja California, safe from sharks and orcas."],"lesson":"Make the long trip. Gray whales swim from the Arctic to Mexico and back every year.","about":"The gray whale (Eschrichtius robustus), also known as the grey whale, is a baleen whale that migrates between feeding and breeding grounds yearly. It reaches a length of 14.9–15.2 m (49–50 ft), a weight of up to 41 to 45 tonnes (45 to 50 short tons; 40 to 44 long tons) and lives between 55 and 70 years, although one female was estimated to be 75–80 years of age.","description":"Species of baleen whale","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Ballena_gris_adulta_con_su_ballenato.jpg/1920px-Ballena_gris_adulta_con_su_ballenato.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"José Eugenio Gómez Rodríguez","license":"CC BY 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ballena_gris_adulta_con_su_ballenato.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_whale","sci":"Eschrichtius robustus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":168},
{"name":"Vaquita","page":"Vaquita","group":"Mammal","where":"Only the northern Gulf of California, Mexico","diet":"Fish and squid","facts":["It's on the brink of extinction, with only a handful left.","It's the smallest porpoise.","It's dying out mainly by drowning in gillnets set illegally for another fish, the totoaba."],"lesson":"It's never too late to care. The last vaquitas still have a chance if the nets come out of their waters.","about":"The vaquita (Phocoena sinus) is a species of porpoise endemic to the northern end of the Gulf of California in Baja California, Mexico. Reaching a maximum body length of 150 cm (4.9 ft) (females) or 140 cm (4.6 ft) (males), it is the smallest of all living cetaceans.","description":"Endangered species of porpoise","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Vaquita4_Olson_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Paula Olson, NOAA","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaquita4_Olson_NOAA.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaquita","sci":"Phocoena sinus","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":169},
{"name":"Amazon River Dolphin","page":"Amazon_river_dolphin","group":"Mammal","where":"Rivers of the Amazon and Orinoco basins","diet":"Fish, crabs and turtles","facts":["It's pink, and males are pinker.","It's the largest river dolphin.","The bones of its neck aren't fused, so it can turn its head to steer between flooded trees."],"lesson":"Bend so you don't break. The river dolphin's flexible neck lets it weave through a flooded forest.","about":"The Amazon river dolphin (Inia geoffrensis), also known as the boto, bufeo or pink river dolphin, is a species of toothed whale endemic to South America and is classified in the family Iniidae. Three subspecies are currently recognized: I. g. geoffrensis (Amazon river dolphin), I. g. boliviensis (Bolivian river dolphin) and I. g. humboldtiana (Orinoco river dolphin).","description":"Species of toothed whale","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Amazonas-Flussdelfin_Orinoko3.jpg/1920px-Amazonas-Flussdelfin_Orinoko3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Oceancetaceen","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amazonas-Flussdelfin_Orinoko3.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_river_dolphin","sci":"Inia geoffrensis","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":170},
{"name":"Walrus","page":"Walrus","group":"Mammal","where":"Arctic seas around the North Pole","diet":"Clams and other animals of the seafloor","facts":["Both males and females have tusks.","It uses its tusks to haul itself out onto the ice.","It finds clams with its sensitive whiskers and sucks them out of their shells."],"lesson":"Use what you've got. The walrus's tusks are tools for climbing, not just for show.","about":"The walrus (Odobenus rosmarus; plural walrus or walruses) is a large pinniped marine mammal with discontinuous distribution about the North Pole in the Arctic Ocean and subarctic seas of the Northern Hemisphere. It is the only extant species in the family Odobenidae and genus Odobenus.","description":"Species of marine mammal with tusks","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Walrus_in_the_Russian_Arctic_National_Park%2C_Novaya_Zemlya_2015-2.jpg/1920px-Walrus_in_the_Russian_Arctic_National_Park%2C_Novaya_Zemlya_2015-2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Nixette","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walrus_in_the_Russian_Arctic_National_Park,_Novaya_Zemlya_2015-2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walrus","sci":"Odobenus rosmarus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":171},
{"name":"Leopard Seal","page":"Leopard_seal","group":"Mammal","where":"Pack ice around Antarctica","diet":"Penguins, other seals, fish and krill","facts":["It's the second-largest seal in the Antarctic.","Its back teeth lock together to strain krill from the water, like a whale.","Its coat has dark spots like a leopard's, which gives it its name."],"lesson":"Have more than one way to eat. The leopard seal hunts penguins, and strains krill when it has to.","about":"The leopard seal (Hydrurga leptonyx), also referred to as the sea leopard, is a long and muscular seal native to the Southern Ocean of Antarctica and southern Patagonia. It is the second largest species of seal in the Antarctic (after the southern elephant seal). It is the same length as, but half the weight of, a walrus.","description":"Macropredatory species of Antarctic seal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Antarctic_Sound-2016-Brown_Bluff%E2%80%93Leopard_seal_%28Hydrurga_leptonyx%29_04.jpg/1920px-Antarctic_Sound-2016-Brown_Bluff%E2%80%93Leopard_seal_%28Hydrurga_leptonyx%29_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Godot13","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antarctic_Sound-2016-Brown_Bluff%E2%80%93Leopard_seal_(Hydrurga_leptonyx)_04.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopard_seal","sci":"Hydrurga leptonyx","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":172},
{"name":"Harp Seal","page":"Harp_seal","group":"Mammal","where":"The North Atlantic and the Arctic Ocean","diet":"Fish and crustaceans","facts":["Pups are born with white coats, which they keep for only a few weeks.","The dark marking on an adult's back is shaped like a harp.","Mothers nurse their pups for only about 12 days, and don't eat the whole time."],"lesson":"Grow fast when you must. Harp seal pups get all their mother's care in under two weeks.","about":"The harp seal (Pagophilus groenlandicus), also known as the saddleback seal or Greenland seal, is a species of earless seal, or true seal, native to the northernmost Atlantic Ocean and Arctic Ocean. Originally in the genus Phoca with a number of other species, it was reclassified into the monotypic genus Pagophilus in 1844.","description":"Species of mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Harp_Seal_%28Pagophilus_groenlandicus%29%2C_Greenland_Sea_IMG_5075.jpg/1920px-Harp_Seal_%28Pagophilus_groenlandicus%29%2C_Greenland_Sea_IMG_5075.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Brian Gratwicke from DC, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harp_Seal_(Pagophilus_groenlandicus),_Greenland_Sea_IMG_5075.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harp_seal","sci":"Pagophilus groenlandicus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":173},
{"name":"Southern Elephant Seal","page":"Southern_elephant_seal","group":"Mammal","where":"The Southern Ocean and the islands around Antarctica","diet":"Squid and fish","facts":["It's the largest seal in the world.","The males' big, trunk-like noses make their roars louder.","It dives again and again, 20 minutes or more each time, to hunt squid 400 to 1,000 meters down."],"lesson":"Your voice carries when you use it. The elephant seal's huge nose makes sure every rival hears it.","about":"The southern elephant seal (Mirounga leonina) is one of two species of elephant seals. It is the largest member of the clade Pinnipedia and the order Carnivora, as well as the largest extant marine mammal that is not a cetacean. It gets its name from its massive size and the large proboscis of the adult male, which is used to produce very loud roars, especially during the breeding season.","description":"Species of marine mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/2020-11_Kerguelen_Islands_-_Southern_elephant_seal_30.jpg/1920px-2020-11_Kerguelen_Islands_-_Southern_elephant_seal_30.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Antoine Lamielle","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-11_Kerguelen_Islands_-_Southern_elephant_seal_30.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_elephant_seal","sci":"Mirounga leonina","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":174},
{"name":"Dugong","page":"Dugong","group":"Mammal","where":"Warm coastal waters of the Indian and western Pacific Oceans","diet":"Seagrass","facts":["It may have inspired sailors' tales of mermaids.","It's the only plant-eating mammal that lives only in the sea.","Its closest living relatives on land are elephants."],"lesson":"Gentle is strong. The dugong spends its whole life grazing peacefully in the seagrass.","about":"The dugong (Dugong dugon) is a marine mammal. It is one of four living species of the order Sirenia, which also includes three species of manatees. It is the only living representative of the once-diverse family Dugongidae; its closest modern relative, Steller's sea cow (Hydrodamalis gigas), was hunted to extinction in the 18th century.","description":"Species of marine mammal","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Dugong.JPG/1920px-Dugong.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Gejuni","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dugong.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dugong","sci":"Dugong dugon","status":"VU","mass":200.0,"length":null,"life":null,"n":175},
{"name":"California Sea Lion","page":"California_sea_lion","group":"Mammal","where":"The Pacific coast of North America, from southeast Alaska to central Mexico","diet":"Fish and squid","facts":["It's very smart and easy to train; even the US Navy trains them.","It walks on land by turning its back flippers forward.","Males bark loudly to guard their stretch of beach."],"lesson":"Learn and you'll thrive. The clever sea lion can be trained for all kinds of jobs, from balancing balls to helping the US Navy.","about":"The California sea lion (Zalophus californianus) is a coastal eared seal native to western North America. It is one of six species of sea lions. Its natural habitat ranges from southeast Alaska to central Mexico, including the Gulf of California.","description":"Species of carnivore","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/California_Sea_Lion%2C_Monterey%2C_California%2C_United_States_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_203598492.jpg/1920px-California_Sea_Lion%2C_Monterey%2C_California%2C_United_States_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_203598492.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"(c) Jonathan Eisen, some rights reserved (CC BY)","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Sea_Lion,_Monterey,_California,_United_States_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_203598492.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_sea_lion","sci":"Zalophus californianus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":176},
{"name":"Harpy Eagle","page":"Harpy_eagle","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests from southern Mexico to northern Argentina","diet":"Sloths, monkeys and other tree-dwelling animals","facts":["It's the largest bird of prey in its range, and among the largest eagles alive.","It snatches sloths and monkeys right out of the treetops.","Outside Panama and Costa Rica, it's nearly gone from Central America."],"lesson":"Strength is nothing without focus. The harpy eagle waits and watches, then strikes once.","about":"The harpy eagle (Harpia harpyja) is a large neotropical species of eagle. It is also called the American harpy eagle to distinguish it from the Papuan eagle, which is sometimes known as the New Guinea harpy eagle or Papuan harpy eagle. It is the largest bird of prey throughout its range, and among the largest extant species of eagles in the world.","description":"Species of eagle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Harpia_harpyja_001_800.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"http://www.birdphotos.com","license":"CC BY 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harpia_harpyja_001_800.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harpy_eagle","sci":"Harpia harpyja","status":"VU","mass":7.5,"length":null,"life":null,"n":177},
{"name":"Philippine Eagle","page":"Philippine_eagle","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests of the Philippines, mostly Mindanao","diet":"Colugos, civets, monkeys and birds","facts":["It's also called the monkey-eating eagle.","It's among the largest eagles in the world.","It's the national bird of the Philippines."],"lesson":"Guard what's irreplaceable. A Philippine eagle pair raises just one chick at a time.","about":"The Philippine eagle (Pithecophaga jefferyi), also known as the monkey-eating eagle or great Philippine eagle, is a critically endangered species of eagle of the family Accipitridae which is endemic to forests in the Philippines. It has brown and white-coloured plumage, a shaggy crest, and generally measures 86 to 102 cm (2.82 to 3.35 ft) in length and weighs 4.04 to 8.0 kg (8.9 to 17.6 lb).","description":"Endangered species of eagle in the Philippines","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Pamarayeg_IIIx2_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Aimee Valencia","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pamarayeg_IIIx2_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippine_eagle","sci":"Pithecophaga jefferyi","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":178},
{"name":"Golden Eagle","page":"Golden_eagle","group":"Bird","where":"Mountains and open country across the Northern Hemisphere","diet":"Hares, rabbits, marmots and ground squirrels","facts":["It can dive at over 240 km/h (150 mph).","Falconers in Kazakhstan and Mongolia hunt foxes with trained golden eagles.","A wild female in Wyoming weighed 7.7 kg (17 lb), the heaviest on record."],"lesson":"Aim high. The golden eagle hunts from the sky, seeing far more than the animals below.","about":"The golden eagle (Aquila chrysaetos) is a bird of prey living in the Northern Hemisphere. It is the most widely distributed species of eagle. Like all eagles, it belongs to the family Accipitridae.","description":"Species of eagle","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/015_Wild_Golden_Eagle_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_%28Switzerland%29_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-015_Wild_Golden_Eagle_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_%28Switzerland%29_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:015_Wild_Golden_Eagle_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_(Switzerland)_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_eagle","sci":"Aquila chrysaetos","status":"LC","mass":5.194,"length":null,"life":null,"n":179},
{"name":"Osprey","page":"Osprey","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts, rivers and lakes almost worldwide, including Florida","diet":"Fish","facts":["It eats almost nothing but fish.","It plunges feet-first into the water to grab fish.","Its outer toe swings backward, so it can hold a slippery fish with two toes in front and two behind."],"lesson":"Get a good grip. The osprey's toes turn to hold on to what it catches.","about":"The osprey (Pandion haliaetus), historically known as sea hawk, river hawk, and fish hawk, is a diurnal, fish-eating bird of prey with a cosmopolitan range. It is a large raptor, reaching more than 60 cm (24 in) in length and a wingspan of 180 cm (71 in). It is brown on the upperparts and predominantly greyish on the head and underparts.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Osprey_Perched_Snag_Heislerville.jpg/1920px-Osprey_Perched_Snag_Heislerville.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler / Focus On Wildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osprey_Perched_Snag_Heislerville.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osprey","sci":"Pandion haliaetus","status":"LC","mass":1.4,"length":null,"life":null,"n":180},
{"name":"Secretarybird","page":"Secretarybird","group":"Bird","where":"Open grasslands and savannas of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Snakes, lizards, rodents and insects","facts":["It hunts on foot, stomping its prey with powerful kicks.","It paces along at about 120 steps a minute while it hunts.","It's on the coats of arms of Sudan and South Africa."],"lesson":"Keep your footing. The secretarybird beats snakes by striking fast and stepping back.","about":"The secretarybird or secretary bird (Sagittarius serpentarius) is a large bird of prey that is endemic to Africa. It is mostly terrestrial, spending most of its time on the ground, and is usually found in the open grasslands and savanna of the sub-Saharan region. John Frederick Miller described the species in 1779.","description":"Bird of prey","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Secretary_bird_Mara_for_WC.jpg/1920px-Secretary_bird_Mara_for_WC.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sumeet Moghe","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secretary_bird_Mara_for_WC.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secretarybird","sci":"Sagittarius serpentarius","status":"EN","mass":4.052,"length":null,"life":null,"n":181},
{"name":"Andean Condor","page":"Andean_condor","group":"Bird","where":"The Andes and the Pacific coast of South America","diet":"Carrion","facts":["It's one of the heaviest birds that can fly.","It can soar for hours, riding rising air, with barely a flap.","It's a national symbol of Bolivia, Chile, Colombia, Ecuador and Peru."],"lesson":"Ride the currents. The condor saves its strength by letting warm air lift it.","about":"The Andean condor (Vultur gryphus) is a South American New World vulture and is the only extant member of the genus Vultur. It is found in the Andes mountains and adjacent Pacific coasts of western South America.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/AndeanCondorMale.jpg/1920px-AndeanCondorMale.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AndeanCondorMale.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andean_condor","sci":"Vultur gryphus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":182},
{"name":"King Vulture","page":"King_vulture","group":"Bird","where":"Tropical lowland forests from southern Mexico to northern Argentina","diet":"Carrion","facts":["Its bare head is colored orange, red, purple and yellow.","Its strong beak can tear open carcasses that other vultures can't.","Other vultures often wait for it to open a carcass before they eat."],"lesson":"Lead by doing. The king vulture makes the first cut in a carcass, which lets the smaller vultures eat too.","about":"The king vulture (Sarcoramphus papa) is a large bird found in Central and South America. It is a member of the New World vulture family Cathartidae. This vulture lives predominantly in tropical lowland forests stretching from southern Mexico to northern Argentina.","description":"Large bird found in Central and South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Urubu_rei_-_Sarcoramphus_papa.jpg/1920px-Urubu_rei_-_Sarcoramphus_papa.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Renato Augusto Martins","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urubu_rei_-_Sarcoramphus_papa.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_vulture","sci":"Sarcoramphus papa","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":183},
{"name":"Bearded Vulture","page":"Bearded_vulture","group":"Bird","where":"High mountains of southern Europe, Africa and Asia","diet":"Bones and bone marrow","facts":["Bones make up most of its diet.","It drops large bones from the air onto rocks to break them.","It dyes its feathers orange by bathing in iron-rich mud."],"lesson":"Make the most of what's left. The bearded vulture lives on the bones everyone else leaves behind.","about":"The bearded vulture (Gypaetus barbatus), also known as the lammergeier and ossifrage, is a very large bird of prey in the monotypic genus Gypaetus. The bearded vulture is the only known vertebrate whose diet consists of 70–90% bone.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/010e_Wild_Bearded_Vulture_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_%28Switzerland%29_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-010e_Wild_Bearded_Vulture_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_%28Switzerland%29_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:010e_Wild_Bearded_Vulture_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_(Switzerland)_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bearded_vulture","sci":"Gypaetus barbatus","status":"NT","mass":6.175,"length":null,"life":null,"n":184},
{"name":"Swallow-tailed Kite","page":"Swallow-tailed_kite","group":"Bird","where":"Summer in the southeastern US, especially Florida; winter in South America","diet":"Insects, lizards, frogs and small snakes","facts":["It drinks while flying, skimming water from the surface with its beak.","It has a long, deeply forked tail, like a swallow's.","It migrates from Florida all the way to South America."],"lesson":"Be graceful under pressure. The swallow-tailed kite usually catches and eats its food without stopping its flight.","about":"The swallow-tailed kite (Elanoides forficatus) is a pernine raptor which breeds from the southeastern United States to eastern Peru and northern Argentina. It is the only species in the genus Elanoides. Most North and Central American breeders winter in South America where the species is resident year round.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Swallow-tailed_Kite_%2834163638494%29.jpg/1920px-Swallow-tailed_Kite_%2834163638494%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andy Morffew from Itchen Abbas, Hampshire, UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swallow-tailed_Kite_(34163638494).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swallow-tailed_kite","sci":"Elanoides forficatus","status":"LC","mass":0.612,"length":null,"life":null,"n":185},
{"name":"Snail Kite","page":"Snail_kite","group":"Bird","where":"Freshwater marshes of Florida, the Caribbean, and Central and South America","diet":"Apple snails","facts":["It eats almost nothing but apple snails.","Its thin, hooked beak pulls the snail out of its shell.","In the US, it lives only in Florida."],"lesson":"Specialize, but stay alert. The snail kite is a master of one food, so it depends on keeping its marsh healthy.","about":"The snail kite (Rostrhamus sociabilis) is a bird of prey within the family Accipitridae, which also includes the eagles, hawks, and Old World vultures. Its relative, the slender-billed kite, is now again placed in Helicolestes, making the genus Rostrhamus monotypic. Usually, it is placed in the Milvine kites, but the validity of that grouping is under investigation.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/087_Male_Snail_kite_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-087_Male_Snail_kite_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:087_Male_Snail_kite_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snail_kite","sci":"Rostrhamus sociabilis","status":"LC","mass":0.446,"length":null,"life":null,"n":186},
{"name":"Great Horned Owl","page":"Great_horned_owl","group":"Bird","where":"Most of North and Central America, including Florida","diet":"Rabbits, rodents, skunks and birds","facts":["It's one of the few animals that regularly hunt skunks.","Its \"horns\" are tufts of feathers, not ears.","It nests earlier in the year than almost any other North American bird."],"lesson":"Don't be put off by a bad smell. The great horned owl eats what others won't touch.","about":"The great horned owl (Bubo virginianus), also known as the tiger owl (originally derived from early naturalists' description as the \"winged tiger\" or \"tiger of the air\") or the hoot owl, is a large owl native to the Americas. It is an extremely adaptable bird with a vast range and is the most widely distributed true owl in the Americas.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Bubo_virginianus_06.jpg/1920px-Bubo_virginianus_06.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubo_virginianus_06.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_horned_owl","sci":"Bubo virginianus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":187},
{"name":"Burrowing Owl","page":"Burrowing_owl","group":"Bird","where":"Open grasslands of the Americas, including Florida","diet":"Insects and small rodents","facts":["It lives in underground burrows, often ones first dug by prairie dogs, ground squirrels or badgers.","Unlike most owls, it's often out by day.","When threatened in its burrow, it makes a rattling hiss like a rattlesnake."],"lesson":"Borrow and make it yours. The burrowing owl moves into tunnels others have dug.","about":"The burrowing owl (Athene cunicularia) is a small, long-legged, primarily terrestrial—though not flightless—species of owl native to the open landscapes of North and South America. They are typically found in grasslands, rangelands, agricultural areas, deserts, or any other open, dry area with low vegetation.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Southern_burrowing_owl_%28Athene_cunicularia_cunicularia%29_Colonia.jpg/1920px-Southern_burrowing_owl_%28Athene_cunicularia_cunicularia%29_Colonia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_burrowing_owl_(Athene_cunicularia_cunicularia)_Colonia.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burrowing_owl","sci":"Athene cunicularia","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":188},
{"name":"Eurasian Eagle-Owl","page":"Eurasian_eagle-owl","group":"Bird","where":"Forests, mountains and deserts of Europe and Asia","diet":"Rodents, hares and birds","facts":["It's one of the largest owls in the world.","It even hunts other owls, and it's a serious predator of long-eared owls.","It usually nests on cliff ledges, in gullies or among rocks."],"lesson":"Quiet confidence goes far. The eagle-owl rules the night without hurrying.","about":"The Eurasian eagle-owl (Bubo bubo) is a species of eagle-owl, a type of bird that resides much over Eurasia. It is often just called the eagle-owl in Europe and Asia. It is one of the largest species of owl.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Bubo_bubo_3_%28Martin_Mecnarowski%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Martin Mecnarowski (http://www.photomecan.eu/)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubo_bubo_3_(Martin_Mecnarowski).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurasian_eagle-owl","sci":"Bubo bubo","status":"LC","mass":2.992,"length":null,"life":null,"n":189},
{"name":"Elf Owl","page":"Elf_owl","group":"Bird","where":"Deserts of the southwestern US and Mexico","diet":"Insects, spiders and scorpions","facts":["It's the lightest owl in the world, about 40 grams (1.4 oz).","It nests in old woodpecker holes in saguaro cacti.","It plays dead when it's caught, so the predator relaxes its grip and it can escape."],"lesson":"Small and wise. The tiny elf owl survives the desert by using shelters others made.","about":"The elf owl (Micrathene whitneyi) is a small grayish-brown owl about the size of a sparrow found in the Southwestern United States, central Mexico, and the Baja California peninsula. It has pale yellow eyes highlighted by thin white \"eyebrows\" and a gray bill with a horn-colored tip. The elf owl frequently inhabits woodpecker holes in saguaro cacti; it also nests in natural tree cavities.","description":"Species of owl","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Micrathene_whitneyi_29APR12_Madera_Canyon_AZ.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"BBODO","license":"CC BY 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Micrathene_whitneyi_29APR12_Madera_Canyon_AZ.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elf_owl","sci":"Micrathene whitneyi","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":190},
{"name":"Wandering Albatross","page":"Wandering_albatross","group":"Bird","where":"The Southern Ocean around Antarctica","diet":"Squid and fish","facts":["It has the longest wingspan of any living bird, up to 3.7 meters (12 feet).","It glides for hours over the ocean, hardly flapping.","Some fly around the whole Southern Ocean three times in a year, over 120,000 km."],"lesson":"Go the distance. The albatross circles the Southern Ocean on wings that barely move.","about":"The snowy albatross (Diomedea exulans), also known as the wandering albatross, white-winged albatross, or goonie, is a large seabird from the family Diomedeidae; they have a circumpolar range in the Southern Ocean. It is the largest species of albatross and was long considered to be the same species as the Tristan albatross and the Antipodean albatross.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Diomedea_exulans_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg/1920px-Diomedea_exulans_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diomedea_exulans_-_SE_Tasmania.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowy_albatross","sci":"Diomedea exulans","status":"VU","mass":9.11,"length":null,"life":null,"n":191},
{"name":"Atlantic Puffin","page":"Atlantic_puffin","group":"Bird","where":"Sea cliffs and islands of the North Atlantic","diet":"Small fish like sand eels","facts":["It can catch several fish in one dive, holding them in a row with its grooved tongue.","It loses its bright face colors at sea in winter, and they come back in spring.","It \"flies\" underwater, using its wings to swim."],"lesson":"Carry a lot, gracefully. The puffin brings home a whole row of fish in one trip.","about":"The Atlantic puffin (Fratercula arctica), also known as the common puffin, is a species of seabird in the auk family. It is the only puffin native to the Atlantic Ocean; two related species, the tufted puffin and the horned puffin, are found in the northeastern Pacific.","description":"Species of seabird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Puffin_%28Fratercula_arctica%29.jpg/1920px-Puffin_%28Fratercula_arctica%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puffin_(Fratercula_arctica).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_puffin","sci":"Fratercula arctica","status":"VU","mass":0.376,"length":0.279,"life":null,"n":192},
{"name":"Blue-footed Booby","page":"Blue-footed_booby","group":"Bird","where":"Pacific coasts from Mexico to Peru, and the Galápagos","diet":"Fish","facts":["Males show off their bright blue feet in a strutting dance.","Its bright blue feet come from its diet, and help it win a mate.","It dives into the sea after fish, sometimes chasing them underwater."],"lesson":"Show what you've got. The male blue-footed booby wins his mate by proudly showing off his feet.","about":"The blue-footed booby (Sula nebouxii) is a species of marine bird in the family Sulidae. The species is native to subtropical and tropical regions of the eastern Pacific Ocean. It is one of six species of the genus Sula, commonly known as boobies.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Blue-footed-booby.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Benjamint444","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue-footed-booby.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue-footed_booby","sci":"Sula nebouxii","status":"LC","mass":1.801,"length":null,"life":null,"n":193},
{"name":"Magnificent Frigatebird","page":"Magnificent_frigatebird","group":"Bird","where":"Tropical coasts of the Atlantic and Pacific, including Florida","diet":"Fish and squid, sometimes stolen from other seabirds","facts":["Males puff up a red throat pouch like a balloon.","It pesters other seabirds until they cough up their meals.","It snatches fish, often flying fish, from the surface without landing."],"lesson":"Stay above it. The frigatebird does its fishing from the air, plucking food from the surface.","about":"The magnificent frigatebird (Fregata magnificens), frigate petrel or man o' war is a seabird of the frigatebird family Fregatidae. With a length of 89–114 centimetres (2 ft 11 in – 3 ft 9 in) and wingspan of 2.17–2.44 m (7 ft 1 in – 8 ft 0 in), it is the largest species of frigatebird.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Fregata_magnificens_-Galapagos%2C_Ecuador_-male-8_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Andrew Turner from Washington, DC, United States","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fregata_magnificens_-Galapagos,_Ecuador_-male-8_(1).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnificent_frigatebird","sci":"Fregata magnificens","status":"LC","mass":0.088,"length":null,"life":null,"n":194},
{"name":"Brown Pelican","page":"Brown_pelican","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts of the Americas, including Florida","diet":"Fish","facts":["It dives bill-first like a kingfisher, often going right under.","It's the state bird of Louisiana.","Pesticides nearly wiped it out, and it came back once DDT was banned."],"lesson":"Recovery is possible. The brown pelican bounced back once the poison was taken out of its world.","about":"The brown pelican (Pelecanus occidentalis) is a bird of the pelican family, Pelecanidae, one of three species found in the Americas and one of two that feed by diving into water. It is found on the Atlantic Coast from New Jersey to the mouth of the Amazon River, and along the Pacific Coast from British Columbia to Peru, including the Galapagos Islands.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Brown_pelican_in_flight_%28Bodega_Bay%29.jpg/1920px-Brown_pelican_in_flight_%28Bodega_Bay%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Frank Schulenburg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brown_pelican_in_flight_(Bodega_Bay).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown_pelican","sci":"Pelecanus occidentalis","status":"LC","mass":3.702,"length":null,"life":null,"n":195},
{"name":"Arctic Tern","page":"Arctic_tern","group":"Bird","where":"Breeds in the Arctic, then flies to Antarctica for the southern summer","diet":"Fish and small sea animals","facts":["It makes the longest migration of any animal, from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year.","It sees more daylight than any other animal.","It can live for up to about 30 years, and the record is 31."],"lesson":"Follow the light. The Arctic tern chases summer from one end of the world to the other.","about":"The Arctic tern (Sterna paradisaea) is a tern in the family Laridae. This bird has a circumpolar breeding distribution covering the Arctic and subarctic regions of Europe (as far south as Brittany), Asia, and North America (as far south as Massachusetts).","description":"Bird that breeds in the Arctic and subarctic and migrates to the Antarctic","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Arctic_tern_%28Sterna_paradisaea%29_Flatey_3.jpg/1920px-Arctic_tern_%28Sterna_paradisaea%29_Flatey_3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctic_tern_(Sterna_paradisaea)_Flatey_3.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_tern","sci":"Sterna paradisaea","status":"LC","mass":0.114,"length":null,"life":null,"n":196},
{"name":"Black Skimmer","page":"Black_skimmer","group":"Bird","where":"Beaches and coasts of the Americas, including Florida","diet":"Small fish","facts":["Its lower bill is longer than the upper one.","It flies low over the water with its lower bill slicing the surface, snapping shut on fish.","Its eyes have catlike vertical pupils, unique among birds."],"lesson":"Use your unique gift. The skimmer's odd bill is exactly what makes it a great fisher.","about":"The black skimmer (Rynchops niger) is a tern-like seabird, one of three similar bird species in the skimmer genus Rynchops in the gull family Laridae. It breeds in North and South America.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Black_skimmer_Rynchops_niger.jpg/1920px-Black_skimmer_Rynchops_niger.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_skimmer_Rynchops_niger.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_skimmer","sci":"Rynchops niger","status":"LC","mass":0.349,"length":null,"life":null,"n":197},
{"name":"Roseate Spoonbill","page":"Roseate_spoonbill","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts and wetlands of Florida, the Gulf, the Caribbean and South America","diet":"Small fish, shrimp and water insects","facts":["Its pink color comes from what it eats.","It sweeps its spoon-shaped bill side to side through the water to feel for food.","In Florida Bay, scientists watch spoonbills to judge the health of the whole bay."],"lesson":"You are what you take in. The spoonbill's pink comes from the food it eats.","about":"The roseate spoonbill (Platalea ajaja) is a social wading bird of the ibis and spoonbill family, Threskiornithidae. It is a resident breeder in both South and North America. The roseate spoonbill's pink color is diet-derived, consisting of the carotenoid pigment canthaxanthin, like the American flamingo.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Roseate_Spoonbill_-_Myakka_River_State_Park.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"User:Mwanner","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roseate_Spoonbill_-_Myakka_River_State_Park.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roseate_spoonbill","sci":"Platalea ajaja","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":198},
{"name":"Wood Stork","page":"Wood_stork","group":"Bird","where":"Wetlands of the southeastern US (including Florida), the Caribbean and Latin America","diet":"Fish","facts":["It's the only stork that breeds in North America.","Its bare head and neck have dark gray, scaly skin.","Its nicknames include flinthead, ironhead and preacher."],"lesson":"Looks aren't everything. The bald wood stork catches its fish without needing to see them, hunting by feel in shallow water.","about":"The wood stork (Mycteria americana) is a large wading bird in the family Ciconiidae (storks). Originally described in 1758 by Carl Linnaeus, this stork is native to the subtropics and tropics of the Americas, where it persists in habitats with fluctuating water levels. It is the only stork species that breeds in North America.","description":"Wading bird found in the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/WoodStorkWhole.JPG/1920px-WoodStorkWhole.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Googie man (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WoodStorkWhole.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_stork","sci":"Mycteria americana","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":199},
{"name":"Shoebill","page":"Shoebill","group":"Bird","where":"Swamps of East Africa, from South Sudan to Zambia","diet":"Lungfish, catfish and other fish","facts":["Its huge bill looks like a wooden shoe.","It stays still for so long that people call it statue-like.","Its closest relative is the hamerkop, and it's grouped with pelicans and herons, not storks."],"lesson":"Patience pays. The shoebill can stand still for hours, waiting for the right moment.","about":"The shoebill (Balaeniceps rex), also known as the whale-headed stork, whalebill, and shoe-billed stork, is a large long-legged wading bird. It is named for its enormous, shoe-shaped bill.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Balaeniceps_rex_-Ueno_Zoo%2C_Tokyo%2C_Japan-8a.jpg/1920px-Balaeniceps_rex_-Ueno_Zoo%2C_Tokyo%2C_Japan-8a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bob Owen","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balaeniceps_rex_-Ueno_Zoo,_Tokyo,_Japan-8a.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoebill","sci":"Balaeniceps rex","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":200},
{"name":"Great Blue Heron","page":"Great_blue_heron","group":"Bird","where":"Wetlands of North and Central America, the Caribbean and the Galápagos","diet":"Fish, frogs and small animals","facts":["It's the largest heron in North America.","Florida has an all-white form called the great white heron.","It stands still in the water, then strikes with its long bill."],"lesson":"Stillness is a strategy. The great blue heron catches its fish by waiting without moving.","about":"The great blue heron (Ardea herodias) is a large wading bird in the heron family (Ardeidae), common near shores of open water and wetlands over most of North and Central America, as well as Northwestern South America, the Caribbean, and the Galápagos Islands. It is occasionally found in the Azores and is a rare vagrant to Europe.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/GreatBlueHeronInARiver.jpg/1920px-GreatBlueHeronInARiver.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"DallasPenner","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GreatBlueHeronInARiver.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_blue_heron","sci":"Ardea herodias","status":"LC","mass":2.48,"length":null,"life":null,"n":201},
{"name":"Scarlet Ibis","page":"Scarlet_ibis","group":"Bird","where":"Mangroves and wetlands of northern South America and Trinidad","diet":"Crabs, shrimp and other small animals","facts":["It's so close to the American white ibis that some scientists call them one species.","It's one of the two national birds of Trinidad and Tobago.","Young birds are gray, brown and white, and turn red over about two years."],"lesson":"Growth shows over time. Young scarlet ibises start out drab, and get redder year by year.","about":"The scarlet ibis, sometimes called red ibis (Eudocimus ruber), is a species of ibis in the bird family Threskiornithidae. It inhabits tropical South America and part of the Caribbean. In form, it resembles most of the other twenty-seven extant species of ibis, but its remarkably brilliant scarlet coloration makes it unmistakable.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Oceanografic_Scarlet_Ibis_02.jpg/1920px-Oceanografic_Scarlet_Ibis_02.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rafa Esteve","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oceanografic_Scarlet_Ibis_02.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarlet_ibis","sci":"Eudocimus ruber","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":202},
{"name":"Whooping Crane","page":"Whooping_crane","group":"Bird","where":"Breeds in Canada's Wood Buffalo National Park; winters on the Texas coast","diet":"Crabs, clams, frogs and grain","facts":["It's the tallest bird in North America.","In 1941, only about 20 were left in the wild.","Young cranes were taught new migration routes by following ultralight aircraft."],"lesson":"Never give up on a long shot. The whooping crane came back from about 20 birds.","about":"The whooping crane (Grus americana) is an endangered crane species, native to North America, named for its \"whooping\" calls. Along with the sandhill crane (Antigone canadensis), it is one of only two crane species native to North America, and it is also the tallest North American bird species, with an estimated 22–30+ year life expectancy in the wild.","description":"Species of large bird from North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Grus_americana_Sasata.jpg/1920px-Grus_americana_Sasata.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sasata","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grus_americana_Sasata.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whooping_crane","sci":"Grus americana","status":"EN","mass":7.497,"length":null,"life":null,"n":203},
{"name":"Sandhill Crane","page":"Sandhill_crane","group":"Bird","where":"Wetlands and fields across North America, including Florida","diet":"Seeds, grains, insects and small animals","facts":["The Platte River in Nebraska is its most important stop on migration.","It's named for Nebraska's Sandhills, along the Platte River.","Its loud, trumpeting call has a rolling 'r' sound and can be heard from far away."],"lesson":"Gather together. Sandhill cranes rest and refuel by the hundreds of thousands on the Platte River.","about":"The sandhill crane (Antigone canadensis) is a species of large crane of North America and extreme northeastern Siberia. The common name of this bird refers to its habitat, such as the Platte River, on the edge of Nebraska's Sandhills on the American Great Plains. Sandhill cranes are known to frequent the edges of bodies of water.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Greater_sandhill_crane_couple.jpg/1920px-Greater_sandhill_crane_couple.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"HappiestFish","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_sandhill_crane_couple.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandhill_crane","sci":"Grus canadensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":204},
{"name":"Red-crowned Crane","page":"Red-crowned_crane","group":"Bird","where":"Wetlands of East Asia, including Hokkaido, Japan","diet":"Fish, insects, crabs and grain","facts":["In East Asia it's a symbol of luck, long life and faithfulness.","Pairs dance and call together.","It's one of the rarest cranes in the world."],"lesson":"Dance with your partner. Red-crowned crane pairs keep their bond strong by dancing together.","about":"The red-crowned crane (Grus japonensis), also called the Japanese crane and the Manchurian crane, is a large Northeast Asian crane among the rarest cranes in the world. In some parts of its range, it is known as a symbol of luck, longevity, and fidelity.","description":"Species of large bird from East Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Grus_japonensis_-Hokkaido%2C_Japan_-several-8_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Grus_japonensis_-Hokkaido%2C_Japan_-several-8_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Alastair Rae from London, United Kingdom","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grus_japonensis_-Hokkaido,_Japan_-several-8_(1).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red-crowned_crane","sci":"Grus japonensis","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":205},
{"name":"Anhinga","page":"Anhinga","group":"Bird","where":"Freshwater swamps of the southeastern US (including Florida), the Caribbean and Latin America","diet":"Fish","facts":["It spears fish with its sharp, pointed bill.","Its feathers soak up water, so it dries off with its wings spread wide.","It's called the \"snakebird,\" because it swims with only its long neck above water."],"lesson":"Take time to dry off. After every dive, the anhinga rests with its wings open to the sun.","about":"The anhinga (Anhinga anhinga), sometimes called darter, American darter, snakebird, or water turkey, is a water bird of the warmer parts of the Americas. The word anhinga comes from a'ñinga in the Brazilian Tupi language and means \"devil bird\" or \"snake bird\". The origin of the name is apparent when swimming: only the neck appears above water, so the bird looks like a snake ready to strike.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Anhinga_anhinga_-Costa_Rica-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Tim from Ithaca","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anhinga_anhinga_-Costa_Rica-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anhinga","sci":"Anhinga anhinga","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":206},
{"name":"Common Loon","page":"Common_loon","group":"Bird","where":"Lakes of Canada and the northern US; winters on both coasts as far south as Mexico","diet":"Fish","facts":["Its eerie, wailing call is a sound of the northern wilderness.","Its legs are so far back that it can barely walk on land.","It's the state bird of Minnesota and the provincial bird of Ontario."],"lesson":"Play to your strengths. The loon is clumsy on land, and a champion underwater.","about":"The common loon or great northern diver (Gavia immer) is a large member of the loon, or diver, family of birds. Breeding adults have a plumage that includes a broad black head and neck with a greenish, purplish, or bluish sheen, blackish or blackish-grey upperparts, and pure white underparts except some black on the undertail coverts and vent.","description":"Species of freshwater bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Gavia_immer_-Minocqua%2C_Wisconsin%2C_USA_-swimming-8.jpg/1920px-Gavia_immer_-Minocqua%2C_Wisconsin%2C_USA_-swimming-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"John Picken from Chicago, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavia_immer_-Minocqua,_Wisconsin,_USA_-swimming-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_loon","sci":"Gavia immer","status":"LC","mass":5.46,"length":null,"life":null,"n":207},
{"name":"Mute Swan","page":"Mute_swan","group":"Bird","where":"Lakes and rivers of Europe and Asia, and brought to North America","diet":"Water plants","facts":["It's quieter than other swans, but not really mute.","In Britain, the Crown claims ownership of unmarked mute swans on open water.","It's one of the heaviest birds that can fly."],"lesson":"Grace doesn't need to be loud. The mute swan is admired without saying a word.","about":"The mute swan (Cygnus olor) is a species of swan and a member of the family Anatidae within the waterfowl order. It is native to much of Europe and parts of Asia and (as a rare winter visitor) the far north of Africa. It is an introduced species in North America, home to the largest populations outside of its native range, with additional smaller introductions in Australasia and Southern Africa.","description":"Large species of waterfowl","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/CygneVaires.jpg/1920px-CygneVaires.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sanchezn","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CygneVaires.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mute_swan","sci":"Cygnus olor","status":"LC","mass":11.8,"length":1.475,"life":null,"n":208},
{"name":"Mandarin Duck","page":"Mandarin_duck","group":"Bird","where":"Wooded ponds and rivers of East Asia, and wild in Britain","diet":"Seeds, plants, snails and insects","facts":["Males have bright orange \"sail\" feathers on their backs.","In Chinese culture, a pair of mandarin ducks stands for a loving couple.","Its ducklings jump from tree nests to the ground soon after hatching."],"lesson":"Dress up for life. The male mandarin duck is a reminder that it's fine to be colorful.","about":"The mandarin duck (Aix galericulata) is a perching duck species native to East and Northeast Asia. It is sexually dimorphic; the males are elaborately coloured, while the females have more subdued colours. It is a medium-sized duck, at 41–49 cm (16–19 in) long with a 65–75 cm (26–30 in) wingspan.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Pair_of_mandarin_ducks.jpg/1920px-Pair_of_mandarin_ducks.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Francis C. Franklin","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pair_of_mandarin_ducks.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandarin_duck","sci":"Aix galericulata","status":"LC","mass":0.041,"length":null,"life":null,"n":209},
{"name":"Wood Duck","page":"Wood_duck","group":"Bird","where":"Wooded swamps and ponds of North America, including Florida","diet":"Seeds, acorns and insects","facts":["It nests in tree holes, and its ducklings leap to the ground the day after hatching.","Its feet have claws, so it can perch in trees.","It readily nests in nest boxes put up near water."],"lesson":"Take the leap. Wood ducklings jump from their nest hole the day after they hatch.","about":"The wood duck or Carolina duck (Aix sponsa) is a partially migratory species of perching duck found in North America. The male is one of the most colorful North American waterfowls.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Wood_Duck_Wissahickon_Creek.png/1920px-Wood_Duck_Wissahickon_Creek.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler d/b/a FocusOnwWildlife","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wood_Duck_Wissahickon_Creek.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_duck","sci":"Aix sponsa","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":210},
{"name":"King Penguin","page":"King_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Islands of the Southern Ocean, like South Georgia","diet":"Lanternfish and squid","facts":["It's the second-largest penguin.","It doesn't build a nest: it balances its egg on its feet and keeps it warm in a brood pouch.","Its chicks take more than a year to grow up."],"lesson":"Some things take more than a year. King penguin parents raise one chick over more than a year.","about":"The king penguin (Aptenodytes patagonicus) is the second largest species of penguin, smaller than but somewhat similar in appearance to the emperor penguin. King penguins mainly eat lanternfish, squid, and krill. On foraging trips, king penguins repeatedly dive to over 100 metres (300 ft), and have been recorded at depths greater than 300 metres (1,000 ft).","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/SGI-2016-South_Georgia_%28Fortuna_Bay%29%E2%80%93King_penguin_%28Aptenodytes_patagonicus%29_04.jpg/1920px-SGI-2016-South_Georgia_%28Fortuna_Bay%29%E2%80%93King_penguin_%28Aptenodytes_patagonicus%29_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Godot13","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SGI-2016-South_Georgia_(Fortuna_Bay)%E2%80%93King_penguin_(Aptenodytes_patagonicus)_04.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_penguin","sci":"Aptenodytes patagonicus","status":"LC","mass":12.8,"length":null,"life":null,"n":211},
{"name":"Adélie Penguin","page":"Adélie_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts of Antarctica","diet":"Krill and small fish","facts":["It's one of only four penguins that nest on Antarctica itself.","It's named after Adèle, the wife of a French explorer.","It and the emperor penguin live farther south than any other penguins."],"lesson":"Be at home where it's hard. The Adélie penguin raises its chicks on the coast of Antarctica.","about":"The Adélie penguin (Pygoscelis adeliae) is a species of penguin common along the entire coast of the Antarctic continent. It is the most widespread penguin species in the Antarctic, and, along with the emperor penguin, is the most southerly distributed of all penguins.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Hope_Bay-2016-Trinity_Peninsula%E2%80%93Ad%C3%A9lie_penguin_%28Pygoscelis_adeliae%29_04.jpg/1920px-Hope_Bay-2016-Trinity_Peninsula%E2%80%93Ad%C3%A9lie_penguin_%28Pygoscelis_adeliae%29_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Godot13","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hope_Bay-2016-Trinity_Peninsula%E2%80%93Ad%C3%A9lie_penguin_(Pygoscelis_adeliae)_04.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ad%C3%A9lie_penguin","sci":"Pygoscelis adeliae","status":"LC","mass":5.35,"length":null,"life":null,"n":212},
{"name":"African Penguin","page":"African_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts and islands of South Africa and Namibia","diet":"Sardines and anchovies","facts":["Its braying call earned it the nickname \"jackass penguin.\"","Pink patches of bare skin above its eyes help it stay cool.","Boulders Beach near Cape Town has a famous colony."],"lesson":"Keep cool in the heat. The African penguin is built for a warm coast, not the ice.","about":"The African penguin (Spheniscus demersus), also known as the Cape penguin or the South African penguin, is a species of penguin confined to southern African waters. It is the only penguin found in the Old World. Like all penguins, it is flightless, with a streamlined body and wings stiffened and flattened into flippers for a marine habitat.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Wikimania_2018%2C_Cape_Town_%28_1050602%29%2C_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Matti Blume","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wikimania_2018,_Cape_Town_(_1050602),_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_penguin","sci":"Spheniscus demersus","status":"CR","mass":0.103,"length":null,"life":null,"n":213},
{"name":"Little Penguin","page":"Little_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Coasts of southern Australia and New Zealand","diet":"Small fish and squid","facts":["It's the smallest penguin, about 33 cm (13 inches) tall.","Its slate-blue feathers give it the names blue penguin and fairy penguin.","On Phillip Island, crowds watch them waddle ashore every evening."],"lesson":"Come home every night. Little penguins return to their burrows at sunset, rain or shine.","about":"The little penguin (Eudyptula minor) is the smallest species of penguin in the world, and originates from New Zealand and Australia. It is commonly known as the fairy penguin, little blue penguin, or blue penguin, owing to its slate-blue plumage, and is also known by its Māori name kororā. It is a marine neritic species that dives for food throughout the day and returns to burrows on the shore at dusk.","description":"Species of penguin","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Eudyptula_minor_Bruny_1.jpg/1920px-Eudyptula_minor_Bruny_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eudyptula_minor_Bruny_1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_penguin","sci":"Eudyptula minor","status":"LC","mass":1.244,"length":null,"life":null,"n":214},
{"name":"Galápagos Penguin","page":"Galápagos_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"The Galápagos Islands, on the equator","diet":"Small fish","facts":["It's the only penguin that lives on the equator, and some live north of it.","It keeps cool by panting and shading its feet with its flippers.","It's one of the rarest penguins in the world."],"lesson":"Adapt to where you are. The Galápagos penguin made a home in the tropics.","about":"The Galápagos penguin (Spheniscus mendiculus) is a penguin endemic to the Galápagos Islands of Ecuador. It is the only penguin found north of the equator. Most inhabit Fernandina Island and the west coast of Isabela Island.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Gal%C3%A1pagos_penguin_%28Spheniscus_mendiculus%29_male.jpg/1920px-Gal%C3%A1pagos_penguin_%28Spheniscus_mendiculus%29_male.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gal%C3%A1pagos_penguin_(Spheniscus_mendiculus)_male.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galapagos_penguin","sci":"Spheniscus mendiculus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":215},
{"name":"Southern Rockhopper Penguin","page":"Southern_rockhopper_penguin","group":"Bird","where":"Rocky islands around the southern tip of South America, like the Falklands","diet":"Krill, squid and small fish","facts":["It hops from rock to rock instead of sliding.","It has spiky yellow eyebrows.","It lays two eggs but usually raises only one chick."],"lesson":"Jump over obstacles. The rockhopper bounds up rocky shores that other penguins avoid.","about":"The western rockhopper penguin (Eudyptes chrysocome) is a species of rockhopper penguin. It occurs in subantarctic waters around the southern coast of South America and nearby islands.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Rockhopper_Penguin_on_Saunders_Island_%285586446214%29.jpg/1920px-Rockhopper_Penguin_on_Saunders_Island_%285586446214%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Liam Quinn from Canada","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rockhopper_Penguin_on_Saunders_Island_(5586446214).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_rockhopper_penguin","sci":"Eudyptes chrysocome","status":"VU","mass":2.47,"length":null,"life":null,"n":216},
{"name":"Great Egret","page":"Great_egret","group":"Bird","where":"Wetlands almost worldwide, including Florida","diet":"Fish, frogs and small animals","facts":["Around 1900, huge numbers were killed for their plumes, called aigrettes, to decorate hats.","It's the symbol of the National Audubon Society.","It feeds on fish and frogs in shallow water, and on mice, snakes and grasshoppers on land."],"lesson":"Beauty is worth protecting. Great egrets were once hunted for their plumes, and conservation brought their numbers back.","about":"The great egret (Ardea alba), also known as the common egret, large egret, great white egret, or great white heron, is a large, widely distributed egret. The four subspecies occur in Asia, Africa, the Americas, and southern Europe. Recently, it has also been spreading to more northern areas of Europe.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Great_Egret_%28Ardea_alba%29_in_Breeding_Plumage%2C_Cape_May_County%2C_New_Jersey%2C_USA_%28cropped%29.png/1920px-Great_Egret_%28Ardea_alba%29_in_Breeding_Plumage%2C_Cape_May_County%2C_New_Jersey%2C_USA_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chuck Homler (FocusOnWildlife.Me)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Egret_(Ardea_alba)_in_Breeding_Plumage,_Cape_May_County,_New_Jersey,_USA_(cropped).png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_egret","sci":"Ardea alba","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":217},
{"name":"Northern Gannet","page":"Northern_gannet","group":"Bird","where":"The North Atlantic","diet":"Fish like mackerel and herring","facts":["It plunge-dives at high speed from as high as 70 meters (230 feet).","Strong neck muscles and a spongy bone plate at the base of its bill help it survive its high-speed dives.","It's the largest seabird of the North Atlantic."],"lesson":"Commit fully. The gannet dives straight in, built to take the impact.","about":"The northern gannet (Morus bassanus) is a seabird, the largest species of the gannet family, Sulidae. It is native to the coasts of the North Atlantic Ocean, breeding in Western Europe and Northeastern North America. It is the largest seabird in the northern Atlantic.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Morus_bassanus_adu.jpg/1920px-Morus_bassanus_adu.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morus_bassanus_adu.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_gannet","sci":"Morus bassanus","status":"LC","mass":2.999,"length":null,"life":null,"n":218},
{"name":"Kākāpō","page":"Kākāpō","group":"Bird","where":"A few predator-free islands of New Zealand","diet":"Leaves, seeds, fruit and pollen","facts":["It's the heaviest parrot in the world.","It can't fly, and it's active at night.","Every known kākāpō has a name and a tag; there were 325 of them in 2026."],"lesson":"Every one counts. With so few kākāpō left, each bird is known by name.","about":"The kākāpō (pl.: kākāpō; Strigops habroptilus), sometimes known as the owl-parrot, is a species of large, nocturnal, ground-dwelling parrot of the superfamily Strigopoidea. It is endemic to New Zealand. Kākāpō can be up to 64 cm (25 in) long.","description":"Parrot endemic to New Zealand","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Sirocco_full_length_portrait.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Department of Conservation","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sirocco_full_length_portrait.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C4%81k%C4%81p%C5%8D","sci":"Strigops habroptila","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":59.0,"n":219},
{"name":"Kea","page":"Kea","group":"Bird","where":"Mountains of New Zealand's South Island","diet":"Roots, berries, insects and carrion","facts":["It lives from lowland river valleys all the way up to alpine peaks like Aoraki / Mount Cook.","It's famously curious and pulls apart car wipers and rubber seals.","It can solve logic puzzles, like pushing and pulling things in the right order to reach food."],"lesson":"Stay curious. The kea's nosiness is how it figures out its harsh mountain world.","about":"The kea (Nestor notabilis) is a species of large parrot in the family Strigopidae that is endemic to the forested and alpine regions of the South Island of New Zealand. About 48 cm (19 in) long, it is mostly olive-green, with brilliant orange under its wings, and has a large, narrow, curved, grey-brown upper beak.","description":"Species of bird endemic to New Zealand","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Nestor_notabilis_-Fiordland%2C_New_Zealand-8b.jpg/1920px-Nestor_notabilis_-Fiordland%2C_New_Zealand-8b.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Mark Whatmough","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nestor_notabilis_-Fiordland,_New_Zealand-8b.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kea","sci":"Nestor notabilis","status":"EN","mass":0.956,"length":null,"life":null,"n":220},
{"name":"Scarlet Macaw","page":"Scarlet_macaw","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests from southern Mexico to the Amazon","diet":"Seeds, nuts, fruit and flowers","facts":["It usually lives 40 to 50 years, and up to 75 or more in captivity.","It lives in humid lowland forests, up to about 1,000 meters (3,300 feet).","It's the national bird of Honduras."],"lesson":"Wear your colors loud. The scarlet macaw is impossible to miss, and proud of it.","about":"The scarlet macaw (Ara macao) also called the red-and-yellow macaw, red-and-blue macaw or red-breasted macaw, is a large yellow, green, red and blue Neotropical parrot native to humid evergreen forests of the Americas.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Scarlet_macaw_%28Ara_macao_cyanopterus%29_Copan.jpg/1920px-Scarlet_macaw_%28Ara_macao_cyanopterus%29_Copan.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scarlet_macaw_(Ara_macao_cyanopterus)_Copan.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarlet_macaw","sci":"Ara macao","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":221},
{"name":"Hyacinth Macaw","page":"Hyacinth_macaw","group":"Bird","where":"Palm swamps and woodlands of central Brazil, Bolivia and Paraguay","diet":"Palm nuts","facts":["At about a meter (3 feet) long, it's the longest parrot in the world.","Its strong beak can crack coconuts and Brazil nut pods.","Most of what it eats is the nuts of a few kinds of palm."],"lesson":"Have the right tool. The hyacinth macaw's strong beak can even crack coconuts.","about":"The hyacinth macaw (Anodorhynchus hyacinthinus), or hyacinthine macaw, is a large parrot native to central and eastern South America. With a length (from the top of its head to the tip of its long pointed tail) of about one meter it is longer than any other species of parrot. It is the largest macaw and the largest flying parrot species.","description":"Species of bird (parrot)","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/008_Hyacinth_macaw_couple_kissing_under_Pink_Ip%C3%AA_tree_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-008_Hyacinth_macaw_couple_kissing_under_Pink_Ip%C3%AA_tree_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:008_Hyacinth_macaw_couple_kissing_under_Pink_Ip%C3%AA_tree_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth_macaw","sci":"Anodorhynchus hyacinthinus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":222},
{"name":"Grey Parrot","page":"Grey_parrot","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests of West and Central Africa","diet":"Fruit, nuts and seeds, plus some flowers, bark and insects","facts":["It's prized for copying human speech, which makes it one of the most popular pet birds.","The ancient Greeks and Romans kept them in cages.","Catching them for the pet trade has made it endangered."],"lesson":"Use your voice. The grey parrot's gift for speech has charmed people since Roman times.","about":"The grey parrot (Psittacus erithacus), also known as the Congo grey parrot, is an African parrot in the family Psittacidae. Psittacus erithacus was previously also known as the African grey parrot until the Timneh parrot (Psittacus timneh), previously treated as a subspecies, was elevated to a full species.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Perroquet_%C3%A0_Yampopo_Beach_-_Douala.jpg/1920px-Perroquet_%C3%A0_Yampopo_Beach_-_Douala.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Florettesokeng","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perroquet_%C3%A0_Yampopo_Beach_-_Douala.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_parrot","sci":"Psittacus erithacus","status":"EN","mass":0.019,"length":null,"life":45.0,"n":223},
{"name":"Sulphur-crested Cockatoo","page":"Sulphur-crested_cockatoo","group":"Bird","where":"Forests and towns of eastern and northern Australia and New Guinea","diet":"Seeds, grain and fruit, including farm crops, and even scraps from bins","facts":["In Sydney, cockatoos learned to flip open garbage bin lids, and the trick spread.","It can live for many decades.","It raises its yellow crest when it's excited."],"lesson":"Learn from your neighbors. Sydney's cockatoos spread their bin-opening trick from suburb to suburb.","about":"The sulphur-crested cockatoo (Cacatua galerita) is a relatively large white cockatoo found in wooded habitats in Australia, New Guinea, and some of the islands of Indonesia. They can be locally very numerous, leading to them sometimes being considered pests. As highly intelligent birds, they are well known in aviculture, although they can be demanding pets.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Cacatua_galerita_Tas_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacatua_galerita_Tas_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulphur-crested_cockatoo","sci":"Cacatua galerita","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":0.55,"life":null,"n":224},
{"name":"Budgerigar","page":"Budgerigar","group":"Bird","where":"Dry grasslands and scrub of inland Australia","diet":"Grass seeds","facts":["It's one of the most popular pet birds in the world.","Wild budgies are nomads, wandering in flocks.","Adults can be told apart, male or female, by the color of the bare patch above the beak, the cere."],"lesson":"Go with the flow. Wild budgies wander the outback in big flocks, following the rain.","about":"The budgerigar (Melopsittacus undulatus), also known as the common parakeet, shell parakeet or budgie (BUJ-ee), is a small, long-tailed, seed-eating parrot native to Australia. Naturally, the species is green and yellow with black, scalloped markings on the nape, back, and wings. Budgies are bred in captivity with colouring of blues, whites, yellows, greys, and even with small crests.","description":"Small, long-tailed, seed-eating parakeet","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Budgerigar-male-strzelecki-qld.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Benjamint444","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Budgerigar-male-strzelecki-qld.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budgerigar","sci":"Melopsittacus undulatus","status":"LC","mass":0.002,"length":null,"life":null,"n":225},
{"name":"Toco Toucan","page":"Toco_toucan","group":"Bird","where":"Savannas and woodlands of central and eastern South America","diet":"Fruit, insects, eggs and small animals","facts":["It's the largest toucan.","Its bill looks heavy, but it's light, because it's mostly hollow inside.","Unlike other toucans, it lives in open country like savannas, not deep forest."],"lesson":"Big doesn't have to mean heavy. The toucan's giant bill is mostly air.","about":"The toco toucan (Ramphastos toco) is a species of bird in the toucan family Ramphastidae. It is the largest species of toucan and has a distinctive appearance, with a black body, a white throat, chest and uppertail-coverts, and red undertail-coverts. Its most conspicuous feature is its huge beak, which is yellow-orange with a black base and a large spot on the tip.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/006_Toco_toucan_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-006_Toco_toucan_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Giles Laurent","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:006_Toco_toucan_in_Encontro_das_%C3%81guas_State_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toco_toucan","sci":"Ramphastos toco","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":226},
{"name":"Great Hornbill","page":"Great_hornbill","group":"Bird","where":"Forests of India and Southeast Asia","diet":"Fruit, especially figs, and small animals","facts":["The female seals herself inside a tree hole to nest, and the male feeds her through a slit.","It has a big yellow casque on top of its bill.","It's the state bird of Kerala and Arunachal Pradesh in India."],"lesson":"Trust your partner. The female hornbill depends on the male to bring every meal while she's walled in.","about":"The great hornbill (Buceros bicornis), also known as the concave-casqued hornbill, great Indian hornbill or great pied hornbill, is one of the larger members of the hornbill family. It occurs in the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia. It is predominantly frugivorous, but also preys on small mammals, reptiles, and birds.","description":"Bird species","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Great_hornbill_Photograph_by_Shantanu_Kuveskar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Shantanu Kuveskar","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_hornbill_Photograph_by_Shantanu_Kuveskar.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_hornbill","sci":"Buceros bicornis","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":227},
{"name":"Laughing Kookaburra","page":"Laughing_kookaburra","group":"Bird","where":"Woodlands and suburbs of eastern Australia","diet":"Insects, lizards, snakes and mice","facts":["Its call sounds like loud, echoing laughter.","It's a big kingfisher, but it eats lizards, snakes and mice more than fish.","Its call is used in films to sound like a jungle."],"lesson":"Laugh often. The kookaburra's chorus starts and ends the day in the Australian bush.","about":"The laughing kookaburra (Dacelo novaeguineae) is a bird in the kingfisher subfamily Halcyoninae. It is a large kingfisher with a whitish head and a brown eye-stripe. The upperparts are mostly dark brown but there is a mottled light-blue patch on the wing coverts.","description":"Species of kingfisher bird in Australia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Laughing_Kookaburra_1.jpg/1920px-Laughing_Kookaburra_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Photo by: Paulweberphoto - paulweberphoto.com","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laughing_Kookaburra_1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laughing_kookaburra","sci":"Dacelo novaeguineae","status":"LC","mass":0.352,"length":null,"life":null,"n":228},
{"name":"Common Kingfisher","page":"Common_kingfisher","group":"Bird","where":"Rivers and streams of Europe, Asia and North Africa","diet":"Small fish","facts":["If a rival enters its territory, they may fight, each trying to hold the other's beak underwater.","It dives from a perch to catch fish.","Its eyes have red oil droplets that may help cut the glare from the water."],"lesson":"Precision beats power. The kingfisher hits the water exactly where the fish is.","about":"The common kingfisher (Alcedo atthis), also known as the Eurasian kingfisher and river kingfisher, is a small kingfisher with seven subspecies recognized within its wide distribution across Eurasia and North Africa. It is resident in much of its range, but migrates from areas where rivers freeze in winter.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Alcedo_atthis_-England-8_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Tony Wood","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcedo_atthis_-England-8_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_kingfisher","sci":"Alcedo atthis","status":"LC","mass":0.004,"length":null,"life":null,"n":229},
{"name":"Wilson's Bird-of-paradise","page":"Wilson's_bird-of-paradise","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests of Waigeo and Batanta islands, Indonesia","diet":"Fruit and insects","facts":["The male has a bare, bright blue crown.","Males clear a patch of forest floor to dance on.","Its two violet tail feathers curl into spirals."],"lesson":"Prepare your stage. The male clears a spotless dance floor before he performs.","about":"Wilson's bird-of-paradise (Diphyllodes respublica) is a species of passerine bird of the family Paradisaeidae. The first footage of the Wilson's bird-of-paradise ever to be filmed was recorded in 1996 by David Attenborough for the BBC documentary Attenborough in Paradise. He did so by dropping leaves on the forest floor, which irritated the bird into clearing them away.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Wilson%27s_Bird-of-Paradise_0A2A1913.jpg/1920px-Wilson%27s_Bird-of-Paradise_0A2A1913.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilson%27s_Bird-of-Paradise_0A2A1913.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilson%27s_bird-of-paradise","sci":"Diphyllodes respublica","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":230},
{"name":"Greater Bird-of-paradise","page":"Greater_bird-of-paradise","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests of southwestern New Guinea and the Aru Islands","diet":"Fruit and insects","facts":["It's said to have \"the most glamorous display in the bird world,\" with clouds of yellow plumes.","Early skins reached Europe without feet, so people thought these birds never landed.","Its scientific name, apoda, means \"footless.\""],"lesson":"Don't believe everything you hear. Europeans once thought birds-of-paradise had no feet.","about":"The greater bird-of-paradise (Paradisaea apoda) is a bird-of-paradise in the genus Paradisaea.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Paradisaea_apoda_-Bali_Bird_Park-5.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Andrea Lawardi","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paradisaea_apoda_-Bali_Bird_Park-5.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_bird-of-paradise","sci":"Paradisaea apoda","status":"LC","mass":0.25,"length":null,"life":null,"n":231},
{"name":"Satin Bowerbird","page":"Satin_bowerbird","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests and woodlands of eastern Australia","diet":"Fruit, leaves and insects","facts":["Males build a bower of sticks and decorate it with blue objects.","Males take seven or eight years to turn glossy blue-black.","As males mature, they get better at picking blue objects for the bower."],"lesson":"Put effort into what you make. The bowerbird's carefully decorated bower is how it wins a mate.","about":"The satin bowerbird (Ptilonorhynchus violaceus) is a species of passerine bird in the bowerbird family Ptilonorhynchidae that is endemic to eastern Australia. It is the only species placed in the genus Ptilonorhynchus.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Satin_Bowerbird_at_Bower_6533.jpg/1920px-Satin_Bowerbird_at_Bower_6533.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satin_Bowerbird_at_Bower_6533.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satin_bowerbird","sci":"Ptilonorhynchus violaceus","status":"LC","mass":0.173,"length":null,"life":null,"n":232},
{"name":"Superb Lyrebird","page":"Superb_lyrebird","group":"Bird","where":"Forests of southeastern Australia","diet":"Earthworms and insects it scratches up from the forest floor","facts":["It mimics other birds and even sounds like camera shutters and chainsaws.","The male's tail feathers spread into the shape of a lyre.","It's on Australia's 10-cent coin."],"lesson":"Listen and learn. Most of the male lyrebird's song is copied from the calls of other birds.","about":"The superb lyrebird (Menura novaehollandiae) is an Australian passerine songbird, one of two species from the family Menuridae, with the other being the much rarer Albert's lyrebird. One of the world's largest songbirds, the superb lyrebird is renowned for its elaborate tail and courtship displays, and for its excellent mimicry.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Superb_lyrbird_in_scrub.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Fir0002","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Superb_lyrbird_in_scrub.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superb_lyrebird","sci":"Menura novaehollandiae","status":"LC","mass":1.1,"length":null,"life":null,"n":233},
{"name":"Northern Cardinal","page":"Northern_cardinal","group":"Bird","where":"Eastern and central US (including Florida), Mexico and Central America","diet":"Seeds, fruit and insects","facts":["It's the state bird of seven US states.","Females sing too: both sexes sing clear, whistled songs.","During courtship, the male feeds the female beak to beak."],"lesson":"Show up in every season. The cardinal stays all winter and brightens the snow.","about":"The northern cardinal (Cardinalis cardinalis), also commonly known as the common cardinal, red cardinal, or simply cardinal, is a bird in the genus Cardinalis. It can be found in southeastern Canada, through the eastern United States from Maine to Minnesota to Texas, New Mexico, southern Arizona, southern California and south through Mexico, Belize, and Guatemala.","description":"Species of North American bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Male_northern_cardinal_in_Central_Park_%2852612%29.jpg/1920px-Male_northern_cardinal_in_Central_Park_%2852612%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_northern_cardinal_in_Central_Park_(52612).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_cardinal","sci":"Cardinalis cardinalis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":0.216,"life":null,"n":234},
{"name":"Northern Mockingbird","page":"Northern_mockingbird","group":"Bird","where":"All across the US, including Florida, and Mexico","diet":"Insects and fruit","facts":["It's the state bird of Florida.","It can learn up to about 200 songs.","Unmated males sing more often, and from higher perches, than mated males."],"lesson":"Keep learning new songs. The mockingbird copies the calls of other birds and even electronic noises.","about":"The northern mockingbird (Mimus polyglottos) is a mockingbird commonly found in North America, of the family Mimidae. The species is also found in some parts of the Caribbean, as well as on the Hawaiian Islands. It is typically a permanent resident across much of its range, but northern mockingbirds may move farther south during inclement weather or prior to the onset of winter.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Mockingbird_in_Bay_Ridge_%2885082%29.jpg/1920px-Mockingbird_in_Bay_Ridge_%2885082%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mockingbird_in_Bay_Ridge_(85082).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_mockingbird","sci":"Mimus polyglottos","status":"LC","mass":0.048,"length":null,"life":null,"n":235},
{"name":"Florida Scrub Jay","page":"Florida_scrub_jay","group":"Bird","where":"Only the scrublands of Florida","diet":"Acorns, insects and small animals","facts":["It's the only bird found in Florida and nowhere else.","Its home, Florida scrub, is dry, with poor soil and frequent wildfires.","It buries thousands of acorns each fall, just under the surface."],"lesson":"Plan ahead. The scrub jay buries thousands of acorns in fall to eat through winter and spring.","about":"The Florida scrub jay (Aphelocoma coerulescens) is one of the species of scrub jay native to North America. It is the only species of bird endemic to the U.S. state of Florida and one of only 15 species endemic to the continental United States. Because of this, it is keenly sought by birders.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Adult_Florida_scrub_jay.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"VvAndromedavV","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adult_Florida_scrub_jay.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florida_scrub_jay","sci":"Aphelocoma coerulescens","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":236},
{"name":"Common Raven","page":"Common_raven","group":"Bird","where":"Mountains, deserts, forests and cities across the Northern Hemisphere","diet":"Almost anything: carrion, insects, grain, eggs and scraps","facts":["It's one of the smartest birds.","It can mimic other sounds, even human speech.","Ravens kept at the Tower of London have lived more than 40 years."],"lesson":"Be resourceful. The raven thrives almost anywhere by using its wits.","about":"The common raven or northern raven (Corvus corax) is a large all-black passerine bird. It is the most widely distributed of all corvids, found across the Northern Hemisphere. There are 11 accepted subspecies with little variation in appearance, although recent research has demonstrated significant genetic differences among populations from various regions.","description":"Large, black species of passerine bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Corvus_corax_clarionensis%2C_Point_Reyes_National_Seashore.jpg/1920px-Corvus_corax_clarionensis%2C_Point_Reyes_National_Seashore.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Frank Schulenburg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corvus_corax_clarionensis,_Point_Reyes_National_Seashore.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_raven","sci":"Corvus corax","status":"LC","mass":1.625,"length":0.69,"life":17.167,"n":237},
{"name":"Blue Jay","page":"Blue_jay","group":"Bird","where":"Forests and suburbs of eastern North America, including Florida","diet":"Acorns, seeds and insects","facts":["Its blue isn't a pigment: it's light scattering off its feathers.","It can mimic the calls of hawks.","It hides acorns and seeds to eat later."],"lesson":"Plant for the future. The blue jay hides acorns today so it has food to eat later.","about":"The blue jay (Cyanocitta cristata) is a passerine bird in the family Corvidae, native to eastern North America. It lives in most of the eastern and central United States; some eastern populations may be migratory. Resident populations are also in Newfoundland, Canada; breeding populations are found across southern Canada.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Blue_jay_in_PP_%2830960%29.jpg/1920px-Blue_jay_in_PP_%2830960%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rhododendrites","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_jay_in_PP_(30960).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_jay","sci":"Cyanocitta cristata","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":238},
{"name":"European Robin","page":"European_robin","group":"Bird","where":"Gardens and woods of Europe, western Asia and North Africa","diet":"Insects, worms and berries","facts":["It follows gardeners to catch worms they turn up.","In brightly lit cities, it may sing at night.","It has starred on Christmas cards since the 1800s."],"lesson":"Stay close to good company. The robin follows the gardener, and both are better for it.","about":"The European robin (Erithacus rubecula), known simply as the robin or robin redbreast in the British Isles, is a small insectivorous passerine bird belonging to the Old World flycatcher family Muscicapidae. It is found across Europe, as far east as Western Siberia, and as far south as North Africa.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Erithacus_rubecula_with_cocked_head.jpg/1920px-Erithacus_rubecula_with_cocked_head.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Francis C. Franklin","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erithacus_rubecula_with_cocked_head.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_robin","sci":"Erithacus rubecula","status":"LC","mass":0.002,"length":null,"life":null,"n":239},
{"name":"Painted Bunting","page":"Painted_bunting","group":"Bird","where":"Southern US (wintering in South Florida), Mexico and Central America","diet":"Seeds and insects","facts":["The male is blue, green and red, and its nickname, nonpareil, means \"without equal.\"","Young males look green, like females, until their second year.","Some spend the winter in South Florida."],"lesson":"Your colors will come. The male painted bunting takes a year to show its full brightness.","about":"The painted bunting (Passerina ciris) is a species of bird in the cardinal family, Cardinalidae. It is native to North America. The bright plumage of the male develops beginning in the second year of life; in the first year, they can be distinguished from the female only by close inspection.","description":"Species of bird native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Male_Painted_Bunting_Singing.jpg/1920px-Male_Painted_Bunting_Singing.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Dakota L.","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_Painted_Bunting_Singing.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Painted_bunting","sci":"Passerina ciris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":240},
{"name":"Superb Fairywren","page":"Superb_fairywren","group":"Bird","where":"Gardens and woodlands of southeastern Australia","diet":"Insects","facts":["Males turn brilliant blue in the breeding season.","It uses a special call as a \"password\" for its chicks, which helps it avoid raising cuckoos.","Groups of 3 to 5 share a territory, with helper birds that hatched there."],"lesson":"Teach early. The fairywren gives its chicks a secret \"password\" call that helps keep cuckoos out.","about":"The superb fairywren (Malurus cyaneus) is a passerine bird in the Australasian wren family, Maluridae, and is common and familiar across south-eastern Australia. It is a sedentary and territorial species, also exhibiting a high degree of sexual dimorphism; the male in breeding plumage has a striking bright blue forehead, ear coverts, mantle, and tail, with a black mask and black or dark blue throat.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Malurus_cyaneus_cyanochlamys_.jpg/1920px-Malurus_cyaneus_cyanochlamys_.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malurus_cyaneus_cyanochlamys_.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superb_fairywren","sci":"Malurus cyaneus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":241},
{"name":"Bee Hummingbird","page":"Bee_hummingbird","group":"Bird","where":"Only Cuba","diet":"Nectar and tiny insects","facts":["It's the smallest bird in the world, about 2 grams.","Each of its eggs is about the size of a coffee bean.","It beats its wings 80 to 200 times a second."],"lesson":"Small can be mighty. The world's tiniest bird lives at full speed.","about":"The bee hummingbird, zunzuncito or Helena hummingbird (Mellisuga helenae) is a species of hummingbird, native to the island of Cuba in the Caribbean. It is the smallest known bird. The bee hummingbird feeds on flower nectar and insects.","description":"Smallest species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Bee_hummingbird_%28Mellisuga_helenae%29_adult_male_in_flight-cropped.jpg/1920px-Bee_hummingbird_%28Mellisuga_helenae%29_adult_male_in_flight-cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bee_hummingbird_(Mellisuga_helenae)_adult_male_in_flight-cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee_hummingbird","sci":"Mellisuga helenae","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":242},
{"name":"Sword-billed Hummingbird","page":"Sword-billed_hummingbird","group":"Bird","where":"Cloud forests of the Andes, from Venezuela to Bolivia","diet":"Nectar from long-tubed flowers","facts":["It's the only bird with a bill longer than its body, not counting the tail.","It grooms itself with its feet, since its bill is too long.","It perches with its bill pointed up to balance it."],"lesson":"Your odd trait is your strength. Only the sword-billed hummingbird can reach the deepest flowers.","about":"The sword-billed hummingbird (Ensifera ensifera), also known as the swordbill, is a neotropical species of hummingbird from the Andean regions of South America. It is the only member in the genus Ensifera. Among the largest species of hummingbird, it is characterized by its unusually long bill, being the only bird to have a beak longer than the rest of its body, excluding the tail.","description":"Species of bird from South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Sword-billed_hummingbird_%28male%29_at_Guango_Lodge%2C_Ecuador_%2821310837273%29.jpg/1920px-Sword-billed_hummingbird_%28male%29_at_Guango_Lodge%2C_Ecuador_%2821310837273%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andy Morffew from Itchen Abbas, Hampshire, UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sword-billed_hummingbird_(male)_at_Guango_Lodge,_Ecuador_(21310837273).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sword-billed_hummingbird","sci":"Ensifera ensifera","status":"LC","mass":0.001,"length":null,"life":null,"n":243},
{"name":"Common Swift","page":"Common_swift","group":"Bird","where":"Summers in Europe and Asia, winters in Africa","diet":"Flying insects","facts":["It can stay in the air for ten months without landing.","It eats, drinks and even sleeps on the wing.","It never lands on the ground by choice."],"lesson":"Find your element. The swift lives nearly its whole life in the sky.","about":"The common swift (Apus apus) is a medium-sized bird, superficially similar to the barn swallow or house martin but somewhat larger, though not stemming from those passerine species, being in the order Apodiformes. The resemblances between the groups are due to convergent evolution, reflecting similar contextual development.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Common_Swift_2025_07_18_02_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Common_Swift_2025_07_18_02_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Alexis Lours","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_Swift_2025_07_18_02_(cropped).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_swift","sci":"Apus apus","status":"LC","mass":0.004,"length":null,"life":null,"n":244},
{"name":"Indian Peafowl","page":"Indian_peafowl","group":"Bird","where":"Forests and farmland of India and Sri Lanka","diet":"Seeds, insects and small animals","facts":["It's the national bird of India.","The male's long train isn't his tail: it's made of long feathers that grow above the tail, with colorful eyespots.","Scientists still argue over whether peahens pick their mates by the male's train."],"lesson":"Show your best self. The peacock's train is his way of saying who he is.","about":"The Indian peafowl (Pavo cristatus), also known as the common peafowl, or blue peafowl, is a species of peafowl native to the Indian subcontinent. While it originated in the Indian subcontinent, it has since been introduced to many other parts of the world. Male peafowl are referred to as peacocks, and female peafowl are referred to as peahens, although both sexes are often referred to colloquially as a \"peacock\".","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Peacock_on_tree_%2852077240794%29.jpg/1920px-Peacock_on_tree_%2852077240794%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Kandukuru Nagarjun from Bangalore, India","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peacock_on_tree_(52077240794).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_peafowl","sci":"Pavo cristatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":245},
{"name":"Wild Turkey","page":"Wild_turkey","group":"Bird","where":"Forests and fields of North America, including Florida","diet":"Acorns, seeds, berries and insects","facts":["It's among the heaviest flying birds in the world.","It usually flies low, for no more than about 400 meters (a quarter mile).","It sees poorly at night, so at dusk it flies up to roost in trees."],"lesson":"Don't underestimate the ordinary. Despite its weight, the wild turkey is an agile, fast flier.","about":"The wild turkey (Meleagris gallopavo) is an upland game bird native to North America, one of two extant species of turkey and the heaviest member of the order Galliformes. It is the ancestor to the domestic turkey (M. g. domesticus), which was originally derived from a southern Mexican subspecies of wild turkey (not the related ocellated turkey).","description":"Species of turkey native to North America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/20260428_tom_wild_turkey_matthaei_botanical_gardens_PD08952.jpg/1920px-20260428_tom_wild_turkey_matthaei_botanical_gardens_PD08952.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Paul Danese","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20260428_tom_wild_turkey_matthaei_botanical_gardens_PD08952.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_turkey","sci":"Meleagris gallopavo","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":246},
{"name":"Greater Sage-Grouse","page":"Greater_sage-grouse","group":"Bird","where":"Sagebrush plains of the western US and Canada","diet":"Sagebrush leaves, plus insects and flowers in summer","facts":["Males puff up two yellowish air sacs on the neck to court females.","In Idaho, some move 80 to 160 km (50 to 100 miles) in winter to escape deep snow.","It's the largest grouse in North America."],"lesson":"Protect your home ground. The sage-grouse can't live without the sagebrush sea.","about":"The greater sage-grouse (Centrocercus urophasianus), also known as the sagehen, is the largest grouse in North America. Its range is sagebrush country in the western United States and southern Alberta and Saskatchewan, Canada. It was known as simply the sage grouse until the Gunnison sage-grouse was recognized as a separate species in 2000.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Centrocercus_urophasianus_-USA_-male-8.jpg/1920px-Centrocercus_urophasianus_-USA_-male-8.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Pacific Southwest Region U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service from Sacramento, US","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centrocercus_urophasianus_-USA_-male-8.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_sage-grouse","sci":"Centrocercus urophasianus","status":"NT","mass":3.2,"length":null,"life":null,"n":247},
{"name":"Golden Pheasant","page":"Golden_pheasant","group":"Bird","where":"Mountain forests of western China","diet":"Seeds, leaves and insects","facts":["The male has a golden crest and a bright red body.","He fans out an orange cape around his face to display.","Wild flocks brought by people also live in Britain."],"lesson":"Shine, but know when to hide. The golden pheasant is dazzling, and still slips away into the forest.","about":"The golden pheasant (Chrysolophus pictus), also known as the Chinese pheasant, and rainbow pheasant, is a gamebird of the order Galliformes (gallinaceous birds) and the family Phasianidae (pheasants). The genus name is from Ancient Greek khrusolophos, \"with golden crest\", and pictus is Latin for \"painted\" from pingere, \"to paint\".","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_male_Golden_Pheasant_%28Chrysolophus_pictus%29_showing_its_colorful_plumage.jpg/1920px-A_male_Golden_Pheasant_%28Chrysolophus_pictus%29_showing_its_colorful_plumage.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bhullargraphic","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_male_Golden_Pheasant_(Chrysolophus_pictus)_showing_its_colorful_plumage.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_pheasant","sci":"Chrysolophus pictus","status":"LC","mass":0.725,"length":null,"life":null,"n":248},
{"name":"Southern Cassowary","page":"Southern_cassowary","group":"Bird","where":"Rainforests of New Guinea and northeastern Australia","diet":"Fallen fruit","facts":["Its inner toe has a dagger-like claw up to 12 cm (4.7 inches) long.","The male sits on the eggs and raises the chicks.","It can safely eat some fruits that are poisonous to other animals."],"lesson":"Fathers matter. The male cassowary raises the chicks alone.","about":"The southern cassowary (Casuarius casuarius), also known as double-wattled cassowary, Australian cassowary, or two-wattled cassowary, is a large, flightless, mostly black bird, found in Indonesia, Papua New Guinea, and northeastern Australia. It is one of the three living species of cassowary, alongside the dwarf cassowary and the northern cassowary.","description":"Species of flightless bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Southern_Cassowary_7071.jpg/1920px-Southern_Cassowary_7071.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Summerdrought","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Cassowary_7071.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_cassowary","sci":"Casuarius casuarius","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":249},
{"name":"Emu","page":"Emu","group":"Bird","where":"Most of mainland Australia","diet":"Plants and insects","facts":["It's the second-tallest bird in the world.","It can run at about 50 km/h (30 mph).","In 1932, Australian soldiers lost the \"Emu War\" against the birds."],"lesson":"Persistence wins. The emus outlasted an army in 1932.","about":"The emu (Dromaius novaehollandiae) is a species of flightless bird endemic to Australia, where it is the tallest native bird. It is the only extant member of the genus Dromaius and the third-tallest living bird after its African ratite relatives, the common ostrich and Somali ostrich. The emu's native ranges cover most of the Australian mainland.","description":"Large flightless bird endemic to Australia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Emu_1_-_Tidbinbilla.jpg/1920px-Emu_1_-_Tidbinbilla.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"JJ Harrison (https://www.jjharrison.com.au/)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emu_1_-_Tidbinbilla.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emu","sci":"Dromaius novaehollandiae","status":"LC","mass":38.3,"length":null,"life":null,"n":250},
{"name":"North Island Brown Kiwi","page":"North_Island_brown_kiwi","group":"Bird","where":"Forests of New Zealand's North Island","diet":"Worms, insects and fallen fruit","facts":["Its nostrils are at the tip of its long bill.","Its eggs are among the largest of any bird for its body size.","It's active at night."],"lesson":"Use your senses. The kiwi sniffs out worms with the nostrils at the end of its bill.","about":"The North Island brown kiwi (Apteryx mantelli) is a nocturnal flightless bird and species of kiwi that is widespread in the northern two-thirds of the North Island of New Zealand and, with about 35,000 remaining, it is the most common kiwi species. The eggs laid by the North Island brown kiwi are among the largest eggs relative to its body size.","description":"Species of flightless bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/TeTuatahianui.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Maungatautari Ecological Island Trust","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TeTuatahianui.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Island_brown_kiwi","sci":"Apteryx mantelli","status":"VU","mass":2.955,"length":null,"life":null,"n":251},
{"name":"Greater Rhea","page":"Greater_rhea","group":"Bird","where":"Grasslands of eastern South America","diet":"Plants, seeds and insects","facts":["It's the largest bird in the Americas.","The male builds the nest and sits on eggs laid by several females.","It stands about 1.5 meters (5 feet) tall."],"lesson":"Share the work. The male rhea takes on the eggs of a whole group.","about":"The greater rhea (Rhea americana) is a species of flightless bird native to eastern South America. Other names for the greater rhea include the grey, common, or American rhea; ema (Portuguese) or ñandú (Guaraní and Spanish). One of two species in the genus Rhea, in the family Rheidae, it inhabits a variety of open areas, such as grasslands, savanna or grassy wetlands.","description":"Species of flightless South American bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Nandu_Rhea_americana_Tierpark_Hellabrunn-1.jpg/1920px-Nandu_Rhea_americana_Tierpark_Hellabrunn-1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rufus46","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nandu_Rhea_americana_Tierpark_Hellabrunn-1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_rhea","sci":"Rhea americana","status":"NT","mass":0.6,"length":null,"life":null,"n":252},
{"name":"Greater Roadrunner","page":"Greater_roadrunner","group":"Bird","where":"Deserts and scrub of the southwestern US and Mexico","diet":"Lizards, snakes, insects and mice","facts":["It can run about 32 km/h (20 mph).","It's also called the snake killer.","It's the state bird of New Mexico."],"lesson":"Keep moving. The roadrunner prefers running to flying, and it's very good at it.","about":"The greater roadrunner (Geococcyx californianus) is a long-legged bird in the cuckoo family, Cuculidae, from the Aridoamerica region in the Southwestern United States and Mexico. The scientific name means \"Californian earth-cuckoo\". Along with the lesser roadrunner, it is one of two species in the genus Geococcyx.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Greater_Roadrunner_Tingley_Beach.jpg/1920px-Greater_Roadrunner_Tingley_Beach.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Polinova","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_Roadrunner_Tingley_Beach.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_roadrunner","sci":"Geococcyx californianus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":253},
{"name":"Pileated Woodpecker","page":"Pileated_woodpecker","group":"Bird","where":"Forests of eastern North America (including Florida), Canada and the Pacific Northwest","diet":"Carpenter ants and beetle larvae","facts":["It's one of the largest woodpeckers in North America.","It chisels big rectangular holes in trees.","Its old holes become homes for owls, tree-nesting ducks and even raccoons."],"lesson":"Your work helps others. The pileated woodpecker's holes shelter many other animals.","about":"The pileated woodpecker (Dryocopus pileatus) is a large, crow-sized woodpecker with a prominent red crest, white neck stripe, and a mostly black body. This woodpecker is native to North America, where it is the largest confirmed extant woodpecker species and the third-largest extant species of woodpecker in the world, after the great slaty woodpecker and the black woodpecker.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/PileatedWoodpeckerFeedingonTree%2C_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Joshlaymon","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PileatedWoodpeckerFeedingonTree,_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pileated_woodpecker","sci":"Dryocopus pileatus","status":null,"mass":0.011,"length":null,"life":null,"n":254},
{"name":"Acorn Woodpecker","page":"Acorn_woodpecker","group":"Bird","where":"Oak woodlands from Oregon through Mexico to Colombia","diet":"Acorns and insects","facts":["It stores acorns in holes it drills in a \"granary\" tree.","A granary has hundreds to thousands of holes, each drilled for one acorn.","It lives in family groups that share the work."],"lesson":"Save together. Acorn woodpecker families build a shared pantry, one acorn at a time.","about":"The acorn woodpecker (Melanerpes formicivorus) is a medium-sized woodpecker with a length of around 20 cm (8 in), and an average weight of 85 g (3.0 oz). It is found across Central America, as well as North into the western United States and South into parts of Colombia.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Acorn_woodpecker_holding_a_nut_in_its_beak-0225.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Frank Schulenburg","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acorn_woodpecker_holding_a_nut_in_its_beak-0225.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorn_woodpecker","sci":"Melanerpes formicivorus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":255},
{"name":"Hoatzin","page":"Hoatzin","group":"Bird","where":"Swamps and riverbanks of the Amazon and Orinoco basins","diet":"Leaves","facts":["Its chicks have claws on their wings, for climbing.","It ferments leaves in its gut like a cow, which makes it smell and earns it the name \"stinkbird.\"","It's the national bird of Guyana."],"lesson":"Be proud of your quirks. The hoatzin is unlike any other bird alive.","about":"The hoatzin (Opisthocomus hoazin) is a species of tropical bird found in swamps, riparian forests, and mangroves of the Amazon and Orinoco Basins in South America. It is the only extant species in the genus Opisthocomus which is the only extant genus in the family Opisthocomidae under the order Opisthocomiformes.","description":"Species of bird in South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Hoatzin_%28Opisthocomus_hoazin%29_Rio_Napo.jpg/1920px-Hoatzin_%28Opisthocomus_hoazin%29_Rio_Napo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoatzin_(Opisthocomus_hoazin)_Rio_Napo.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoatzin","sci":"Opisthocomus hoazin","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":256},
{"name":"Resplendent Quetzal","page":"Resplendent_quetzal","group":"Bird","where":"Cloud forests from southern Mexico to Panama","diet":"Fruit, especially wild avocados, and insects","facts":["Males have tail streamers up to about a meter (3 feet) long.","It was sacred to the Maya and Aztecs.","Guatemala's money, the quetzal, is named after it."],"lesson":"Freedom is precious. Legend says the quetzal can't live in a cage.","about":"The resplendent quetzal (Pharomachrus mocinno) is a small bird found in Central America and southern Mexico that lives in tropical forests, particularly montane cloud forests. They are part of the family Trogonidae and have two recognized subspecies, P. m. mocinno and P. m. costaricensis.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Resplendent_quetzal_san_gerardo_de_dota_3.31.24_DSC_3989-topaz-denoiseraw.jpg/1920px-Resplendent_quetzal_san_gerardo_de_dota_3.31.24_DSC_3989-topaz-denoiseraw.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"lwolfartist","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resplendent_quetzal_san_gerardo_de_dota_3.31.24_DSC_3989-topaz-denoiseraw.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resplendent_quetzal","sci":"Pharomachrus mocinno","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":257},
{"name":"Bar-tailed Godwit","page":"Bar-tailed_godwit","group":"Bird","where":"Breeds in the Arctic and Alaska; winters in New Zealand and Australia","diet":"Bristle-worms, small clams, snails and crustaceans","facts":["It holds the record for the longest nonstop flight of any bird, from Alaska to Tasmania, over 13,000 km.","It carries more fat than any other migrating bird studied, and shrinks its gut to make room.","That flight is also the longest journey any animal makes without stopping to feed."],"lesson":"Prepare for the big leap. The godwit stores up huge fat reserves before its record flight.","about":"The bar-tailed godwit (Limosa lapponica) is a large and strongly migratory wader in the family Scolopacidae, which feeds on bristle-worms and shellfish on coastal mudflats and estuaries. It has distinctive red breeding plumage, long legs, and a long upturned bill.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg/1920px-Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bar-tailed_Godwit.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar-tailed_godwit","sci":"Limosa lapponica","status":"NT","mass":0.354,"length":null,"life":null,"n":258},
{"name":"Common Cuckoo","page":"Common_cuckoo","group":"Bird","where":"Europe and Asia in summer, Africa in winter","diet":"Hairy caterpillars and other insects","facts":["It lays its eggs in other birds' nests.","Its newly hatched chick pushes the host's eggs out of the nest.","The whole cuckoo family is named after the male's call."],"lesson":"Do your own work. The cuckoo is famous for making others raise its young.","about":"The cuckoo, common cuckoo, European cuckoo or Eurasian cuckoo (Cuculus canorus) is a member of the cuckoo order of birds, Cuculiformes, which includes the roadrunners, the anis and the coucals. This species is a widespread summer migrant to Europe and Asia, and winters in Africa.","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Cuckoo_%2851169010335%29.jpg/1920px-Cuckoo_%2851169010335%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andy Morffew from Itchen Abbas, Hampshire, UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuckoo_(51169010335).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_cuckoo","sci":"Cuculus canorus","status":"LC","mass":0.117,"length":null,"life":null,"n":259},
{"name":"Barn Swallow","page":"Barn_swallow","group":"Bird","where":"Nearly worldwide: summers in the north, winters in the south","diet":"Flying insects","facts":["It's the most widespread swallow in the world.","It builds a cup-shaped nest of mud under eaves and bridges.","It migrates to spend the winter as far away as Africa and southern Asia."],"lesson":"Build with what's around you. The barn swallow makes its home from mud, one beakful at a time.","about":"The barn swallow (Hirundo rustica) is the most widespread species of swallow in the world, occurring on all continents, with vagrants reported even in Antarctica. It is a distinctive passerine bird with blue upperparts and a long, deeply forked tail. In Anglophone Europe, it is just called the swallow; in northern Europe, it is the only member of family Hirundinidae called a \"swallow\" rather than a \"martin\".","description":"Species of bird","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Rauchschwalbe_Hirundo_rustica.jpg/1920px-Rauchschwalbe_Hirundo_rustica.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Andreas Trepte","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rauchschwalbe_Hirundo_rustica.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barn_swallow","sci":"Hirundo rustica","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":260},
{"name":"American Alligator","page":"American_alligator","group":"Reptile","where":"Freshwater swamps, rivers and lakes of the southeastern US, including all of Florida","diet":"Fish, turtles, birds and mammals","facts":["The temperature of the nest decides whether the babies hatch male or female.","Hunting made it endangered in 1973, and it was taken off the list in 1987.","It's the official state reptile of Florida, Louisiana and Mississippi."],"lesson":"Comebacks are possible. The alligator went from endangered to everywhere in Florida.","about":"The American alligator (Alligator mississippiensis), sometimes referred to as a common alligator or just gator, is a large crocodilian reptile native to the Southeastern United States. It is one of the two extant species in the genus Alligator, and is larger than the only other living alligator species, the Chinese alligator.","description":"Crocodilian native to the Southeastern United States","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/American_Alligator.jpg/1920px-American_Alligator.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"User:Postdlf","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Alligator.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_alligator","sci":"Alligator mississippiensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":261},
{"name":"American Crocodile","page":"American_crocodile","group":"Reptile","where":"Coastal mangroves and salty water of South Florida, the Caribbean and Latin America","diet":"Fish, crabs, birds and turtles","facts":["South Florida is the only place in the world where crocodiles and alligators live side by side.","It handles salt water much better than the alligator.","It has a narrower snout and lighter color than an alligator."],"lesson":"Know your neighbors. The croc and the gator share South Florida, each in its own kind of water.","about":"The American crocodile (Crocodylus acutus) is a species of crocodilian found in the Neotropics. It is the most widespread of the four extant species of crocodiles from the Americas, with populations present from South Florida, the Caribbean islands of Cuba, Jamaica, and Hispaniola, and the coasts of Mexico to as far south as Peru, Ecuador, Colombia, and Venezuela.","description":"Species of crocodile from the Neotropics","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Crocodylus_acutus_mexico_02-edit1.jpg/1920px-Crocodylus_acutus_mexico_02-edit1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Tomás Castelazo","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crocodylus_acutus_mexico_02-edit1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_crocodile","sci":"Crocodylus acutus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":262},
{"name":"Gharial","page":"Gharial","group":"Reptile","where":"Big rivers of northern India and Nepal","diet":"Fish","facts":["Its long, very thin snout is built for snapping up fish.","Adult males grow a knob on the tip of the snout, named after an Indian pot, the ghara.","It's critically endangered, left in only about 2 percent of its old range."],"lesson":"Be built for your job. The gharial's thin snout is perfect for catching fish, and nothing else.","about":"The gharial (Gavialis gangeticus), also known as gavial or fish-eating crocodile, is a crocodilian in the family Gavialidae and among the longest of all living crocodilians. Mature females are 2.6–4.5 m (8.5–14.8 ft) long, and males 3–6 m (9.8–19.7 ft). Adult males have a distinct boss at the end of the snout, which resembles an earthenware pot known as a ghara, hence the name \"gharial\".","description":"Crocodilian native to the Indian subcontinent","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Gharial_%28Gavialis_gangeticus%29_male.jpg/1920px-Gharial_%28Gavialis_gangeticus%29_male.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gharial_(Gavialis_gangeticus)_male.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gharial","sci":"Gavialis gangeticus","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":263},
{"name":"Chinese Alligator","page":"Chinese_alligator","group":"Reptile","where":"Wetlands of the lower Yangtze River, China","diet":"Snails, clams, fish and small animals","facts":["It's one of the most endangered crocodilians in the world.","It's much smaller than the American alligator, about 1.5 to 2.1 meters (5 to 7 feet) long.","It spends the winter dormant in burrows."],"lesson":"Rest when you must. The Chinese alligator sleeps out the cold winter in its burrow.","about":"The Chinese alligator (Alligator sinensis; simplified Chinese: 鼍; traditional Chinese: 鼉; pinyin: tuó), also known as the Yangtze alligator (simplified Chinese: 扬子鳄; traditional Chinese: 揚子鱷; pinyin: yángzǐ'è), China alligator, or historically the muddy dragon, is a crocodilian endemic to China.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/ChineseAlligator15.JPG/1920px-ChineseAlligator15.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChineseAlligator15.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_alligator","sci":"Alligator sinensis","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":264},
{"name":"Leatherback Sea Turtle","page":"Leatherback_sea_turtle","group":"Reptile","where":"Every ocean, nesting on tropical beaches, including Florida's","diet":"Jellyfish","facts":["It's the largest turtle in the world.","Instead of a hard shell, it has leathery skin over its bones.","Its front flippers can span 2.7 meters (almost 9 feet), the biggest of any sea turtle."],"lesson":"Go your own way. The leatherback has no hard shell, and still crosses whole oceans.","about":"The leatherback sea turtle (Dermochelys coriacea), sometimes called the lute turtle, leathery turtle or simply the luth, is a large species of sea turtle. The largest of all living turtles and the heaviest non-crocodilian reptile, it reaches lengths of up to 2.7 metres (8 ft 10 in) and weights of 500 kilograms (1,100 lb). It is the only living species in the genus Dermochelys and family Dermochelyidae.","description":"Species of marine reptile","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Leatherback_sea_turtle_Tinglar%2C_USVI_%285839996547%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Southeast Region","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leatherback_sea_turtle_Tinglar,_USVI_(5839996547).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leatherback_sea_turtle","sci":"Dermochelys coriacea","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":265},
{"name":"Green Sea Turtle","page":"Green_sea_turtle","group":"Reptile","where":"Warm seas worldwide, nesting on beaches including Florida's","diet":"Seagrass and algae","facts":["It's named for the greenish color of its fat, not its shell.","Adults eat only plants, unlike other sea turtles.","Females return to the beach where they hatched to lay their own eggs."],"lesson":"Remember where you came from. Green turtles return to the beach where they were born.","about":"The green sea turtle (Chelonia mydas), also known as the green turtle, black sea turtle, and Pacific green turtle, is a species of large sea turtle of the family Cheloniidae. It is the only species in the genus Chelonia. Its range extends throughout tropical and subtropical seas around the world, with two distinct populations in the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans, but it is also found in the Indian Ocean.","description":"Species of large sea reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Green_sea_turtle_%28Chelonia_mydas%29_Moorea.jpg/1920px-Green_sea_turtle_%28Chelonia_mydas%29_Moorea.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_sea_turtle_(Chelonia_mydas)_Moorea.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_sea_turtle","sci":"Chelonia mydas","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":266},
{"name":"Loggerhead Sea Turtle","page":"Loggerhead_sea_turtle","group":"Reptile","where":"Oceans worldwide; most US nests are on Florida's beaches","diet":"Crabs, conchs, clams and jellyfish","facts":["Florida's beaches host most of the loggerhead nests in the United States.","Its big head and strong jaws crush shellfish.","It's the largest hard-shelled turtle in the world."],"lesson":"Mind the light. Bright beach lights can lead loggerhead hatchlings the wrong way.","about":"The loggerhead sea turtle (Caretta caretta), loggerhead turtle or loggerhead, is a species of sea turtle distributed throughout the world. It is a marine reptile, belonging to the family Cheloniidae. The average loggerhead measures around 90 cm (35 in) in carapace length when fully grown.","description":"Species of marine reptile distributed throughout the world","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Loggerhead_sea_turtle.jpg/1920px-Loggerhead_sea_turtle.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"ukanda","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loggerhead_sea_turtle.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loggerhead_sea_turtle","sci":"Caretta caretta","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":267},
{"name":"Gopher Tortoise","page":"Gopher_tortoise","group":"Reptile","where":"Sandy pine and scrub lands of the southeastern US, especially Florida","diet":"Grasses, leaves and fruit","facts":["Its burrows shelter hundreds of other kinds of animals.","Its burrows average about 4.6 meters (15 feet) long, and can stretch up to 15 meters (48 feet).","It's Florida's state tortoise."],"lesson":"Make room for others. The gopher tortoise's burrow is a shelter for hundreds of other species.","about":"The gopher tortoise (Gopherus polyphemus) is a species of tortoise in the family Testudinidae. The species is native to the Southeastern United States. The gopher tortoise is seen as a keystone species because it digs burrows that provide shelter for at least 360 other animal species.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Gopher_Tortoise_-_Gopherus_polyphemus%2C_Lake_June-in-Winter_Scrub_State_Park%2C_Lake_Placid%2C_Florida_-_31527638716.jpg/1920px-Gopher_Tortoise_-_Gopherus_polyphemus%2C_Lake_June-in-Winter_Scrub_State_Park%2C_Lake_Placid%2C_Florida_-_31527638716.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Judy Gallagher","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gopher_Tortoise_-_Gopherus_polyphemus,_Lake_June-in-Winter_Scrub_State_Park,_Lake_Placid,_Florida_-_31527638716.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gopher_tortoise","sci":"Gopherus polyphemus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":268},
{"name":"Alligator Snapping Turtle","page":"Alligator_snapping_turtle","group":"Reptile","where":"Rivers and swamps of the southeastern US, including the Florida Panhandle","diet":"Fish, turtles and other animals","facts":["It lures fish into its mouth with a pink, worm-like flap on its tongue.","It's one of the heaviest freshwater turtles in the world.","It likes to hide among sunken trees, root balls and undercut banks."],"lesson":"Let opportunity come to you. The alligator snapper waits with its mouth open and its lure wiggling.","about":"The alligator snapping turtle (Macrochelys temminckii) is a large species of turtle in the family Chelydridae. They are the largest freshwater turtle in North America. The species is endemic to freshwater habitats in the United States.","description":"Species of turtle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Alligator_snapping_turtle.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Gary M. Stolz/U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alligator_snapping_turtle.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alligator_snapping_turtle","sci":"Macrochelys temminckii","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":269},
{"name":"Mata Mata","page":"Mata_mata","group":"Reptile","where":"Slow rivers and swamps of the Amazon basin and the eastern Guianas","diet":"Fish","facts":["Its shell looks like bark, and its head like a fallen leaf.","It sucks in fish by opening its mouth suddenly.","In 2020, scientists found that the mata matas of the Orinoco are a separate species."],"lesson":"Blend in and wait. The mata mata looks like debris until a fish comes too close.","about":"The mata mata, mata-mata, or matamata (Chelus fimbriata) is a South American species of freshwater turtle found in the Amazon basin and river system of the eastern Guianas. It was formerly believed to also occur in the Orinoco basin, western Guianas and upper Rio Negro–Branco system, but in 2020 these populations were found to belong to a separate species, Chelus orinocensis (Orinoco mata mata).","description":"Species of freshwater turtle","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/2009_Chelus_fimbriatus.JPG/1920px-2009_Chelus_fimbriatus.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"J. Patrick Fischer","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2009_Chelus_fimbriatus.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mata_mata","sci":"Chelus fimbriatus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":270},
{"name":"Aldabra Giant Tortoise","page":"Aldabra_giant_tortoise","group":"Reptile","where":"Aldabra Atoll in the Seychelles","diet":"Grasses, leaves and woody plants","facts":["It's one of the largest tortoises in the world.","Males weigh about 250 kg (550 lb) on average.","One named Adwaita, taken from the Seychelles in the 1700s, ended up at Calcutta Zoo."],"lesson":"Slow and steady lasts. Giant tortoises can live for well over a century.","about":"The Aldabra giant tortoise (Aldabrachelys gigantea), Aldabra tortoise, or simply giant tortoise, is a species of tortoise in the family Testudinidae and genus Aldabrachelys. The species is endemic to the Seychelles, with the nominate subspecies, A. g. gigantea native to Aldabra atoll. It is one of the largest tortoises in the world.","description":"Species of tortoise","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Giant_Tortoise.JPG/1920px-Giant_Tortoise.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Yotcmdr","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_Tortoise.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldabra_giant_tortoise","sci":"Aldabrachelys gigantea","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":271},
{"name":"Green Iguana","page":"Green_iguana","group":"Reptile","where":"Central and South America, and spreading in South Florida","diet":"Leaves, flowers and fruit","facts":["It has a \"third eye\" on top of its head that senses light.","In South Florida, cold snaps make iguanas freeze and drop out of trees.","It can drop its tail to escape, and grow a new one."],"lesson":"Let go of what holds you back. The iguana drops its tail to get away.","about":"The green iguana (Iguana iguana), also known as the American iguana or the common green iguana, is a large, arboreal, mostly herbivorous species of lizard of the genus Iguana. Usually, this animal is simply called the iguana. The green iguana ranges over a large geographic area; it is native from southern Brazil and Paraguay as far north as Mexico.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Iguana_iguana_%28male_resting%29.jpg/1920px-Iguana_iguana_%28male_resting%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Hans Hillewaert","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iguana_iguana_(male_resting).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_iguana","sci":"Iguana iguana","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":272},
{"name":"Marine Iguana","page":"Marine_iguana","group":"Reptile","where":"Rocky coasts of the Galápagos Islands","diet":"Seaweed","facts":["It's the only living lizard that spends time in the sea.","In El Niño years it can shrink by up to 20 percent, then grow back when food returns.","It's found only on the Galápagos Islands."],"lesson":"Adapt to hard times. The marine iguana even shrinks when the food runs out.","about":"The marine iguana (Amblyrhynchus cristatus), also known as the sea iguana, saltwater iguana, or Galápagos marine iguana, is a species of iguana found only on the Galápagos Islands (Ecuador). Unique among modern lizards, it is a marine reptile that has the ability to forage in the sea for algae, which make up almost all of its diet. Marine iguanas are the only extant lizard that spends time in a marine environment.","description":"Species of marine reptile endemic to Galapagos Islands","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Amblyrhynchus_cristatus_%283838137696%29.jpg/1920px-Amblyrhynchus_cristatus_%283838137696%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"RAF-YYC from Calgary, Canada","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amblyrhynchus_cristatus_(3838137696).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_iguana","sci":"Amblyrhynchus cristatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":273},
{"name":"Thorny Devil","page":"Thorny_devil","group":"Reptile","where":"Deserts and scrub of central and western Australia","diet":"Ants","facts":["It drinks through its skin: tiny grooves carry water to its mouth.","It eats thousands of ants a day.","It has a \"false head\" on its neck to fool predators."],"lesson":"Prickly outside, gentle inside. The thorny devil looks fierce, but it only eats ants.","about":"The thorny devil (Moloch horridus), also known commonly as the mountain devil, thorny lizard, thorny dragon, and moloch, is a species of lizard in the family Agamidae. The species is endemic to Australia. It is the sole species in the genus Moloch.","description":"Species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Thornydevil.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Bäras (talk · contribs)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thornydevil.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thorny_devil","sci":"Moloch horridus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":274},
{"name":"Frilled-neck Lizard","page":"Frill-necked_lizard","group":"Reptile","where":"Woodlands of northern Australia and southern New Guinea","diet":"Insects and small animals","facts":["It opens a big frill around its neck to scare off threats.","It can run on its back legs to hunt or escape.","It basks upright on tree trunks in the morning and evening."],"lesson":"Look bigger than your problems. The frilled lizard spreads its frill and stands its ground.","about":"The frilled lizard (Chlamydosaurus kingii), also known commonly as the frilled agama, frillneck lizard, frill-necked lizard or frilled dragon, is a species of lizard in the family Agamidae. The species is native to northern Australia and southern New Guinea and is the only member of the genus Chlamydosaurus.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Frill-necked_Lizard_%28Chlamydosaurus_kingii%29_%288692622586%29.jpg/1920px-Frill-necked_Lizard_%28Chlamydosaurus_kingii%29_%288692622586%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Matt from Melbourne, Australia","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frill-necked_Lizard_(Chlamydosaurus_kingii)_(8692622586).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frilled_lizard","sci":"Chlamydosaurus kingii","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":275},
{"name":"Gila Monster","page":"Gila_monster","group":"Reptile","where":"Deserts of the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico","diet":"Eggs, baby birds and small mammals","facts":["It's one of the few venomous lizards.","A diabetes medicine, exenatide, was developed from a hormone in its saliva.","It stores fat in its thick tail to get through lean times."],"lesson":"Something feared can heal. The Gila monster's saliva led to a medicine for millions.","about":"The Gila monster (Heloderma suspectum) is a species of venomous lizard native to the Southwestern United States and the northwestern Mexican state of Sonora. It is a heavy, slow-moving reptile, up to 22 in (56 cm) long, and it is the only venomous lizard native to the United States.","description":"Largest species of lizard in the United States","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Gila_monster2.JPG/1920px-Gila_monster2.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Blueag9","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gila_monster2.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gila_monster","sci":"Heloderma suspectum","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":276},
{"name":"Tuatara","page":"Tuatara","group":"Reptile","where":"Islands off New Zealand","diet":"Beetles, crickets, spiders and eggs","facts":["It looks like a lizard, but it's the only survivor of an ancient group, the rhynchocephalians.","Its name is Māori for \"peaks on the back.\"","Its closest living relatives are the lizards and snakes."],"lesson":"Endure. The tuatara's kind has lasted since the age of the dinosaurs.","about":"The tuatara (Sphenodon punctatus) is a species of reptile endemic to New Zealand. Despite its close resemblance to lizards, it is the only extant member of a distinct lineage, the previously highly diverse order Rhynchocephalia. The name tuatara is derived from the Māori language and means \"peaks on the back\".","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Tuatara_%285205719005%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Sid Mosdell from New Zealand","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuatara_(5205719005).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuatara","sci":"Sphenodon","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":277},
{"name":"Common Basilisk","page":"Common_basilisk","group":"Reptile","where":"Rainforests near rivers in Central America and northwestern South America","diet":"Insects and small animals","facts":["It can run on water, which earns it the nickname \"Jesus Christ lizard.\"","Fringes of skin on its back toes help it stay up.","When it slows down, it sinks and swims."],"lesson":"Move fast through hard places. The basilisk crosses water by never slowing down.","about":"The common basilisk (Basiliscus basiliscus) is a species of lizard in the family Corytophanidae. The species is endemic to Central America and South America, where it is found near rivers and streams in rainforests. It is also known as the Jesus Christ lizard, Jesus lizard, South American Jesus lizard, or lagarto de Jesus Cristo for its ability to run on the surface of water.","description":"Species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Common_basilisk_%28Basiliscus_basiliscus%29_male_2.jpg/1920px-Common_basilisk_%28Basiliscus_basiliscus%29_male_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_basilisk_(Basiliscus_basiliscus)_male_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_basilisk","sci":"Basiliscus basiliscus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":278},
{"name":"Tokay Gecko","page":"Tokay_gecko","group":"Reptile","where":"Forests and houses of Southeast Asia, and wild in South Florida","diet":"Insects","facts":["The male's loud mating call sounds like \"to-kay,\" which gives it its name.","It often moves into houses, hunting insects on walls and ceilings at night.","It's thought to be the third-largest gecko in the world."],"lesson":"Speak up. The tokay gecko is named after its own loud call.","about":"The tokay gecko (Gekko gecko) is a nocturnal arboreal gecko in the genus Gekko, the true geckos. It is native to Asia and some Pacific Islands.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Gekko_gecko_192144834.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Gerard Chartier","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gekko_gecko_192144834.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokay_gecko","sci":"Gekko gecko","status":"LC","mass":0.06,"length":0.152,"life":null,"n":279},
{"name":"Satanic Leaf-tailed Gecko","page":"Satanic_leaf-tailed_gecko","group":"Reptile","where":"Rainforests of northern and central Madagascar","diet":"Insects","facts":["Its tail looks just like a dead leaf, even with notches.","By day, its shape and color make it blend into the branches.","It has no eyelids, and licks its eyes clean."],"lesson":"Stillness is a skill. The leaf-tailed gecko hides in plain sight by not moving.","about":"Uroplatus phantasticus, the satanic leaf-tailed gecko, eyelash leaf-tailed gecko or the phantastic leaf-tailed gecko, is a species of gecko indigenous to the island of Madagascar. First described in 1888 by George Albert Boulenger, U. phantasticus is the smallest in body of the Uroplatus geckos, though there is an ongoing debate as to whether one of its cousins, U. ebenaui, is smaller because of its shorter tail.","description":"Species of gecko endemic to Madagascar","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Uroplatus_phantasticus_12893423.jpg/1920px-Uroplatus_phantasticus_12893423.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Julien Renoult","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uroplatus_phantasticus_12893423.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uroplatus_phantasticus","sci":"Uroplatus phantasticus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":280},
{"name":"Texas Horned Lizard","page":"Texas_horned_lizard","group":"Reptile","where":"Dry plains of the southern US and northern Mexico","diet":"Harvester ants","facts":["It can squirt blood from its eyes to scare off predators like coyotes.","Harvester ants are most of what it eats.","It's the state reptile of Texas."],"lesson":"Have a surprise in reserve. The horned lizard's last defense startles even coyotes.","about":"The Texas horned lizard (Phrynosoma cornutum) is one of about 21 North American species of spikey-bodied reptiles called horned lizards, all belonging the genus Phrynosoma. It occurs in south-central regions of the US and northeastern Mexico, as well as several isolated introduced records and populations from Southern United States.","description":"Species of lizard","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Phrynosoma_cornutum_-_Flickr_-_aspidoscelis_%289%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Patrick Alexander from Las Cruces, NM","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phrynosoma_cornutum_-_Flickr_-_aspidoscelis_(9).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_horned_lizard","sci":"Phrynosoma cornutum","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":281},
{"name":"Green Anole","page":"Carolina_anole","group":"Reptile","where":"Trees and shrubs of the southeastern US, including Florida","diet":"Insects and spiders","facts":["It can turn from green to brown.","Males show off a pink throat fan, called a dewlap.","It competes more and more with the brown anole, brought in from the Caribbean."],"lesson":"Hold your ground. The green anole keeps its place even as newcomers move in.","about":"Anolis carolinensis or green anole (among other names below) is a tree-dwelling species of anole lizard native to the southeastern United States and introduced to islands in the Pacific and Caribbean. A small to medium-sized lizard, the green anole is a trunk-crown ecomorph and can change its color to several shades from brown to green.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Anole_Lizard_Hilo_Hawaii_edit.jpg/1920px-Anole_Lizard_Hilo_Hawaii_edit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Paul Hirst (Phirst)","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anole_Lizard_Hilo_Hawaii_edit.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anolis_carolinensis","sci":"Anolis carolinensis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":282},
{"name":"King Cobra","page":"King_cobra","group":"Reptile","where":"Forests of India and Southeast Asia","diet":"Mostly other snakes","facts":["It's the longest venomous snake in the world.","It mainly hunts other snakes, even other king cobras.","It's the only snake that builds a nest, out of dry leaves."],"lesson":"Respect power. The king cobra is the world's longest venomous snake, and usually avoids people.","about":"The king cobra (Ophiophagus hannah) is a species complex of snakes endemic to Asia. With an average length of 3.18 to 4 m (10.4 to 13.1 ft) and a record length of 5.85 m (19.2 ft), it is the world's longest venomous snake and among the heaviest. Under the genus Ophiophagus, it is not phylogenetically a true cobra despite its common name and some resemblance.","description":"Group of venomous snakes from Asia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/12_-_The_Mystical_King_Cobra_and_Coffee_Forests.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Michael Allen Smith from Seattle, USA","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12_-_The_Mystical_King_Cobra_and_Coffee_Forests.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_cobra","sci":"Ophiophagus hannah","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":283},
{"name":"Black Mamba","page":"Black_mamba","group":"Reptile","where":"Savannas and woodlands of eastern and southern Africa","diet":"Rodents, birds and small animals","facts":["It's named for the inky black inside of its mouth, not its gray skin.","It can move at up to 16 km/h (10 mph) for short distances.","It's the second-longest venomous snake in the world, after the king cobra."],"lesson":"Speed and warning go together. The black mamba shows its black mouth before it strikes.","about":"The black mamba (Dendroaspis polylepis) is a species of highly venomous snake belonging to the family Elapidae. It is native to parts of sub-Saharan Africa. First formally described by Albert Günther in 1864, it is the second-longest venomous snake after the king cobra; mature specimens generally exceed 2 m (6 ft 7 in) and commonly grow to 3 m (9.8 ft).","description":"Species of venomous snake","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Dendroaspis_polylepis_%2814%29.jpg/1920px-Dendroaspis_polylepis_%2814%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"TimVickers","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dendroaspis_polylepis_(14).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_mamba","sci":"Dendroaspis polylepis","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":284},
{"name":"Green Anaconda","page":"Green_anaconda","group":"Reptile","where":"Swamps and slow rivers of tropical South America","diet":"Capybaras, caimans, deer and fish","facts":["It's the heaviest snake in the world.","It gives birth to live young.","It spends much of its time in the water."],"lesson":"Move gracefully in your element. The huge anaconda is clumsy on land and powerful in water.","about":"The green anaconda (Eunectes murinus) is a species of snake in the family Boidae (boas). The species is native to northern South America. It is one of the longest and heaviest known extant snake species.","description":"Species of snake","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Sucuri_verde.jpg/1920px-Sucuri_verde.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"MKAMPIS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sucuri_verde.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_anaconda","sci":"Eunectes murinus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":285},
{"name":"Reticulated Python","page":"Reticulated_python","group":"Reptile","where":"Rainforests and grasslands of Southeast Asia","diet":"Mammals and birds","facts":["It's the longest snake in the world.","Its name comes from its net-like pattern.","It's a strong swimmer and has reached many small islands."],"lesson":"Length takes time. The reticulated python grows into the longest snake in the world.","about":"The reticulated python (Malayopython reticulatus) is a python species native to South and Southeast Asia. It is the world's longest snake, and the third heaviest after the green anaconda and Burmese python. It is a non-venomous constrictor and an excellent swimmer that has been reported far out at sea.","description":"Species of snake","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Python_reticulatus_%D1%81%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD-2.jpg/1920px-Python_reticulatus_%D1%81%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD-2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"This photography was created by Mariluna. Other photos see here.","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Python_reticulatus_%D1%81%D0%B5%D1%82%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BD-2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reticulated_python","sci":"Python reticulatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":286},
{"name":"Eastern Diamondback Rattlesnake","page":"Eastern_diamondback_rattlesnake","group":"Reptile","where":"Pine woods and scrub of the southeastern US, including Florida","diet":"Rabbits, rats and squirrels","facts":["It's the largest rattlesnake, and one of the heaviest venomous snakes in the Americas.","It often shelters in gopher tortoise burrows.","Its rattle gets a new segment each time it sheds its skin."],"lesson":"Give fair warning. The rattlesnake rattles first, and would rather be left alone.","about":"The eastern diamondback rattlesnake (Crotalus adamanteus) is a species of pit viper in the family Viperidae. The species is endemic to the Southeastern United States. It is the largest rattlesnake species and one of the heaviest venomous snakes in the Americas.","description":"Species of reptile endemic to the southeastern US","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Adult_Crotalus_adamanteus.jpg/1920px-Adult_Crotalus_adamanteus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Caudatejake","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adult_Crotalus_adamanteus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_diamondback_rattlesnake","sci":"Crotalus adamanteus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":287},
{"name":"Eastern Indigo Snake","page":"Eastern_indigo_snake","group":"Reptile","where":"Pine woods and scrub of Florida and southeastern Georgia","diet":"Other snakes, frogs, rodents and birds","facts":["It's the longest native snake in the United States.","It eats other snakes, even venomous rattlesnakes.","It shelters in gopher tortoise burrows in winter."],"lesson":"Gentle strength. The indigo snake is huge and calm, and keeps rattlesnakes in check.","about":"The eastern indigo snake (Drymarchon couperi) is a species of large, nonvenomous snake in the subfamily Colubrinae of the family Colubridae. Native to the Southeastern United States, it is the longest native snake species in the country.","description":"Species of snake","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Eastern_Indigo_Snake.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Dirk Stevenson","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Indigo_Snake.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_indigo_snake","sci":"Drymarchon couperi","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":288},
{"name":"Gaboon Viper","page":"Gaboon_viper","group":"Reptile","where":"Rainforests and woodlands of Africa south of the Sahara","diet":"Rodents, birds and frogs","facts":["It has the longest fangs of any snake.","It's the heaviest viper in Africa.","Its pattern makes it almost invisible among fallen leaves."],"lesson":"Patience is power. The Gaboon viper lies still for long periods, waiting for the right moment.","about":"The Gaboon viper (Bitis gabonica), also called the Gaboon adder, is a large and highly venomous species of viper found in the rainforests and savannas of sub-Saharan Africa. It is the largest member of the genus Bitis. Like all other vipers, it is venomous, and it has the longest fangs of any venomous snake – up to 2 inches (5.1 cm) in length – and the highest venom yield of any snake.","description":"Species of snake","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Bitis_gabonica.jpg/1920px-Bitis_gabonica.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Marius Burger","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bitis_gabonica.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaboon_viper","sci":"Bitis gabonica","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":289},
{"name":"Yellow-lipped Sea Krait","page":"Yellow-lipped_sea_krait","group":"Reptile","where":"Coral reefs of the Indian and western Pacific Oceans","diet":"Eels","facts":["It hunts in the sea but comes onto land to rest, digest and lay eggs.","It isn't aggressive, and bites only when it feels threatened.","Its tail is flat like a paddle for swimming."],"lesson":"Know when to come ashore. The sea krait hunts in the water and rests on land.","about":"The yellow-lipped sea krait (Laticauda colubrina), also known as the banded sea krait or colubrine sea krait, is a species of highly venomous snake found in tropical Indo-Pacific oceanic waters. The snake has distinctive black stripes and a yellow snout, with a paddle-like tail for use in swimming. It spends much of its time under water to hunt, but returns to land to digest, rest, and reproduce.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Banded_Sea_Krait_%28Laticauda_colubrina%29_%2814641328165%29.jpg/1920px-Banded_Sea_Krait_%28Laticauda_colubrina%29_%2814641328165%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bernard DUPONT from FRANCE","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banded_Sea_Krait_(Laticauda_colubrina)_(14641328165).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow-lipped_sea_krait","sci":"Laticauda colubrina","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":290},
{"name":"Sidewinder","page":"Crotalus_cerastes","group":"Reptile","where":"Sandy deserts of the southwestern US and northwestern Mexico","diet":"Lizards and rodents","facts":["Its name comes from the way it moves, throwing its body sideways across the sand.","It has little horn-like scales over its eyes.","It often lies almost buried in sand, and its horns may keep sand out of its eyes."],"lesson":"Find your own way forward. The sidewinder crosses loose sand by not going straight.","about":"Crotalus cerastes, also known commonly as the horned rattlesnake, the sidewinder, and the sidewinder rattlesnake, is a species of pit viper, a venomous snake in the subfamily Crotalinae of the family Viperidae. The species is native to desert regions of the Southwestern United States and adjacent northwestern Mexico. Three subspecies are recognized as being valid.","description":"Species of snake","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Crotalus_cerastes_mesquite_springs_CA-2.jpg/1920px-Crotalus_cerastes_mesquite_springs_CA-2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"derivative work: Victorrocha (talk · contribs) Crotalus_cerastes_mesquite_spring","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crotalus_cerastes_mesquite_springs_CA-2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crotalus_cerastes","sci":"Crotalus cerastes","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":291},
{"name":"Common Flying Dragon","page":"Draco_volans","group":"Reptile","where":"Rainforests of Southeast Asia","diet":"Ants and termites","facts":["It glides between trees on \"wings\" of skin stretched over long ribs.","The colors of its \"wings\" and throat flap play a key part in courtship.","It's often seen running along branches, showing off and gliding."],"lesson":"Leap when you're ready. The flying dragon glides from tree to tree instead of climbing down.","about":"Draco volans also commonly known as the common flying dragon, is a species of lizard in the family Agamidae. The species is endemic to Southeast Asia. Like other members of genus Draco, this species has the ability to glide using winglike lateral extensions of skin called patagia.","description":"Species of reptile","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Draco_volans_01.JPG/1920px-Draco_volans_01.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"H. Zell","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Draco_volans_01.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draco_volans","sci":"Draco volans","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":292},
{"name":"Red-eyed Tree Frog","page":"Agalychnis_callidryas","group":"Amphibian","where":"Rainforests of Central America, from Honduras to northern Colombia","diet":"Insects","facts":["It lays its eggs on leaves over water, and the tadpoles drop in.","Its embryos can sense a snake's vibrations and hatch early to escape.","Its smooth green skin helps it blend into the leaves."],"lesson":"Be ready to move. The red-eyed tree frog can hatch early to escape danger.","about":"The red-eyed tree frog or red-eyed leaf frog (Agalychnis callidryas) is a species of tree frog in the subfamily Phyllomedusinae native to forests of Central America and northwestern South America.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Red-eyed_Tree_Frog_%28Agalychnis_callidryas%29_1.png/1920px-Red-eyed_Tree_Frog_%28Agalychnis_callidryas%29_1.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Red-eyed Tree Frog (Agalychnis callidryas) 3.jpg: Geoff Gallice from Gainesville","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-eyed_Tree_Frog_(Agalychnis_callidryas)_1.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agalychnis_callidryas","sci":"Agalychnis callidryas","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":293},
{"name":"Reticulated Glass Frog","page":"Hyalinobatrachium_valerioi","group":"Amphibian","where":"Rainforest streams of Costa Rica, Panama, Colombia and Ecuador","diet":"Insects","facts":["Its belly is see-through, though its heart is hidden by white tissue.","The father stays with the eggs day and night.","It lays its eggs on leaves over streams."],"lesson":"Be transparent. The glass frog has nothing to hide.","about":"Hyalinobatrachium valerioi, sometimes known as the La Palma glass frog, is a species of frog in the family Centrolenidae. It is found in central Costa Rica and south to Panama and the Pacific lowlands and slopes of western Colombia and Ecuador; also in the Magdalena River Valley of Colombia (Colombian distribution is poorly resolved).","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Hyalinobatrachium_valerioi_Costa_Rica.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Gabriele Kothe-Heinrich","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyalinobatrachium_valerioi_Costa_Rica.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyalinobatrachium_valerioi","sci":"Hyalinobatrachium valerioi","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":294},
{"name":"Goliath Frog","page":"Goliath_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"Rainforest rivers of Cameroon and Equatorial Guinea","diet":"Insects, crabs, fish and frogs","facts":["It's the largest living frog: with its legs stretched out, over 80 cm (31 inches) long.","It builds round nests in or beside rivers for its eggs.","Its tadpoles are no bigger than other frogs'."],"lesson":"Start small, grow big. Goliath frogs begin as ordinary tadpoles.","about":"The goliath frog (Conraua goliath), otherwise known commonly as the giant slippery frog and the goliath bullfrog, is a species of frog in the family Conrauidae. The goliath frog is the largest living frog. Specimens can reach up to about 45 centimetres (18 in) in snout–vent length and 4.5 kilograms (9.9 lb) in weight.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Conraua_goliath_photo_from_iNaturalist_539752953.jpg/1920px-Conraua_goliath_photo_from_iNaturalist_539752953.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"jeanlouisamiet","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conraua_goliath_photo_from_iNaturalist_539752953.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goliath_frog","sci":"Conraua goliath","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":295},
{"name":"Cane Toad","page":"Cane_toad","group":"Amphibian","where":"Central and South America; invasive in Australia and Florida","diet":"Insects and almost anything else","facts":["It was brought to Australia in 1935 to eat beetles, and spread across the country.","Glands on its skin make a strong poison.","A female can lay tens of thousands of eggs at once."],"lesson":"Think before you act. The cane toad was meant to solve one problem and became a bigger one.","about":"The cane toad (Rhinella marina), also known as the giant neotropical toad or marine toad, is a large, terrestrial true toad native to South and mainland Central America, but which has been introduced to various islands throughout Oceania and the Caribbean, as well as Northern Australia.","description":"World's largest true toad","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Canetoadmale.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Benjamint444","license":"GFDL 1.2","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canetoadmale.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cane_toad","sci":"Rhinella marina","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":296},
{"name":"American Bullfrog","page":"American_bullfrog","group":"Amphibian","where":"Ponds and lakes of North America","diet":"Insects, fish, frogs and almost anything it can swallow","facts":["It's the largest true frog in North America.","Its deep call sounds like a bull's moo.","It eats almost anything it can fit in its mouth."],"lesson":"Have a big voice. The bullfrog's call carries across the whole pond.","about":"The American bullfrog (Lithobates catesbeianus), often simply known as the bullfrog in Canada and the United States, is a species of large frog in the family Ranidae (true frogs). The species is native to eastern North America. It typically inhabits large permanent water bodies such as swamps, ponds, and lakes.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/North-American-bullfrog1.jpg/1920px-North-American-bullfrog1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Carl D. Howe","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North-American-bullfrog1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_bullfrog","sci":"Rana catesbeiana","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":297},
{"name":"Wood Frog","page":"Wood_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"Forests of Alaska, Canada and the northeastern US","diet":"Insects","facts":["It freezes solid in winter and thaws back to life in spring.","While frozen, it has no heartbeat, breathing or brain activity.","It lives farther north than any other North American frog."],"lesson":"You can come back. The wood frog thaws out every spring.","about":"Lithobates sylvaticus or Rana sylvatica, commonly known as the wood frog, is a frog species that has a broad distribution over North America, extending from the boreal forest of the north to the southern Appalachians, with several notable disjunct populations including lowland eastern North Carolina.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Lithobates_sylvaticus_%28Woodfrog%29.jpg/1920px-Lithobates_sylvaticus_%28Woodfrog%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Brian Gratwicke","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithobates_sylvaticus_(Woodfrog).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_frog","sci":"Lithobates sylvaticus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":298},
{"name":"Chinese Giant Salamander","page":"Chinese_giant_salamander","group":"Amphibian","where":"Mountain streams of China","diet":"Fish, crabs, frogs and insects","facts":["It can grow to 1.8 meters (6 feet) long and 50 kg (110 lb).","Some of its cries sound like a young child's, so in Chinese it's called the \"infant fish.\"","It's critically endangered, mostly from being caught for food."],"lesson":"Protect the elders. The giant salamander's line goes back to the age of dinosaurs.","about":"The Chinese giant salamander (Andrias davidianus) is the largest salamander and the largest amphibian in the world. It is fully aquatic, and is endemic to rocky mountain streams and lakes in the Yangtze river basin of central China. It has also been introduced to Kyoto Prefecture in Japan, and possibly to Taiwan.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Velemlok_%C4%8D%C3%ADnsk%C3%BD_zoo_praha_1.jpg/1920px-Velemlok_%C4%8D%C3%ADnsk%C3%BD_zoo_praha_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Petr Hamerník","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velemlok_%C4%8D%C3%ADnsk%C3%BD_zoo_praha_1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_giant_salamander","sci":"Andrias davidianus","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":299},
{"name":"Fire Salamander","page":"Fire_salamander","group":"Amphibian","where":"Damp forests of Europe","diet":"Insects, worms and slugs","facts":["Its bright yellow and black warn that its skin is poisonous.","One lived for more than 50 years in a German museum.","Its young grow up in small, clean brooks and ponds."],"lesson":"Show your boundaries. The fire salamander's colors tell predators to keep away.","about":"The fire salamander (Salamandra salamandra) is a common species of salamander found in Europe. It is black with yellow spots or stripes to a varying degree; some specimens can be nearly completely black while on others the yellow is dominant. Shades of red and orange may sometimes appear, either replacing or mixing with the yellow according to subspecies.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Salamandra_salamandra_MHNT_1.jpg/1920px-Salamandra_salamandra_MHNT_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Didier Descouens","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salamandra_salamandra_MHNT_1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_salamander","sci":"Salamandra salamandra","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":300},
{"name":"Texas Blind Salamander","page":"Texas_blind_salamander","group":"Amphibian","where":"Underground waters of the Edwards Aquifer, near San Marcos, Texas","diet":"Blind cave shrimp, snails and other tiny water animals","facts":["Its tiny eyes are hidden under its skin and can't form pictures.","It feels its way with sensors along its body that pick up movement in the water.","The first ones were found in 1895, in water pumped up from a new well 58 meters (190 feet) deep."],"lesson":"Use the senses you have. The Texas blind salamander can't see, so it feels its way through the dark.","about":"The Texas blind salamander (Eurycea rathbuni) is a rare and endangered cave-dwelling troglobite amphibian native to San Marcos, Hays County, Texas, specifically the San Marcos Pool of the Edwards Aquifer. This species resembles the olm, another stygofaunal salamander from Europe.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Texas_blind_salamander.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Photograph courtesy of Joe N. Fries, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Texas_blind_salamander.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_blind_salamander","sci":"Eurycea rathbuni","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":301},
{"name":"Hellbender","page":"Hellbender","group":"Amphibian","where":"Clear, rocky streams of the eastern US","diet":"Crayfish and small fish","facts":["It's the largest salamander in North America.","It breathes mostly through the wrinkly folds of its skin.","Its nicknames include \"snot otter\" and \"lasagna lizard.\""],"lesson":"Clean water matters. The hellbender only lives in cool, clean streams.","about":"The hellbender (Cryptobranchus alleganiensis), also known as the hellbender salamander, is a species of aquatic giant salamander endemic to the eastern and central United States. It is the largest salamander in North America. A member of the family Cryptobranchidae, the hellbender is the only extant member of the genus Cryptobranchus.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Hellbender.jpg/1920px-Hellbender.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Brian Gratwicke","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hellbender.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellbender","sci":"Cryptobranchus alleganiensis","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":302},
{"name":"Tomato Frog","page":"Dyscophus_antongilii","group":"Amphibian","where":"Northeastern Madagascar","diet":"Insects","facts":["It's as red-orange as a ripe tomato.","When threatened, its skin oozes a white, glue-like slime.","It lives in swamps and shallow pools."],"lesson":"Stick to your defenses. The tomato frog's slime gums up a predator's mouth.","about":"Dyscophus antongilii, the Madagascar tomato frog, or simply tomato frog, is a species of frog in the family Microhylidae.","description":"Species of frog","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Tomato_Frog%2C_Maroantsetra%2C_Madagascar_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_455535654.jpg/1920px-Tomato_Frog%2C_Maroantsetra%2C_Madagascar_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_455535654.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"(c) Rob Foster, some rights reserved (CC BY)","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomato_Frog,_Maroantsetra,_Madagascar_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_455535654.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyscophus_antongilii","sci":"Dyscophus antongilii","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":303},
{"name":"Purple Frog","page":"Purple_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"The Western Ghats mountains of India","diet":"Termites","facts":["It digs underground and eats termites there with its pointed snout.","One of its names is the purple pig-nosed frog.","Its closest relatives live in the Seychelles, a sign of ancient land connections."],"lesson":"Some things take a lifetime to find. The adult purple frog wasn't formally described by science until 2003.","about":"The purple frog (Nasikabatrachus sahyadrensis), also known commonly as the Indian purple frog, the Mahabali frog, and the pignose frog, is a frog species in the family Nasikabatrachidae. The species is endemic to the Western Ghats in India. Although the adult frog was formally described in October 2003, the juvenile form of the species had been described earlier in 1917.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Nasikabatrachus_sahyadrensis.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Original uploader was Karthickbala at ta.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasikabatrachus_sahyadrensis.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple_frog","sci":"Nasikabatrachus sahyadrensis","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":304},
{"name":"Common Surinam Toad","page":"Common_Surinam_toad","group":"Amphibian","where":"Slow rivers and swamps of tropical South America, from the Amazon to the Guianas and Trinidad","diet":"Fish and small animals","facts":["Its eggs sink into the skin of the mother's back, and tiny toads pop out 4 to 5 months later.","It's almost completely flat, like a dead leaf.","It has no tongue, and it's also called the star-fingered toad."],"lesson":"Carry your future. The Surinam toad raises its young right in its own skin.","about":"The common Surinam toad, the Suriname toad, or star-fingered toad (Pipa pipa), is a fully-aquatic species of frog, in the family Pipidae, with a widespread range across much of tropical South America and the island of Trinidad.","description":"Species of frog","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Pipa_pipa01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Hugo Claessen","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pipa_pipa01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Surinam_toad","sci":"Pipa pipa","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":305},
{"name":"Squirrel Tree Frog","page":"Squirrel_tree_frog","group":"Amphibian","where":"The southeastern US, including Florida, and the Bahamas","diet":"Insects","facts":["It's small, about 4 cm (1.5 inches) long.","Its breeding is closely tied to rain.","It can change color, from green to brown."],"lesson":"Read the signs. The squirrel tree frog seems to know the rain is coming.","about":"The squirrel tree frog (Dryophytes squirellus) is a small species of tree frog found in the southeastern United States, from Texas to Virginia. It is an introduced species to the Bahamas. Squirrel tree frogs are small frogs, about 1.5 inches in length as adults.","description":"Species of amphibian","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Hyla_squirella2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jim Conrad","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyla_squirella2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squirrel_tree_frog","sci":"Dryophytes squirellus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":306},
{"name":"Whale Shark","page":"Whale_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Warm oceans around the world","diet":"Plankton and small fish","facts":["It's the largest fish in the world.","It feeds by filtering plankton out of the water.","Each one's pattern of spots is unique, and scientists use it to tell them apart."],"lesson":"Gentle can be giant. The biggest fish in the sea lives on the smallest food.","about":"The whale shark (Rhincodon typus) is a slow-moving, filter-feeding carpet shark and the largest known extant fish species. An individual with a length of 18.8 m (61.7 ft) has been considered the largest reliably recorded. The whale shark holds many records for size in the animal kingdom, most notably being by far the largest living non-cetacean animal.","description":"Largest living species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Similan_Dive_Center_-_great_whale_shark.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Abe Khao Lak","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Similan_Dive_Center_-_great_whale_shark.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale_shark","sci":"Rhincodon typus","status":"EN","mass":12000.0,"length":14.021,"life":null,"n":307},
{"name":"Basking Shark","page":"Basking_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Cool coastal seas around the world","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It's the second-largest fish in the world.","It swims slowly with its huge mouth wide open, straining plankton.","It's named for the way it seems to bask at the surface."],"lesson":"Take it slow. The basking shark gets all it needs by cruising calmly, mouth open.","about":"The basking shark (Cetorhinus maximus) is the second-largest living shark and fish, after the whale shark. It is one of three plankton-eating shark species, along with the whale shark and megamouth shark. Typically, basking sharks reach 7.9 m (26 ft) in length, but large individuals have been known to grow more than 10 m (33 ft) long.","description":"Species of shark","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Cetorhinus_maximus_by_greg_skomal.JPG/1920px-Cetorhinus_maximus_by_greg_skomal.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Greg Skomal / NOAA Fisheries Service","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cetorhinus_maximus_by_greg_skomal.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basking_shark","sci":"Cetorhinus maximus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":308},
{"name":"Tiger Shark","page":"Tiger_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Tropical and warm seas, including around Florida","diet":"Fish, sea turtles, birds and almost anything else","facts":["It eats almost anything, and license plates and tires have been found in tiger sharks' stomachs.","The dark stripes that give it its name fade as it grows up.","Its notched teeth can saw through sea turtle shells."],"lesson":"Be open to new things, within reason. The tiger shark will try almost anything.","about":"The tiger shark (Galeocerdo cuvier) is a species of ground shark, and the only extant member of the genus Galeocerdo and family Galeocerdonidae. It is a large predator, with females capable of attaining a length of over 5 m (16 ft 5 in). Populations are found in many tropical and temperate waters, especially around central Pacific islands.","description":"Species of ground shark","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Tiger_shark.jpg/1920px-Tiger_shark.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiger_shark.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger_shark","sci":"Galeocerdo cuvier","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":309},
{"name":"Bull Shark","page":"Bull_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Warm coasts, rivers and lakes around the world, including Florida","diet":"Fish, dolphins, turtles and birds","facts":["It can live in fresh water, and swims far up rivers like the Mississippi and the Amazon.","It even lives in freshwater Lake Nicaragua, where people call it the Lake Nicaragua shark.","It mostly lives in warm, shallow water along coasts and in rivers."],"lesson":"Be at home in more than one world. The bull shark moves between salt and fresh water with ease.","about":"The bull shark (Carcharhinus leucas), also known as the Zambezi shark (informally zambi) in Africa and Lake Nicaragua shark in Nicaragua, is a species of requiem shark commonly found worldwide in warm, shallow waters along coasts and in rivers. It is known for its aggressive nature, and presence mainly in warm, shallow brackish and freshwater systems including estuaries and (usually) lower reaches of rivers.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Bull_shark_%28Carcharhinus_leucas%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"João D'Andretta","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_shark_(Carcharhinus_leucas).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bull_shark","sci":"Carcharhinus leucas","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":310},
{"name":"Goblin Shark","page":"Goblin_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Deep waters around the world, from Japan to South Africa","diet":"Fish, squid and crustaceans","facts":["Its jaws shoot forward from its head to snatch prey.","Its pinkish skin is see-through enough to show the blood beneath.","It belongs to a family of sharks about 125 million years old."],"lesson":"Reach out when the moment comes. The goblin shark's jaws spring forward at just the right time.","about":"The goblin shark (Mitsukurina owstoni) is a rare species of deep-sea shark. Sometimes called a \"living fossil\", it is the only extant representative of the family Mitsukurinidae, a lineage some 125 million years old. This pink-skinned animal has a distinctive profile with an elongated, flat snout, and highly protrusible jaws containing prominent nail-like teeth.","description":"Deep-sea shark","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Goblin_shark_%28Mitsukurina_owstoni%29.jpg/1920px-Goblin_shark_%28Mitsukurina_owstoni%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Michigan State University/USFWS","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goblin_shark_(Mitsukurina_owstoni).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goblin_shark","sci":"Mitsukurina owstoni","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":311},
{"name":"Greenland Shark","page":"Greenland_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Cold waters of the Arctic and North Atlantic","diet":"Fish, seals and carrion","facts":["It may live between about 270 and 500 years, longer than any other known animal with a backbone.","It swims slower for its size than any other fish.","Its flesh is poisonous when fresh; in Iceland it's fermented into a dish called hákarl."],"lesson":"Slow and steady, for centuries. The Greenland shark takes its time, and outlives every other known animal with a backbone.","about":"The Greenland shark (Somniosus microcephalus), also known as the grey shark or gurry shark, is a large shark of the family Somniosidae (\"sleeper sharks\"), closely related to the Pacific and southern sleeper sharks. Inhabiting the North Atlantic and Arctic Oceans, they are notable for their exceptional longevity, although they are poorly studied because of the depth and remoteness of their natural habitat.","description":"Species of shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Somniosus_microcephalus_okeanos.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"NOAA Okeanos Explorer Program","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somniosus_microcephalus_okeanos.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland_shark","sci":"Somniosus microcephalus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":312},
{"name":"Shortfin Mako","page":"Shortfin_mako_shark","group":"Fish","where":"Warm and temperate oceans around the world","diet":"Squid and fish like mackerel, herring and tuna","facts":["It's the fastest shark.","It can leap high out of the water.","It keeps its body warmer than the water around it."],"lesson":"Speed takes energy. The mako keeps its body warm, which helps make it the fastest shark.","about":"The shortfin mako shark (Isurus oxyrinchus), also known as the shortfin mako, blue pointer, or bonito shark, is a species of large mackerel shark. It is commonly referred to as the mako shark, as is the longfin mako shark (Isurus paucus).","description":"Species of shark","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Shortfin_mako_%28Isurus_oxyrinchus%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Fábio Olmos","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shortfin_mako_(Isurus_oxyrinchus).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shortfin_mako_shark","sci":"Isurus oxyrinchus","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":313},
{"name":"Giant Oceanic Manta Ray","page":"Giant_oceanic_manta_ray","group":"Fish","where":"Tropical and temperate oceans around the world","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It's the largest ray in the world, up to about 7 meters (23 feet) wide.","It has one of the largest brains of any fish.","It visits \"cleaning stations,\" where small fish pick it clean."],"lesson":"Let others help you. The manta ray stops at reef cleaning stations to be looked after.","about":"The giant oceanic manta ray, giant manta ray, oceanic manta ray, or pelagic manta ray (Mobula birostris) is a species of ray in the family Mobulidae and the largest type of ray in the world. It is circumglobal and is typically found in tropical and subtropical waters but can also be found in temperate waters.","description":"Species of cartilaginous fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Manta_birostris-Thailand4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"jon hanson from london, UK","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manta_birostris-Thailand4.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_oceanic_manta_ray","sci":"Mobula birostris","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":314},
{"name":"Spotted Eagle Ray","page":"Spotted_eagle_ray","group":"Fish","where":"Warm coastal waters of the Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Clams, snails, shrimp and crabs","facts":["It sometimes leaps right out of the water.","It digs clams out of the sand with its snout.","Its chevron-shaped teeth crush the hard shells of clams and snails."],"lesson":"Take a leap now and then. The spotted eagle ray sometimes flies right out of the sea.","about":"The spotted eagle ray (Aetobatus narinari) is a cartilaginous fish of the eagle ray family, Aetobatidae. As traditionally recognized, it is found globally in tropical regions, including the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian Oceans.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Eagle_Ray_Turks_and_Caicos_Dec_15_2006.JPG/1920px-Eagle_Ray_Turks_and_Caicos_Dec_15_2006.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"the manta ray had a family","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eagle_Ray_Turks_and_Caicos_Dec_15_2006.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spotted_eagle_ray","sci":"Aetobatus narinari","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":315},
{"name":"Smalltooth Sawfish","page":"Smalltooth_sawfish","group":"Fish","where":"Shallow coasts and estuaries of the warm Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Mostly fish","facts":["Its long, toothed \"saw\" senses prey and slashes it.","It's a ray, not a shark.","Southwest Florida is its last stronghold in the United States."],"lesson":"Protect the last places. Southwest Florida's mangroves are the sawfish's last refuge in the US.","about":"The smalltooth sawfish (Pristis pectinata) is a species of sawfish in the family Pristidae. It is found in shallow tropical and subtropical waters in coastal and estuarine parts of the Atlantic. Reports from elsewhere are now believed to be misidentifications of other species of sawfish.","description":"Species of cartilaginous fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Pristis_pectinata_SI.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"D Ross Robertson","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pristis_pectinata_SI.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smalltooth_sawfish","sci":"Pristis pectinata","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":316},
{"name":"Ocean Sunfish","page":"Ocean_sunfish","group":"Fish","where":"Temperate and tropical oceans around the world","diet":"Small fish, squid, crustaceans and some jellyfish","facts":["It's one of the heaviest bony fish in the world.","A female can carry 300 million eggs, more than any other known animal with a backbone.","It often lies on its side at the surface, as if sunbathing."],"lesson":"Rest in the light. The ocean sunfish warms up at the surface after diving into the cold.","about":"The ocean sunfish (Mola mola), also known as the common mola, is one of the largest ray-finned fish in the world. It is the type species of the genus Mola, and one of five extant species in the family Molidae. It was formerly misidentified as the heaviest bony fish, which is actually a different and closely related species of sunfish, Mola alexandrini.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Mola_mola.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"U.S. National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mola_mola.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_sunfish","sci":"Mola mola","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":317},
{"name":"Atlantic Bluefin Tuna","page":"Atlantic_bluefin_tuna","group":"Fish","where":"The Atlantic Ocean and the Mediterranean","diet":"Fish, squid and crustaceans","facts":["It has been recorded at up to 680 kg (1,500 lb).","Western bluefins spawn in the Gulf of Mexico.","Fish over 150 kg (330 lb) are called giant bluefin."],"lesson":"Grow into greatness. A bluefin starts as a tiny larva and can grow to 680 kg.","about":"The Atlantic bluefin tuna (Thunnus thynnus) is a species of tuna in the family Scombridae. It is variously known as the northern bluefin tuna (mainly when including Pacific bluefin as a subspecies), giant bluefin tuna (for individuals exceeding 150 kg [330 lb]), and formerly as the tunny. Atlantic bluefins are native to both the western and eastern Atlantic Ocean, as well as the Mediterranean Sea.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Bluefin-big.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bluefin-big.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_bluefin_tuna","sci":"Thunnus thynnus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":318},
{"name":"Indo-Pacific Sailfish","page":"Indo-Pacific_sailfish","group":"Fish","where":"Warm waters of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Fast fish like tuna and mackerel","facts":["Its upper jaw is long and pointed, like a spear.","Its first back fin is huge, shaped like a sail.","It has spread into the Mediterranean from the Red Sea, through the Suez Canal."],"lesson":"Raise your sail. The sailfish shows its full size when the moment counts.","about":"The Indo-Pacific sailfish (Istiophorus platypterus) is a sailfish native to the Indian and Pacific Oceans and is naturalized in the Atlantic where it has entered the Mediterranean Sea via the Suez Canal as a Lessepsian migrant.","description":"Species of ray-finned fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/Istiophorus_platypterus_101956558.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Robert Webster","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istiophorus_platypterus_101956558.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indo-Pacific_sailfish","sci":"Istiophorus platypterus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":319},
{"name":"Swordfish","page":"Swordfish","group":"Fish","where":"Oceans around the world","diet":"Fish and squid","facts":["It slashes at prey with its sword rather than spearing it.","It has a special organ that warms its eyes and brain.","Adults have no scales and no teeth."],"lesson":"Use your tools wisely. The swordfish's bill is for slashing, not stabbing.","about":"The swordfish (Xiphias gladius), also known as the broadbill in some countries, are large, highly migratory predatory fish characterized by a long, flat, sword-like, pointed bill. They are the sole member of the family Xiphiidae. They are a popular sport fish of the billfish category.","description":"Fish which has a long, pointed bill","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Xiphias_gladius_Linnaeus%2C_1758_2599925021.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Naturalis Biodiversity Center","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiphias_gladius_Linnaeus,_1758_2599925021.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swordfish","sci":"Xiphias gladius","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":320},
{"name":"West Indian Ocean Coelacanth","page":"West_Indian_Ocean_coelacanth","group":"Fish","where":"Deep waters off the Comoros Islands and East Africa","diet":"Fish, which it hunts at night","facts":["Scientists first recognized it from one caught off South Africa in 1938.","It can move both its upper and lower jaw, unlike any other living bony animal with a backbone.","The 1938 coelacanth is still on display at the East London Museum in South Africa."],"lesson":"Never write anything off. Coelacanths were thought to be long extinct, until a fishing boat brought one up in 1938.","about":"The West Indian Ocean coelacanth (Latimeria chalumnae), sometimes known as the gombessa, African coelacanth, and simply coelacanth, is a crossopterygian, one of two extant species of coelacanth, a rare order of vertebrates more closely related to lungfish and tetrapods than to the common ray-finned fishes. The other extant species is the Indonesian coelacanth (L. menadoensis).","description":"Species of lobe-finned bony fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Coelacanth_off_Pumula_on_the_KwaZulu-Natal_South_Coast%2C_South_Africa%2C_on_22_November_2019.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Bruce A.S. Henderson","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coelacanth_off_Pumula_on_the_KwaZulu-Natal_South_Coast,_South_Africa,_on_22_November_2019.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Indian_Ocean_coelacanth","sci":"Latimeria chalumnae","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":321},
{"name":"Red Lionfish","page":"Red_lionfish","group":"Fish","where":"Native to the Indo-Pacific; invasive in the Atlantic, Caribbean and Florida","diet":"Small fish and shrimp","facts":["Its long spines are venomous.","It has become invasive in the Caribbean and along the US East Coast.","It probably first got loose off Florida in the 1980s, from the aquarium trade."],"lesson":"Watch what you bring in. Lionfish released from aquariums now crowd Florida's reefs.","about":"The red lionfish (Pterois volitans) is a venomous coral reef fish in the family Scorpaenidae, order Scorpaeniformes. It is mainly native to the Indo-Pacific region, but has become an invasive species in the Caribbean Sea, as well as along the East Coast of the United States and East Mediterranean and also found in Brazil at Fernando de Noronha.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Pterois_volitans_Manado-e_edit.jpg/1920px-Pterois_volitans_Manado-e_edit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Photo by Jens Petersen, Edited by User:Olegiwit (cloned in part of fins) and Fir","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pterois_volitans_Manado-e_edit.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_lionfish","sci":"Pterois volitans","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":322},
{"name":"Mandarinfish","page":"Synchiropus_splendidus","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the western Pacific","diet":"Tiny crustaceans and worms","facts":["It's one of only two animals with a backbone known to get blue color from pigment cells.","In other animals, blue comes from the structure of skin or feathers, not pigment.","It's covered in a smelly, bitter slime instead of scales."],"lesson":"Beauty can protect itself. The dazzling mandarinfish's bitter slime probably helps keep predators away.","about":"Synchiropus splendidus, the mandarinfish or mandarin dragonet, is a small, brightly colored member of the dragonet family, which is popular in the saltwater aquarium trade. The mandarinfish is native to the Pacific, ranging approximately from the Ryukyu Islands south to Australia. It can usually be found in some of the warmer waters.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Synchiropus_splendidus_2_Luc_Viatour.jpg/1920px-Synchiropus_splendidus_2_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Luc Viatour","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Synchiropus_splendidus_2_Luc_Viatour.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synchiropus_splendidus","sci":"Synchiropus splendidus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":323},
{"name":"Atlantic Blue Tang","page":"Atlantic_blue_tang","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the western Atlantic, the Caribbean and Florida","diet":"Algae","facts":["It changes from yellow as a youngster to blue as an adult.","It even eats algae off the bodies of other fish.","It has many nicknames, including blue doctor and yellow barber."],"lesson":"Change is part of growing. The blue tang starts out yellow and grows into its blue.","about":"Acanthurus coeruleus is a species of marine ray-finned fish belonging to the family Acanthuridae, the surgeonfishes, unicornfishes and tangs, found in the Atlantic Ocean. It can grow up to 39 centimetres (15 in) long. Common names include Atlantic blue tang, blue barber, blue doctor, blue doctorfish, blue tang, blue tang surgeonfish, yellow barber, and yellow doctorfish.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Acanthurus_coeruleus_-_Sanc1516.jpg/1920px-Acanthurus_coeruleus_-_Sanc1516.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"NOAA Photo Library","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acanthurus_coeruleus_-_Sanc1516.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acanthurus_coeruleus","sci":"Acanthurus coeruleus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":324},
{"name":"Queen Angelfish","page":"Queen_angelfish","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the western Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Sponges","facts":["It eats mostly sponges.","A blue \"crown\" spot on its forehead gives it its name.","Young ones set up cleaning stations and pick parasites off bigger fish."],"lesson":"Serve before you rule. Young queen angelfish clean other fish before they wear the crown.","about":"The queen angelfish (Holacanthus ciliaris), also known as the blue angelfish, golden angelfish, or yellow angelfish, is a species of marine angelfish found in the western Atlantic Ocean. It is a benthic (ocean floor) warm-water species that lives in coral reefs. It is recognized by its blue and yellow coloration and a distinctive spot or \"crown\" on its forehead.","description":"Species of marine angelfish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Queen_Angelfish.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Pedro Lastra","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Angelfish.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_angelfish","sci":"Holacanthus ciliaris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":325},
{"name":"Stoplight Parrotfish","page":"Stoplight_parrotfish","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the Caribbean, Florida and the western Atlantic","diet":"Algae scraped from coral","facts":["It scrapes algae off dead coral with its beak-like mouth.","It can change from female to male.","At night it hides under coral boulders."],"lesson":"Tend your garden. The stoplight parrotfish keeps reefs clear by grazing the algae.","about":"The stoplight parrotfish (Sparisoma viride) is a species of marine ray-finned fish, a parrotfish from the family Scaridae, inhabiting coral reefs in Florida, Caribbean Sea, Gulf of Mexico, Bermuda and as far south as Brazil. It mainly feeds on algae by scraping and excavating it with its teeth. Like most parrotfish, it is able to change sex.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Stoplight-parrotfish.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Adona9 at English Wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoplight-parrotfish.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoplight_parrotfish","sci":"Sparisoma viride","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":326},
{"name":"Spot-fin Porcupinefish","page":"Spot-fin_porcupinefish","group":"Fish","where":"Tropical and subtropical seas around the world","diet":"Snails, crabs and sea urchins","facts":["When in danger, it swallows water to puff up, and raises its spines.","Parts of its body, like its liver and skin, hold a poison called tetrodotoxin.","Its scales have turned into spines."],"lesson":"Make yourself hard to swallow. The porcupinefish puffs up so no one can take a bite.","about":"The spot-fin porcupinefish (Diodon hystrix), also known as the spotted porcupinefish, black-spotted porcupinefish or simply porcupinefish, is a member of the family Diodontidae.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pez_erizo_moteado_%28Diodon_hystrix%29%2C_Cabo_Pulmo%2C_Baja_California%2C_M%C3%A9xico%2C_2024-12-19%2C_DD_23.jpg/1920px-Pez_erizo_moteado_%28Diodon_hystrix%29%2C_Cabo_Pulmo%2C_Baja_California%2C_M%C3%A9xico%2C_2024-12-19%2C_DD_23.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Diego Delso","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pez_erizo_moteado_(Diodon_hystrix),_Cabo_Pulmo,_Baja_California,_M%C3%A9xico,_2024-12-19,_DD_23.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spot-fin_porcupinefish","sci":"Diodon hystrix","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":0.91,"life":null,"n":327},
{"name":"Humpback Anglerfish","page":"Humpback_anglerfish","group":"Fish","where":"The deep sea around the world","diet":"Fish and almost anything else it meets","facts":["The female lures prey with a glowing \"fishing rod\" on her snout.","Its glow comes from bacteria living in the lure.","Scientists once thought the tiny males were a different kind of fish."],"lesson":"Bring your own light. In the dark deep sea, the female anglerfish carries her own.","about":"The humpback anglerfish (Melanocetus johnsonii) is a species of black seadevil in the family of Melanocetidae, which means \"black sea monster\". The species is named after James Yate Johnson, an English naturalist who discovered the first specimen in Madeira in 1863. The common names include anglerfish, viperfish, and fangtoothfish.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Melanocetus_johnsonii1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ryan Somma","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melanocetus_johnsonii1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humpback_anglerfish","sci":"Melanocetus johnsonii","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":328},
{"name":"Atlantic Wolffish","page":"Atlantic_wolffish","group":"Fish","where":"Cold rocky seafloor of the North Atlantic","diet":"Crabs, sea urchins, clams and snails","facts":["Fang-like front teeth and rows of crushing molars let it eat hard-shelled prey.","It makes its own antifreeze to survive near-freezing water.","Both parents help care for the eggs."],"lesson":"Stay warm inside. The wolffish makes its own antifreeze to thrive in icy water.","about":"The Atlantic wolffish (Anarhichas lupus), also known as the seawolf, Atlantic catfish, ocean catfish, devil fish, wolf eel (the common name for its Pacific relative), woof or sea cat, is a marine fish of the wolffish family Anarhichadidae, native to the North Atlantic Ocean.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Anarhichas-lupus-Atlanterhavsparken.jpg/1920px-Anarhichas-lupus-Atlanterhavsparken.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anarhichas-lupus-Atlanterhavsparken.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_wolffish","sci":"Anarhichas lupus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":329},
{"name":"Sockeye Salmon","page":"Sockeye_salmon","group":"Fish","where":"The North Pacific and the rivers and lakes around it","diet":"Mostly zooplankton, plus small shrimp and insects","facts":["It turns bright red with a green head when it swims upriver to spawn.","It dies after it spawns.","Alaska's Bristol Bay has the world's largest sockeye catch, up to 30 million fish a year."],"lesson":"Give your all. The sockeye swims upriver to spawn, spending everything on the next generation.","about":"The sockeye salmon (Oncorhynchus nerka), also called red salmon, kokanee salmon, blueback salmon, or simply sockeye, is an anadromous species of salmon found in the Northern Pacific Ocean and rivers discharging into it. This species is a Pacific salmon that is primarily red in hue during spawning. They can grow up to 84 cm (2 ft 9 in) in length and weigh 2.3 to 7 kg (5–15 lb).","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Sockeye_salmon_swimming_right.jpg/1920px-Sockeye_salmon_swimming_right.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Milton Love, Marine Science Institute, University of California, Santa Barbara, ","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sockeye_salmon_swimming_right.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sockeye_salmon","sci":"Oncorhynchus nerka","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":330},
{"name":"Electric Eel","page":"Electrophorus_electricus","group":"Fish","where":"Rivers and swamps of northern South America","diet":"Fish","facts":["Special organs in its body make electric shocks.","Despite its name, it isn't an eel, but a kind of knifefish.","It comes up to breathe about every ten minutes, and gets most of its oxygen from the air."],"lesson":"Know your power. The electric eel stuns its prey with electricity before it bites.","about":"Electrophorus electricus is the best-known species of electric eel. It is a South American electric fish. Until the discovery of two additional species in 2019, the genus was classified as the monotypic, with this species the only one in the genus.","description":"South American electric fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Electric-eel.jpg/1920px-Electric-eel.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Steven G. Johnson","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electric-eel.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrophorus_electricus","sci":"Electrophorus electricus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":331},
{"name":"Red-bellied Piranha","page":"Red-bellied_piranha","group":"Fish","where":"Rivers of South America","diet":"Fish, insects, worms and carrion","facts":["It's famous as a fierce predator, but it's mostly a scavenger.","It travels in groups for protection, but rarely hunts as a pack.","In a feeding frenzy, a school can eat a large animal within minutes."],"lesson":"Fear is often exaggerated. Piranhas are mostly scavengers that school for safety.","about":"The red-bellied piranha, also known as the red piranha (Pygocentrus nattereri), is a species of freshwater ray-finned fish belonging to the family Serrasalmidae, which includes the pacus, piranhas and related fishes. The red-bellied piranha occurs in South America, found in the Amazon, Paraguay, Paraná and Essequibo basins, as well as coastal rivers of northeastern Brazil.","description":"Fish species native to South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Pygocentrus_nattereri_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_01.jpg/1920px-Pygocentrus_nattereri_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"H. Zell","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pygocentrus_nattereri_-_Karlsruhe_Zoo_01.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red-bellied_piranha","sci":"Pygocentrus nattereri","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":332},
{"name":"Arapaima","page":"Arapaima_gigas","group":"Fish","where":"Rivers and floodplains of the Amazon basin","diet":"Fish","facts":["It's one of the largest freshwater fish in the world.","It must come up to breathe air every few minutes.","Its tough, armor-like scales help protect it from piranha attacks."],"lesson":"Armor up for tough places. The arapaima's armor-like scales help protect it from piranhas.","about":"Arapaima gigas, also known simply as Arapaima, pirarucu, or paiche, is a species of arapaima native to the basin of the Amazon River. Once believed to be the sole species in the genus, it is among the largest freshwater fish. It is the best-known species of Arapaimas.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Arapaima_gigas_at_Beijing_aquarium.JPG/1920px-Arapaima_gigas_at_Beijing_aquarium.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Shizhao","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arapaima_gigas_at_Beijing_aquarium.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arapaima_gigas","sci":"Arapaima gigas","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":333},
{"name":"Banded Archerfish","page":"Banded_archerfish","group":"Fish","where":"Mangroves and rivers of Southeast Asia and northern Australia","diet":"Insects","facts":["It shoots a jet of water to knock insects off leaves above the water.","Young archerfish learn to aim in schools, by watching each other.","It can reach a fallen insect within 50 milliseconds of it hitting the water."],"lesson":"Aim carefully. The archerfish hits its target from below the water.","about":"The banded archerfish (Toxotes jaculatrix) is a perciform fish of the archerfish genus Toxotes. Found in brackish water, it is silvery in colour and has a dorsal fin towards the posterior end. It has distinctive, semi-triangular markings along its sides.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Toxotes_jaculatrix.jpg/1920px-Toxotes_jaculatrix.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Chrumps","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toxotes_jaculatrix.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banded_archerfish","sci":"Toxotes jaculatrix","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":334},
{"name":"Atlantic Mudskipper","page":"Atlantic_mudskipper","group":"Fish","where":"Mudflats and mangroves of West Africa","diet":"Small crabs, worms and insects","facts":["It spends much of its time out of water, on the mud.","It skips across the mud using its fins.","It breathes through its skin and the water kept in its gill chambers."],"lesson":"Go where others can't. The mudskipper walks out of the water and onto the mud.","about":"The Atlantic mudskipper (Periophthalmus barbarus) is a species of mudskipper native to fresh, marine, and brackish waters of the tropical Atlantic coasts of Africa, including most offshore islands. The Greek scientific name Periophthalmus barbarus is named after the eyes that provide the Atlantic mudskipper with a wide field of vision.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/GambianMudskippers.jpg/1920px-GambianMudskippers.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GambianMudskippers.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_mudskipper","sci":"Periophthalmus barbarus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":335},
{"name":"Tropical Two-wing Flyingfish","page":"Tropical_two-wing_flyingfish","group":"Fish","where":"Tropical oceans around the world","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It glides over the water on its wing-like fins.","It leaps out of the water to escape predators.","It's also called the blue flyingfish, and grows to about 30 cm (12 inches)."],"lesson":"Rise above it. The flying fish escapes danger by leaving the water entirely.","about":"Exocoetus volitans, commonly known as the tropical two-wing flyingfish or blue flyingfish, is a species of ray-finned fish native to tropical and subtropical seas. It can glide above the surface of the sea to escape predators.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Exvol_u0.gif?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exvol_u0.gif"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exocoetus_volitans","sci":"Exocoetus volitans","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":336},
{"name":"Barreleye","page":"Macropinna_microstoma","group":"Fish","where":"The deep North Pacific","diet":"Small animals, perhaps stolen from jellyfish","facts":["Its head is see-through, like a fighter pilot's canopy.","Its tube-shaped eyes can turn to look up through its own head.","What look like eyes on its face are really its nostrils."],"lesson":"Look up. The barreleye's eyes turn upward to find food drifting above.","about":"The Pacific barreleye fish (Macropinna) is a genus of ray-finned fish belonging to Opisthoproctidae, the barreleye family. It contains one species, M. microstoma. It is recognized for a highly unusual transparent, fluid-filled shield on its head, through which the vivid green lenses of its eyes can be seen.","description":"Genus of ray-finned fishes","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Barreleye-fish_GoK.jpg/1920px-Barreleye-fish_GoK.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barreleye-fish_GoK.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macropinna","sci":"Macropinna","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":337},
{"name":"Weedy Seadragon","page":"Weedy_seadragon","group":"Fish","where":"Rocky reefs and seaweed of southern Australia","diet":"Tiny shrimp-like animals","facts":["The male carries the eggs on his tail.","Leafy flaps on its body help it hide among seaweed.","It's the marine emblem of the state of Victoria."],"lesson":"Share the load. The male weedy seadragon carries the eggs until they hatch.","about":"The common seadragon or weedy seadragon (Phyllopteryx taeniolatus) is a marine fish of the order Syngnathiformes, which also includes the similar pipefishes, seahorses, and trumpetfishes among other species.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Phyllopteryx_taeniolatus1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Richard Ling <wikipedia@rling.com> -->Rling","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phyllopteryx_taeniolatus1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_seadragon","sci":"Phyllopteryx taeniolatus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":338},
{"name":"Atlantic Goliath Grouper","page":"Atlantic_goliath_grouper","group":"Fish","where":"Reefs, wrecks and mangroves of the western Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Slow fish like stingrays and catfish, plus crabs and other crustaceans","facts":["It can grow to 2.5 meters (8 feet) long and 363 kg (800 lb).","Its young grow up in mangroves.","Fishing for it was banned in US waters in 1990, and since 2023 Florida has allowed 200 lottery permits a year."],"lesson":"Protection works. Since fishing for it was banned, the goliath grouper has moved up from critically endangered to vulnerable.","about":"The Atlantic goliath grouper, the jewfish or itajara (Epinephelus itajara), is a saltwater fish of the grouper family and one of the largest species of bony fish. The species can be found in the West Atlantic ranging from northeastern Florida, south throughout the Gulf of Mexico and the Caribbean Sea, and along South America to Brazil. In the East Pacific it ranges from Mexico to Peru.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Itajara.JPG/1920px-Itajara.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Albert kok","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Itajara.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_goliath_grouper","sci":"Epinephelus itajara","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":339},
{"name":"Atlantic Tarpon","page":"Atlantic_tarpon","group":"Fish","where":"Coastal waters of the Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Fish and crabs","facts":["Anglers call it the \"silver king.\"","It can fill its swim bladder with air and use it like a simple lung.","It evolved about 18 million years ago, making it one of the oldest living fish."],"lesson":"Keep your shine. The tarpon's silver scales flash as it leaps.","about":"The Atlantic tarpon (Megalops atlanticus) is a ray-finned fish that inhabits coastal waters, estuaries, lagoons, and rivers. It is also known as the silver king. It is found in the Atlantic Ocean, typically in tropical and subtropical regions, though it has been reported as far north as Nova Scotia and the Atlantic coast of southern France, and as far south as Argentina.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/Megalops_atlanticus.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"This illustration was made by Citron You must credit this : Citron / CC-BY-SA-3.","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Megalops_atlanticus.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_tarpon","sci":"Megalops atlanticus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":340},
{"name":"Green Moray","page":"Green_moray","group":"Fish","where":"Reefs and rocks of the western Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Fish and crabs","facts":["Its green color comes from a layer of mucus; underneath, it's darker.","It's mostly active at night.","Some fish, like the Brazilian snapper, seem to team up with green morays to hunt."],"lesson":"Teamwork across differences. Green morays and snappers sometimes hunt together.","about":"The green moray (Gymnothorax funebris) is a moray eel of the family Muraenidae, found in the western Atlantic Ocean from Long Island, New York, to Bermuda, to the northern Gulf of Mexico and to Brazil, at depths down to 40 m (130 ft). With a length up to 2.5 m (8.2 ft), it is the largest moray species of the tropical Atlantic and one of the largest species of moray eels known.","description":"Species of eel","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/GreenMorayEel.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Blueag9","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GreenMorayEel.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_moray","sci":"Gymnothorax funebris","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":341},
{"name":"Siamese Fighting Fish","page":"Siamese_fighting_fish","group":"Fish","where":"Rice paddies, ponds and slow streams of Thailand and nearby","diet":"Insects and their larvae","facts":["Males fight other males.","Males build floating nests of bubbles.","It breathes air at the surface."],"lesson":"Build, don't fight. The male betta's real work is the bubble nest, not the battle.","about":"The Siamese fighting fish (Betta splendens), commonly known as the Siamese fighter, or locally as the betta, is a freshwater fish native to Mainland Southeast Asia, namely Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar, Malaysia, Thailand, and Vietnam.","description":"Species of fish native to Southeast Asia","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/HM_Orange_M_Sarawut.jpg/1920px-HM_Orange_M_Sarawut.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Daniella Vereeken","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HM_Orange_M_Sarawut.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siamese_fighting_fish","sci":"Betta splendens","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":342},
{"name":"Australian Lungfish","page":"Australian_lungfish","group":"Fish","where":"Slow rivers of Queensland, Australia","diet":"Frogs, tadpoles, fish, small invertebrates and plants","facts":["It has a lung and can breathe air.","One named Granddad lived at Chicago's Shedd Aquarium for over 80 years.","It has barely changed in 100 million years."],"lesson":"If it works, keep it. The lungfish's design has lasted 100 million years.","about":"The Australian lungfish (Neoceratodus forsteri), also known as the Queensland lungfish, Burnett salmon and barramunda, is the only surviving member of the family Neoceratodontidae, and endemic to Australia. It is one of only six extant lungfish species in the world. Fossil records of this group date back 380 million years, around the time when the higher vertebrate classes were beginning to evolve.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Neoceratodus_forsteri%2C_2014-09-19.JPG/1920px-Neoceratodus_forsteri%2C_2014-09-19.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Mitch Ames","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neoceratodus_forsteri,_2014-09-19.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_lungfish","sci":"Neoceratodus forsteri","status":"EN","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":343},
{"name":"Beluga Sturgeon","page":"Beluga_(sturgeon)","group":"Fish","where":"The Caspian and Black Seas and the rivers that run into them","diet":"Fish","facts":["It's one of the largest bony fish in the world.","Its eggs are the costly beluga caviar.","It can live more than 100 years."],"lesson":"Value what's rare. The beluga is critically endangered because people value its eggs too much.","about":"The beluga, also known as the beluga sturgeon or great sturgeon (Huso huso), is a species of sturgeon, which are anadromous fish of the family Acipenseridae of the order Acipenseriformes. It is found primarily in the Caspian and Black Sea basins, and formerly in the Adriatic Sea. Based on maximum size, it is the third-most-massive living species of bony fish.","description":"Species of fish in the sturgeon family","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/%D0%91%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B0_%28Huso_huso%29.jpg/1920px-%D0%91%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B0_%28Huso_huso%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Максим Яковлєв","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B3%D0%B0_(Huso_huso).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beluga_(sturgeon)","sci":"Huso huso","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":344},
{"name":"Giant Oarfish","page":"Giant_oarfish","group":"Fish","where":"The deep oceans of the world","diet":"Krill and small animals","facts":["It's the longest bony fish in the world.","It may be behind old tales of sea serpents.","Some are called \"earthquake fish,\" though there's no proof they predict quakes."],"lesson":"Legends often hold a grain of truth. The oarfish is a real-life sea serpent.","about":"The giant oarfish (Regalecus glesne) is a species of oarfish of the family Regalecidae. It is an oceanodromous species with a worldwide distribution, excluding polar regions. Other common names include Pacific oarfish, king of herrings, and ribbonfish.","description":"Species of oceanic ray-finned fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Regalecus_glesne%2C_Naturhistorisches_Museum_Wien.jpg/1920px-Regalecus_glesne%2C_Naturhistorisches_Museum_Wien.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Sandstein","license":"CC BY 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regalecus_glesne,_Naturhistorisches_Museum_Wien.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_oarfish","sci":"Regalecus glesne","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":345},
{"name":"Hairy Frogfish","page":"Hairy_frogfish","group":"Fish","where":"Warm seas around the world, from the Indo-Pacific to the Caribbean","diet":"Fish","facts":["The \"hairs\" covering it are thin spines of skin that help it hide.","Its first back spine is shaped like a fishing rod, with a lure at the tip.","Its big mouth stretches to swallow prey as large as itself."],"lesson":"Be ready. The frogfish waits camouflaged, then swallows its prey whole.","about":"The striated frogfish or hairy frogfish (Antennarius striatus) is a species of marine ray-finned fish belonging to the family Antennariidae, the frogfishes. This species is found in the Indo-Pacific and eastern Atlantic Ocean.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Hairy_frogfish_%28Antennarius_striatus%29_%2816059517767%29.jpg/1920px-Hairy_frogfish_%28Antennarius_striatus%29_%2816059517767%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Rickard Zerpe","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hairy_frogfish_(Antennarius_striatus)_(16059517767).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Striated_frogfish","sci":"Antennarius striatus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":346},
{"name":"Mexican Tetra","page":"Mexican_tetra","group":"Fish","where":"Rivers and caves of Texas and northeastern Mexico","diet":"Insects and small animals","facts":["Some live in caves, and many of those have lost their eyes.","The cave fish are pale and pinkish.","Scientists study it to learn how eyes are lost in evolution."],"lesson":"Lose what you don't need. In the dark caves, the tetra traded its eyes for other senses.","about":"The Mexican tetra (Astyanax mexicanus), also known as the blind cave fish, blind cave characin or the blind cave tetra, is a species of freshwater ray-finned fish belonging to the family Acestrorhamphidae, the American characins. This fish is found in the lower Rio Grande, and the Nueces and Pecos Rivers in Texas, into the Central Plateau and eastern states of Mexico.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Mexican_Tetra_%28Astyanax_mexicanus%29_%282687270083%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Clinton & Charles Robertson from RAF Lakenheath, UK & San Marcos, TX, USA & UK","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mexican_Tetra_(Astyanax_mexicanus)_(2687270083).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_tetra","sci":"Astyanax mexicanus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":347},
{"name":"Clown Triggerfish","page":"Clown_triggerfish","group":"Fish","where":"Coral reefs of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Sea urchins, crabs and clams","facts":["It raises its first back spine to lock itself into a hiding place.","It has big white spots on its dark belly.","Its mouth is small but has strong teeth."],"lesson":"Lock in when you need to. The triggerfish wedges itself safe into the reef.","about":"The clown triggerfish (Balistoides conspicillum), also known as the bigspotted triggerfish, is a demersal marine fish.","description":"Species of fish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Balistoides_conspicillum_2607.jpg/1920px-Balistoides_conspicillum_2607.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Amada44","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balistoides_conspicillum_2607.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clown_triggerfish","sci":"Balistoides conspicillum","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":348},
{"name":"Atlas Moth","page":"Atlas_moth","group":"Insect","where":"Forests of South and Southeast Asia","diet":"Caterpillars eat leaves; adults don't eat at all","facts":["Its wings can span 25 to 30 cm (10 to 12 inches), among the largest of any moth.","Adults have no working mouth, so they can't eat, and live on fat stored as caterpillars.","Its Cantonese name means \"snake's head moth,\" for its snake-head wingtips."],"lesson":"Make your time count. The atlas moth lives only days as an adult, and spends them well.","about":"Attacus atlas, the Atlas moth, is a large saturniid moth endemic to the forests of Asia. The species was described by Carl Linnaeus in his 1758 10th edition of Systema Naturae. The Atlas moth is one of the largest lepidopterans, with a wingspan often measuring from 250mm to 300mm. and a wing surface area of about 160 cm2 (25 in2).","description":"Species of moth","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Attacus_atlas_qtl3.jpg/1920px-Attacus_atlas_qtl3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Quartl","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attacus_atlas_qtl3.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attacus_atlas","sci":"Attacus atlas","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":349},
{"name":"Luna Moth","page":"Luna_moth","group":"Insect","where":"Forests of eastern North America, including Florida","diet":"Caterpillars eat leaves of walnut, hickory and sweetgum","facts":["It's pale green, with long, sweeping tails on its back wings.","Its caterpillars are green too.","Its spinning tails may confuse the sonar of hunting bats."],"lesson":"Grace is its own defense. The luna moth's elegant tails help it slip away from bats.","about":"The luna moth (Actias luna), also called the American moon moth, is a Nearctic moth in the family Saturniidae, subfamily Saturniinae, a group commonly named the giant silk moths. The moth has lime-green wings and a white body. Its caterpillars are also green.","description":"Species of insect","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Actias_luna-male.jpg/1920px-Actias_luna-male.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"David notMD","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Actias_luna-male.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_moth","sci":"Actias luna","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":0.019,"n":350},
{"name":"Menelaus Blue Morpho","page":"Morpho_menelaus","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Juice of rotting fruit","facts":["Its shimmering blue comes from tiny structures in its wing scales.","The undersides of its wings are brown, with eyespots.","The eyespots may steer a predator's attack away from its body."],"lesson":"Show both sides. The morpho is bright blue on top and brown with eyespots underneath.","about":"The Menelaus blue morpho (Morpho menelaus) is one of 30 species of butterfly in the subfamily Morphinae. Its wingspan is about 12 cm (4.7 in), and its dorsal forewings and hindwings are a bright, iridescent blue with black edges, while the ventral surfaces are brown. Its iridescent wings are an area of interest in research due to their unique nanostructure.","description":"Species of butterfly","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Morpho_menelaus_huebneri_MHNT_Male_Dos.jpg/1920px-Morpho_menelaus_huebneri_MHNT_Male_Dos.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Didier Descouens","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morpho_menelaus_huebneri_MHNT_Male_Dos.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morpho_menelaus","sci":"Morpho menelaus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":351},
{"name":"Goliath Beetle","page":"Goliathus_goliatus","group":"Insect","where":"Equatorial Africa, from Nigeria east to Uganda and western Kenya","diet":"Tree sap and fruit","facts":["It's one of the largest insects in the world.","Males can be up to 11 cm (4.3 inches) long.","Males use a Y-shaped horn as a pry bar in fights over food and mates."],"lesson":"Use your strength wisely. The Goliath beetle's horn is for prying rivals away, not for killing.","about":"Goliathus goliatus is a very large species of beetle of the family Scarabaeidae, native to tropical Africa.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Goliathus_goliatus_vol.jpg/1920px-Goliathus_goliatus_vol.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Didier Descouens","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goliathus_goliatus_vol.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goliathus_goliatus","sci":"Goliathus goliatus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":352},
{"name":"Hercules Beetle","page":"Hercules_beetle","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America and the Lesser Antilles","diet":"Rotting fruit","facts":["It's one of the longest beetles in the world, counting the male's horn.","Only the males have the huge horns.","Its wing covers change color with the humidity."],"lesson":"Strength is for protecting what matters. The Hercules beetle uses its horns to grab, lift and throw rivals in fights over mates.","about":"The Hercules beetle (Dynastes hercules) is a species of rhinoceros beetle native to the tropical forests of southern Mexico, Central America, South America, and the Lesser Antilles. It is the longest extant species of beetle in the world, and is also one of the largest flying insects in the world.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Dynastes_hercules_ecuatorianus_MHNT.jpg/1920px-Dynastes_hercules_ecuatorianus_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Didier Descouens","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynastes_hercules_ecuatorianus_MHNT.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hercules_beetle","sci":"Dynastes hercules","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":353},
{"name":"Sacred Scarab","page":"Scarabaeus_sacer","group":"Insect","where":"Coasts of southern Europe, North Africa and West Asia, near the Mediterranean and Black Sea","diet":"Dung","facts":["It rolls dung into balls and pushes them away to bury.","Ancient Egyptians saw the god Khepri in it, rolling the sun across the sky.","It lays a single egg with a ball of dung, and the young feed on it after hatching."],"lesson":"Turn waste into worth. The scarab rolls dung into the start of a new life.","about":"Scarabaeus sacer, common name sacred scarab, is the type species of the genus Scarabaeus and the family Scarabaeidae. This dung beetle is native to southern Europe, North Africa, West Asia, and was venerated in ancient Egypt.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Scarabaeus.sacer.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Sarefo","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scarabaeus.sacer.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarabaeus_sacer","sci":"Scarabaeus sacer","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":354},
{"name":"Orchid Mantis","page":"Hymenopus_coronatus","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Southeast Asia","diet":"Insects","facts":["It looks like an orchid flower.","It hunts the pollinating insects that come to visit the \"flower.\"","Males can be less than half the size of females."],"lesson":"Be the flower. The orchid mantis waits, and its prey comes to it.","about":"Hymenopus coronatus is a mantis from the tropical forests of Southeast Asia. It is known by various common names, including walking flower mantis, orchid-blossom mantis and (pink) orchid mantis. It is one of several species known as flower mantises, a reference to their unique physical form and behaviour, which often involves moving with a \"swaying\" motion, as if being \"blown\" in the breeze.","description":"Species of praying mantis","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Mantis_Hymenopus_coronatus_6_Luc_Viatour.jpg/1920px-Mantis_Hymenopus_coronatus_6_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Luc Viatour","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mantis_Hymenopus_coronatus_6_Luc_Viatour.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hymenopus_coronatus","sci":"Hymenopus coronatus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":355},
{"name":"Atta Leafcutter Ant","page":"Atta_cephalotes","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"A fungus it grows on cut leaves","facts":["It carries cut leaves home to grow a fungus garden, which it eats.","A colony can have up to 5 million ants, with one queen that can live over 20 years.","Workers come in different sizes, called castes, each with its own job."],"lesson":"Every role matters. Leafcutter colonies thrive because each ant does its part.","about":"Atta cephalotes, commonly known as the hairy-headed leafcutter ant, is a species of leafcutter ant in the tribe Attini (fungus-growing ants). A colony can contain up to 5 million members, and each colony has a single queen that can live more than 20 years. The colony comprises different castes, known as \"task partitioning\", and each caste has a different job to do.","description":"Species of ant","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Atta_cephalotes-pjt.jpg/1920px-Atta_cephalotes-pjt.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Pjt56","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atta_cephalotes-pjt.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atta_cephalotes","sci":"Atta cephalotes","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":356},
{"name":"Bullet Ant","page":"Paraponera_clavata","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Nectar and small insects","facts":["Its sting is said to be the most painful of any insect.","In Venezuela it's called the \"24-hour ant,\" for how long the pain lasts.","The Sateré-Mawé people of Brazil wear gloves woven with bullet ants in their initiation rites."],"lesson":"Courage isn't the absence of pain. Young Sateré-Mawé men prove it by facing the bullet ant.","about":"Paraponera clavata, commonly known as the bullet ant, is a species of ant known for its extremely painful sting. It inhabits humid lowland rainforests in Central and South America.","description":"Species of ant","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Paraponera_clavata.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Hans Hillewaert","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paraponera_clavata.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraponera_clavata","sci":"Paraponera clavata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":357},
{"name":"Burchell's Army Ant","page":"Eciton_burchellii","group":"Insect","where":"Rainforests of Central and South America","diet":"Insects and other small animals","facts":["It has no fixed nest: the ants link their bodies into a living shelter called a bivouac.","Its huge, organized swarm raids sweep the forest floor.","Birds called antbirds follow the raids to catch fleeing insects."],"lesson":"Together, you can build anything. Army ants make their home out of themselves.","about":"Eciton burchellii is a species of New World army ant in the genus Eciton. This species performs expansive, organized swarm raids that give it the informal name, Eciton army ant. This species displays a high degree of worker polymorphism.","description":"Species of ant","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Eciton_burchellii_%2814336577830%29.jpg/1920px-Eciton_burchellii_%2814336577830%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Graham Wise from Brisbane, Australia","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eciton_burchellii_(14336577830).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eciton_burchellii","sci":"Eciton burchellii","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":358},
{"name":"Seven-spot Ladybird","page":"Coccinella_septempunctata","group":"Insect","where":"Gardens and fields of Europe and Asia, and brought to North America","diet":"Aphids","facts":["It was brought to North America to fight aphids on crops.","Its genus name, Coccinella, comes from the Latin for \"scarlet.\"","It's been called one of the most successful aphid-eating insects."],"lesson":"Small helpers make a big difference. The ladybird protects gardens one aphid at a time.","about":"Coccinella septempunctata, commonly known as the seven-spot ladybird (in North America, seven-spotted ladybug, seven-spotted lady beetle), often abbreviated C-7, is a carnivorous beetle native to Europe, most of Asia, and North Africa. It inhabits many regions with a temperate climate.","description":"Species of beetle","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/7-Spotted-Ladybug-Coccinella-septempunctata-sq1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Photographer: Dominik Stodulski Graphic Processing: User:MathKnight","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:7-Spotted-Ladybug-Coccinella-septempunctata-sq1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coccinella_septempunctata","sci":"Coccinella septempunctata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":359},
{"name":"Globe Skimmer","page":"Pantala_flavescens","group":"Insect","where":"Tropical and subtropical regions on every continent except Antarctica","diet":"Small flying insects","facts":["It's the most widespread dragonfly on Earth.","Its migration of about 18,000 km (11,000 miles) takes several generations, one of the longest of any insect.","It lives on every continent except Antarctica."],"lesson":"Go far, together. The globe skimmer's journey takes generations to finish.","about":"Pantala flavescens, the globe skimmer, globe wanderer or wandering glider, is a wide-ranging dragonfly of the family Libellulidae. This species and Pantala hymenaea, the \"spot-winged glider\", are the only members of the genus Pantala. It was first described by Johan Christian Fabricius in 1798.","description":"Species of dragonfly","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Pantala_flavescens-Kadavoor-2017-05-04-002.jpg/1920px-Pantala_flavescens-Kadavoor-2017-05-04-002.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jeevan Jose, Kerala, India","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pantala_flavescens-Kadavoor-2017-05-04-002.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantala_flavescens","sci":"Pantala flavescens","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":360},
{"name":"European Mantis","page":"Mantis_religiosa","group":"Insect","where":"Europe, Africa and Asia, and brought to North America","diet":"Insects","facts":["The female sometimes eats the male during or after mating.","Its triangular head is very mobile, with big eyes facing forward.","It's the state insect of Connecticut."],"lesson":"Stillness and focus win. The mantis waits perfectly still for the right moment.","about":"The European mantis (Mantis religiosa) is a large hemimetabolic insect in the family Mantidae, which is the largest family of the order Mantodea (mantises). Their common name praying mantis is derived from the distinctive posture of the first pair of legs, which can be observed when the mantis is in repose, resembling a praying position. Both males and females have elongated bodies with two pairs of wings.","description":"Species of praying mantis","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/European_praying_mantis_%28Mantis_religiosa%29_green_female_Dobruja.jpg/1920px-European_praying_mantis_%28Mantis_religiosa%29_green_female_Dobruja.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_praying_mantis_(Mantis_religiosa)_green_female_Dobruja.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_mantis","sci":"Mantis religiosa","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":361},
{"name":"Giant Prickly Stick Insect","page":"Extatosoma_tiaratum","group":"Insect","where":"Forests of Queensland and New South Wales, Australia","diet":"Eucalyptus leaves","facts":["When there are no males around, females lay eggs that hatch anyway.","Ants carry its eggs home to eat a fatty part, and leave the rest to hatch.","When threatened, it raises its tail in a scorpion pose."],"lesson":"Look tougher than you are. The stick insect's scorpion pose scares off bigger animals.","about":"Extatosoma tiaratum, commonly known as the spiny leaf insect, the Australian giant prickly stick insect, Macleay's spectre, or the Australian walking stick, is a large species of Australian stick insect.","description":"Species of stick insect native to Australia","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Australian_Walking_Stick.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Rosa Pineda","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_Walking_Stick.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extatosoma_tiaratum","sci":"giant stick insect","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":362},
{"name":"Seventeen-year Cicada","page":"Magicicada_septendecim","group":"Insect","where":"Forests of the eastern United States","diet":"Sap from tree roots","facts":["It lives about 17 years, nearly all of it underground as a young nymph.","Huge numbers come out of the ground in the same year.","The male's song sounds like someone calling \"weeee-whoa\" or \"Pharaoh.\""],"lesson":"Good things come to those who wait. The cicada spends 17 years underground before its moment.","about":"Magicicada septendecim, sometimes called the Pharaoh cicada or the 17-year locust, is native to Canada and the United States and is the largest and most northern species of periodical cicada with a 17-year lifecycle.","description":"Species of periodical cicada","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/Magicicada_septendecim-female_ventral.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"peterwchen","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magicicada_septendecim-female_ventral.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magicicada_septendecim","sci":"Magicicada septendecim","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":363},
{"name":"Zebra Longwing","page":"Zebra_longwing","group":"Insect","where":"Florida, Texas, Central and South America","diet":"Nectar and pollen","facts":["It's Florida's state butterfly.","Groups of up to 60 roost together at night, for safety.","In warm months, some fly north into other states."],"lesson":"Find your people. Zebra longwings gather every night at the same roost.","about":"Heliconius charithonia, the zebra longwing or zebra heliconian, is a species of butterfly belonging to the subfamily Heliconiinae of the family Nymphalidae. It was first described by Carl Linnaeus in his 1767 12th edition of Systema Naturae. The boldly striped black and white wing pattern is aposematic, warning off predators.","description":"Species of butterfly","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Heliconius_charithonia_2021.jpg/1920px-Heliconius_charithonia_2021.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Nosferattus","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heliconius_charithonia_2021.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliconius_charithonia","sci":"Heliconius charithonia","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":364},
{"name":"Northern Giant Hornet","page":"Vespa_mandarinia","group":"Insect","where":"East, South and Southeast Asia, including Japan and Korea","diet":"Insects, especially honey bees","facts":["It's the largest hornet in the world.","A handful of them can wipe out a colony of European honey bees.","Japanese honey bees fight back by swarming a hornet and heating it to death with their bodies."],"lesson":"Small can beat big together. Japanese honey bees defeat giant hornets by working as one.","about":"The Asian giant hornet (Vespa mandarinia), also known as the northern giant hornet, is the world's largest hornet. It is native to temperate and tropical East Asia, South Asia, mainland Southeast Asia, and parts of the Russian Far East. It was also briefly found in the Pacific Northwest of North America from late 2019 but was eradicated by December 2024.","description":"Predatory hornet, largest in the world","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f5/Vespa_mandarinia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Gary Alpert","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vespa_mandarinia.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asian_giant_hornet","sci":"Vespa mandarinia","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":365},
{"name":"Common Eastern Bumble Bee","page":"Bombus_impatiens","group":"Insect","where":"Eastern North America","diet":"Nectar and pollen","facts":["It's used to pollinate tomatoes, blueberries, raspberries and pumpkins.","It does well in suburbs and cities, and greenhouses buy colonies to pollinate crops.","Most bees in the colony eat honey made from the nectar and pollen they gather."],"lesson":"Do your part. The bumble bee helps grow our tomatoes, blueberries and pumpkins.","about":"Bombus impatiens, the common eastern bumblebee, is the most commonly encountered bumblebee across much of eastern North America. They can be found in the Eastern temperate forest region of the eastern United States, southern Canada, and the eastern Great Plains. They are adapted to suburban and urban environments, and as a pollinator species in commercial use by the greenhouse industry.","description":"Species of insect","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Bombus_impatiens%2C_f%2C_queen%2C_charles_co_%2818239590966%29.jpg/1920px-Bombus_impatiens%2C_f%2C_queen%2C_charles_co_%2818239590966%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"USGS Bee Inventory and Monitoring Lab from Beltsville, Maryland, USA","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bombus_impatiens,_f,_queen,_charles_co_(18239590966).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombus_impatiens","sci":"Bombus impatiens","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":366},
{"name":"Goliath Birdeater","page":"Theraphosa_blondi","group":"Arachnid","where":"Rainforests of northern South America","diet":"Insects, worms, frogs and small animals","facts":["It's the largest spider in the world by weight, up to 175 grams (6 oz).","Despite its name, it rarely eats birds.","When threatened, it rubs its belly and sheds hairs that badly irritate skin."],"lesson":"Don't believe the name. The Goliath birdeater mostly eats insects and worms.","about":"The Goliath birdeater (Theraphosa blondi) is a large spider that belongs to the tarantula family Theraphosidae. Found in northern South America, it is the largest spider in the world by mass (175 g (6.2 oz)) and body length (up to 13 cm (5.1 in)), and second to the giant huntsman spider by leg span.","description":"Species of tarantula from South America","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Theraphosa_blondi_MHNT.jpg/1920px-Theraphosa_blondi_MHNT.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Didier Descouens","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theraphosa_blondi_MHNT.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goliath_birdeater","sci":"Theraphosa blondi","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":367},
{"name":"Peacock Spider","page":"Maratus_volans","group":"Arachnid","where":"Parts of eastern Australia, Tasmania and Western Australia","diet":"Small insects","facts":["The male raises a colorful flap on his back and dances for the female.","Males and females are only about 5 mm (a fifth of an inch) long.","Females are brown, without the male's bright colors."],"lesson":"Put your heart into it. The male peacock spider dances his best to win a mate.","about":"Maratus volans is a species in the jumping spider family (Salticidae), belonging to the genus Maratus (peacock spiders). These spiders are native to certain areas in Australia and occupy a wide distribution of habitats. They have a specialized visual system that allows them to see the full visible spectrum as well as in the ultraviolet-range; this helps them detect and pursue prey.","description":"Species of spider","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/MalePeacockSpider.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jürgen Otto","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MalePeacockSpider.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maratus_volans","sci":"Maratus volans","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":368},
{"name":"Golden Silk Orb-weaver","page":"Trichonephila_clavipes","group":"Arachnid","where":"Warm parts of the Americas, including Florida","diet":"Flying insects","facts":["It's known for the golden color of its silk.","It builds big, lopsided round webs between trees and shrubs.","It spins several kinds of silk, some strong and some flexible."],"lesson":"Build strong. The golden silk orb-weaver keeps repairing its big web whenever bugs, birds or debris damage it.","about":"Trichonephila clavipes (formerly known as Nephila clavipes), commonly known as the golden silk orb-weaver, golden silk spider, golden orb weaver spider or colloquially banana spider (a name shared with several others), is an orb-weaving spider species which inhabits forests and wooded areas ranging from the southern US to Argentina. It is indigenous to both continental North and South America.","description":"Species of spider native to the Americas","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Golden_silk_orb-weaver_spider_%28Nephila_clavipes%29_female.jpg/1920px-Golden_silk_orb-weaver_spider_%28Nephila_clavipes%29_female.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_silk_orb-weaver_spider_(Nephila_clavipes)_female.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trichonephila_clavipes","sci":"Trichonephila clavipes","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":369},
{"name":"Southern Black Widow","page":"Latrodectus_mactans","group":"Arachnid","where":"The southeastern US, including Florida","diet":"Insects","facts":["The female has a red hourglass on her belly.","The female may eat the male after mating.","Its venom can be painful, but it's rarely deadly to healthy people."],"lesson":"Respect, don't panic. The black widow is shy and bites only when pressed.","about":"Latrodectus mactans, known as southern black widow or simply black widow, and the shoe-button spider, is a venomous species of spider in the genus Latrodectus. The females are well known for their distinctive black and red coloring and because they occasionally eat their mates after reproduction. The species is native to North America.","description":"Species of spider","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Latrodectus_mactans_eating.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latrodectus_mactans_eating.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latrodectus_mactans","sci":"Latrodectus mactans","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":370},
{"name":"Emperor Scorpion","page":"Pandinus_imperator","group":"Arachnid","where":"Rainforests and savannas of West Africa","diet":"Insects and small animals","facts":["It's one of the largest scorpions in the world.","Like all scorpions, it glows blue-green under ultraviolet light.","Its sting is mild, about like a bee's."],"lesson":"Big isn't always dangerous. The huge emperor scorpion has a mild sting.","about":"The emperor scorpion (Pandinus imperator) is a species of scorpion native to rainforests and savannas in West Africa. It is one of the largest scorpions in the world and lives for six to eight years. Its body is black, but like other scorpions it glows pastel green or blue under ultraviolet light.","description":"Species of scorpion","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Female_Emperor_Scorpion.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Rosa Pineda","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_Emperor_Scorpion.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_scorpion","sci":"Pandinus imperator","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":371},
{"name":"Regal Jumping Spider","page":"Phidippus_regius","group":"Arachnid","where":"The southeastern US, including Florida, and the Caribbean","diet":"Insects","facts":["It's the largest jumping spider in eastern North America.","Males are black with white markings; females come in browns, oranges and grays.","It belongs to the jumping spiders, a family famous for sharp eyesight."],"lesson":"Look before you leap. The jumping spider sizes up its target carefully, then jumps.","about":"Phidippus regius, commonly known as the regal jumper, is a species of jumping spider (family Salticidae) found in parts of the United States and the Caribbean. It is the largest species of jumping spider in eastern North America.","description":"Species of jumping spider in the genus ''Phidippus''","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Female_Jumping_Spider_-_Phidippus_regius_-_Florida.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Thomas Shahan","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_Jumping_Spider_-_Phidippus_regius_-_Florida.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phidippus_regius","sci":"Phidippus regius","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":372},
{"name":"Diving Bell Spider","page":"Argyroneta_aquatica","group":"Arachnid","where":"Ponds and slow streams of Europe and northern Asia","diet":"Water insects","facts":["It's the only spider known to live almost entirely underwater.","It builds a bubble of air, a \"diving bell,\" held in a silk web.","It surfaces only briefly to restock its air."],"lesson":"Bring what you need. The diving bell spider carries its air into a world without it.","about":"The diving bell spider or water spider (Argyroneta aquatica) is the only species of spider known to live almost entirely under water. It is the only member of the genus Argyroneta and the type genus of the family Argyronetidae. When out of the water, the spider ranges in colour from mid to dark brown, although the hairs on the abdomen give it a dark grey, velvet-like appearance.","description":"Genus of spiders","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Argyroneta_aquatica_Paar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Norbert Schuller Baupi","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argyroneta_aquatica_Paar.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diving_bell_spider","sci":"Argyroneta aquatica","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":373},
{"name":"Coconut Crab","page":"Coconut_crab","group":"Crustacean","where":"Islands of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Fruit, nuts, seeds and carrion","facts":["It's the largest arthropod that lives on land, up to 4.1 kg (9 lb).","It can crack open coconuts with its claws.","It's also called the robber crab or palm thief."],"lesson":"Be resourceful. The coconut crab gets into food that few animals can.","about":"The coconut crab (Birgus latro) is a terrestrial species of giant hermit crab, and is also known as the robber crab or palm thief. It is the largest living terrestrial arthropod, with a weight up to 4.1 kg (9.0 lb). The distance from the tip of one leg to the tip of another can be as wide as 90 cm (35 in).","description":"Species of crustacean","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Coconut_Crab_Birgus_latro.jpg/1920px-Coconut_Crab_Birgus_latro.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Drew Avery","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coconut_Crab_Birgus_latro.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coconut_crab","sci":"Birgus latro","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":374},
{"name":"Japanese Spider Crab","page":"Japanese_spider_crab","group":"Crustacean","where":"Deep waters around Japan","diet":"Carrion, shellfish and plants","facts":["Its legs span about 3.75 meters (12 feet), the widest of any arthropod.","It decorates its shell with sponges and other animals to hide.","It's named after Engelbert Kaempfer, a German naturalist who lived in Japan in the 1690s."],"lesson":"Dress for where you are. The spider crab covers its shell in sponges to blend in.","about":"The Japanese giant spider crab (Macrocheira kaempferi) is a species of marine crab and is the largest crab found in the waters around Japan. At around 3.75 metres (12.3 ft), it has the largest leg-span of any arthropod. The Japanese name for this species is taka-ashi-gani, (Japanese: 高脚蟹; タカアシガニ), literally translating to \"tall-legged crab\".","description":"Species of crab","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Macrocheira_kaempferi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Lycaon (Hans Hillewaert)","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macrocheira_kaempferi.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_spider_crab","sci":"Macrocheira kaempferi","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":375},
{"name":"Bigclaw Snapping Shrimp","page":"Alpheus_heterochaelis","group":"Crustacean","where":"Shallow waters of the western Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Small worms, crabs and fish","facts":["Its big snapping claw is half as long as its whole body.","It's a pistol shrimp, named for the gunshot-like snap of its claw.","It's the largest snapping shrimp where it lives, up to 5.5 cm (2 inches)."],"lesson":"Small but heard. The snapping shrimp makes itself known with one big claw.","about":"Alpheus heterochaelis, the bigclaw snapping shrimp, is a snapper or pistol shrimp in the family Alpheidae. It is found in the western Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico.","description":"Species of crustacean","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Alpheus_heterochaelis.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"The original uploader was Hkchan123 at Chinese Wikipedia.","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpheus_heterochaelis.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpheus_heterochaelis","sci":"Alpheus heterochaelis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":376},
{"name":"Caribbean Spiny Lobster","page":"Caribbean_spiny_lobster","group":"Crustacean","where":"Reefs of the western Atlantic, including Florida","diet":"Snails, clams, crabs and sea urchins","facts":["Its antennae are longer than its body and covered in spines.","Sometimes hundreds line up and march across the seafloor off Florida and the Bahamas.","It lives on reefs and in mangrove swamps."],"lesson":"Follow the line. In the fall, spiny lobsters sometimes line up by the hundreds and march across the seafloor together.","about":"Panulirus argus, the Caribbean spiny lobster, is a species of spiny lobster that lives on reefs and in mangrove swamps in the western Atlantic Ocean.","description":"Species of crustacean","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Langosta_com%C3%BAn_del_Caribe_%28Panulirus_argus%29%2C_Cozumel%2C_M%C3%A9xico%2C_2025-12-20%2C_DD_70.jpg/1920px-Langosta_com%C3%BAn_del_Caribe_%28Panulirus_argus%29%2C_Cozumel%2C_M%C3%A9xico%2C_2025-12-20%2C_DD_70.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Diego Delso","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langosta_com%C3%BAn_del_Caribe_(Panulirus_argus),_Cozumel,_M%C3%A9xico,_2025-12-20,_DD_70.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panulirus_argus","sci":"Panulirus argus","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":377},
{"name":"Atlantic Sand Fiddler Crab","page":"Leptuca_pugilator","group":"Crustacean","where":"Sandy and muddy shores from Massachusetts to Florida and along the Gulf of Mexico to Texas","diet":"Tiny bits of food sifted from the sand","facts":["The male has one huge claw.","That claw can be longer than the crab's whole body.","It lives in burrows on muddy and sandy flats, sometimes in huge numbers."],"lesson":"Wave your flag. The fiddler crab's big claw says \"here I am.\"","about":"Leptuca pugilator, the sand fiddler crab, Atlantic sand fiddler crab, or Calico fiddler, is a species of fiddler crab that is found from Massachusetts to the Gulf of Mexico. It lives in burrows in coastal and estuarine mud-flats, and can be extremely abundant. It can be differentiated from the morphologically similar Minuca pugnax and Minuca minax by the smoothness of the inside of its claws.","description":"Species of crab","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Uca_pugilator_and_hole_crop.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ianaré Sévi","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uca_pugilator_and_hole_crop.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leptuca_pugilator","sci":"Leptuca pugilator","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":378},
{"name":"Christmas Island Red Crab","page":"Christmas_Island_red_crab","group":"Crustacean","where":"Christmas Island, in the Indian Ocean","diet":"Leaves, fruit and flowers","facts":["Millions march to the sea at the same time each year to spawn.","The island closes roads and sets up detours for them.","An invasive ant, the yellow crazy ant, has killed about a third of them."],"lesson":"Make room for each other. Christmas Island closes its roads so the crabs can pass.","about":"The Christmas Island red crab (Gecarcoidea natalis) is a species of land crab that is endemic to Christmas Island and Cocos (Keeling) Islands in the Indian Ocean. Although restricted to a relatively small area, an estimated 43.7 million adult red crabs once lived on Christmas Island alone, but the accidental introduction of the yellow crazy ant is believed to have reduced the population by about a third.","description":"Species of crustacean (Gecarcoidea natalis)","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Christmas_Island_%285774532171%29.jpg/1920px-Christmas_Island_%285774532171%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"DIAC images","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christmas_Island_(5774532171).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_Island_red_crab","sci":"Gecarcoidea natalis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":0.12,"life":null,"n":379},
{"name":"Antarctic Krill","page":"Antarctic_krill","group":"Crustacean","where":"The Southern Ocean around Antarctica","diet":"Phytoplankton","facts":["It's one of the most abundant animals on Earth, about 500 million tonnes in all.","Its swarms can pack 10,000 to 30,000 krill into a cubic meter.","Whales, seals, penguins and fish all depend on it."],"lesson":"Little things hold up the world. Tiny krill feed almost everything in the Antarctic.","about":"Antarctic krill (Euphausia superba) is a species of krill found in the Antarctic waters of the Southern Ocean. It is a small, swimming crustacean that lives in large schools, called swarms, sometimes reaching densities of 10,000–30,000 animals per cubic metre.","description":"Species of krill","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Antarctic_krill_%28Euphausia_superba%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Krill666.jpg: Uwe Kils I am willing to give the image in 1700 resolution to Wiki","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antarctic_krill_(Euphausia_superba).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antarctic_krill","sci":"Euphausia superba","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":380},
{"name":"Atlantic Horseshoe Crab","page":"Atlantic_horseshoe_crab","group":"Arthropod","where":"Coasts of the Atlantic and Gulf of Mexico, including Florida","diet":"Worms, clams and small animals","facts":["It isn't a true crab: it's more closely related to spiders.","Its blood is used to make a test that checks medicines for bacterial toxins.","Its blood cells clot when they touch bacterial toxins, which is what the test uses."],"lesson":"Old ways can still help. The horseshoe crab's ancient blood keeps our medicines safe.","about":"The Atlantic horseshoe crab (Limulus polyphemus), also known as the American horseshoe crab, is a species of horseshoe crab, a kind of marine and brackish chelicerate arthropod. It is found in the Gulf of Mexico and along the Atlantic coast of North America. The main area of annual migration is Delaware Bay along the South Jersey Delaware Bayshore.","description":"Species of arthropod","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Limulus_polyphemus_%28aq.%29.jpg/1920px-Limulus_polyphemus_%28aq.%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Hans Hillewaert","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limulus_polyphemus_(aq.).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_horseshoe_crab","sci":"Limulus polyphemus","status":"VU","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":381},
{"name":"Giant Pacific Octopus","page":"Giant_Pacific_octopus","group":"Mollusc","where":"The North Pacific, from California to Japan","diet":"Crabs, clams, shrimp and fish","facts":["It's the largest octopus in the world.","One fisherman reported catching one of 270 kg (600 lb), 9.8 meters (32 feet) across.","It's known for its intelligence, and is studied in aquariums and labs."],"lesson":"Keep learning. The giant Pacific octopus is a quick, curious problem-solver.","about":"The giant Pacific octopus (Enteroctopus dofleini), also known as the North Pacific giant octopus, is a large marine cephalopod belonging to the genus Enteroctopus and Enteroctopodidae family.","description":"Species of cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Enteroctopus_dolfeini.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"NOAA/R. N. Lea [1]","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enteroctopus_dolfeini.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_Pacific_octopus","sci":"Enteroctopus dofleini","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":382},
{"name":"Mimic Octopus","page":"Mimic_octopus","group":"Mollusc","where":"Sandy seafloor of Indonesia and the tropical Indo-Pacific","diet":"Fish, crabs and worms","facts":["It impersonates other animals, like lionfish, sea snakes and flatfish.","It was discovered off Sulawesi, Indonesia, in 1998.","It can switch between disguises of up to 18 different sea animals."],"lesson":"Be adaptable. The mimic octopus becomes whatever the moment needs.","about":"The mimic octopus (Thaumoctopus mimicus) is a species of octopus from the Indo-Pacific region. Like other octopuses, it uses its chromatophores to disguise itself. It is noteworthy for being able to impersonate a wide variety of other marine animals.","description":"Indo-Pacific species of octopus","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Mimic_Octopus_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Steve Childs","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mimic_Octopus_2.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mimic_octopus","sci":"Thaumoctopus mimicus","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":383},
{"name":"Greater Blue-ringed Octopus","page":"Greater_blue-ringed_octopus","group":"Mollusc","where":"Reefs and tide pools of the Indian and western Pacific Oceans","diet":"Crabs and shrimp","facts":["It's one of four kinds of extremely venomous blue-ringed octopuses.","Its blue rings flash bright when it's alarmed.","It's no more than 10 cm (4 inches) across, arms included."],"lesson":"Size doesn't measure power. The little blue-ringed octopus is one of the most dangerous animals in the sea.","about":"The greater blue-ringed octopus (Hapalochlaena lunulata) is one of four species of extremely venomous blue-ringed octopuses belonging to the family Octopodidae. This particular species of blue-ringed octopus is known as one of the most toxic marine animals in the world.","description":"Species of venomous cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Hapalochlaena_lunulata2.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jens Petersen","license":"CC BY 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hapalochlaena_lunulata2.JPG"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_blue-ringed_octopus","sci":"Hapalochlaena lunulata","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":384},
{"name":"Chambered Nautilus","page":"Chambered_nautilus","group":"Mollusc","where":"Deep reef slopes of the Indian and western Pacific Oceans","diet":"Carrion, shellfish and crabs","facts":["It floats by changing the gas and liquid in the chambers of its shell.","Its eyes have no lens, and work like a pinhole camera.","It lives near reefs and on the seafloor off Australia, Japan and Micronesia."],"lesson":"Add to yourself, chamber by chamber. The nautilus grows its shell one room at a time.","about":"The chambered nautilus (Nautilus pompilius), also called the pearly nautilus, is the best-known species of nautilus. The shell, when cut away, reveals a lining of lustrous nacre and displays a nearly perfect equiangular spiral, although it is not a golden spiral. The shell exhibits countershading, being light on the bottom and dark on top.","description":"Species of nautilus","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Nautilus_pompilius_%28detail%29.jpg/1920px-Nautilus_pompilius_%28detail%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Hans Hillewaert","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nautilus_pompilius_(detail).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chambered_nautilus","sci":"Nautilus pompilius","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":385},
{"name":"Humboldt Squid","page":"Humboldt_squid","group":"Mollusc","where":"The eastern Pacific, from California to Chile","diet":"Fish, crustaceans and other squid","facts":["It flashes red and white, earning the nickname \"red devil.\"","Its two stretchy tentacles lash out fast to grab prey.","It lives only 1 to 2 years."],"lesson":"Live fully. The Humboldt squid packs a lot of life into a short time.","about":"The Humboldt squid (Dosidicus gigas), also known as the red devil, jumbo squid, or jumbo flying squid, is a large, predatory squid living in the eastern Pacific Ocean. It is the only known species of the genus Dosidicus of the subfamily Ommastrephinae, family Ommastrephidae.","description":"Species of cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Dosidicus_gigas.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Image courtesy of NOAA/MBARI 2006","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dosidicus_gigas.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humboldt_squid","sci":"Dosidicus gigas","status":"DD","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":386},
{"name":"Colossal Squid","page":"Colossal_squid","group":"Mollusc","where":"The deep Southern Ocean around Antarctica","diet":"Fish","facts":["It's the heaviest invertebrate in the world.","It has the largest eyes of any known animal.","Its arms have swiveling hooks as well as suckers."],"lesson":"Some wonders stay hidden. The colossal squid lives so deep that it's almost never seen alive.","about":"The colossal squid (Mesonychoteuthis hamiltoni) is a species of very large squid belonging to the family Cranchiidae, that of the cockatoo squids, or glass squids. It is sometimes called the Antarctic cranch squid or giant squid (not to be confused with the giant squid in genus Architeuthis) and is believed to be the largest squid species in terms of mass.","description":"Species of squid","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Colossal_squid_in_Te_Papa.jpg/1920px-Colossal_squid_in_Te_Papa.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Scotted400","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colossal_squid_in_Te_Papa.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colossal_squid","sci":"Mesonychoteuthis hamiltoni","status":"LC","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":387},
{"name":"Vampire Squid","page":"Vampire_squid","group":"Mollusc","where":"The deep ocean around the world","diet":"\"Marine snow,\" bits of falling debris","facts":["It isn't a squid or an octopus, but the only survivor of its own order.","It eats \"marine snow\" that drifts down from above.","Its name comes from its dark color and cloak-like webbing; it eats debris, not blood."],"lesson":"Gentle despite the name. The vampire squid lives on scraps and harms no one.","about":"The vampire squid (Vampyroteuthis infernalis, lit. 'vampire squid from hell') is a small cephalopod found throughout temperate and tropical oceans in extreme deep sea conditions. The vampire squid uses its bioluminescent organs and its unique oxygen metabolism to thrive in the parts of the ocean with the lowest concentrations of oxygen.","description":"Species of cephalopod","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/MBNMS_juvenile_vampire_squid_%2849041024167%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"National Marine Sanctuaries","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MBNMS_juvenile_vampire_squid_(49041024167).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vampire_squid","sci":"Vampyroteuthis infernalis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":388},
{"name":"Giant Clam","page":"Tridacna_gigas","group":"Mollusc","where":"Coral reefs of the Indian and western Pacific Oceans","diet":"Food made by algae living in its body, plus plankton","facts":["It's the largest clam in the world, over 200 kg (440 lb).","Algae living in its fleshy mantle make food from sunlight for it.","It can live for more than 100 years."],"lesson":"Partners make you stronger. The giant clam lives on what its algae partners make.","about":"Tridacna gigas, the giant clam, is the best-known species of the giant clam genus Tridacna. Giant clams are the largest living bivalve molluscs. Several other species of \"giant clam\" in the genus Tridacna are often misidentified as Tridacna gigas.","description":"Species of bivalve","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Giant_clam_%28Tridacna_gigas%29_Michaelmas_Cay.jpg/1920px-Giant_clam_%28Tridacna_gigas%29_Michaelmas_Cay.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Charles J. Sharp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giant_clam_(Tridacna_gigas)_Michaelmas_Cay.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant_clam","sci":"Tridacna gigas","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":389},
{"name":"Queen Conch","page":"Queen_conch","group":"Mollusc","where":"Seagrass beds of the Caribbean and Florida","diet":"Algae","facts":["Its big shell has a glossy pink lip.","In Florida, young conchs are called \"rollers,\" because waves roll their shells around.","It's also called the pink conch, and caracol rosa in Mexico."],"lesson":"Grow solid. Young conchs get tossed by the waves; grown ones are too heavy to budge.","about":"Aliger gigas, originally known as Strombus gigas or more recently as Lobatus gigas, commonly known as the queen conch, is a species of large sea snail, a marine gastropod mollusc in the family of true conches, the Strombidae. This species is one of the largest molluscs native to the Caribbean Sea, and tropical northwestern Atlantic, reaching up to 35.2 centimetres (13.9 in) in shell length.","description":"Species of mollusc","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Researcher_holding_juvenile_queen_conch.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Jennifer Doerr, NOAA SEFSC Galveston","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Researcher_holding_juvenile_queen_conch.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aliger_gigas","sci":"Lobatus gigas","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":390},
{"name":"Blue Dragon","page":"Glaucus_atlanticus","group":"Mollusc","where":"Floating on the open ocean around the world","diet":"Portuguese man o' war and other floating stingers","facts":["It floats upside down, held up by the water's surface tension and a bubble of air in its stomach.","It stores the stinging cells of the man o' war it eats, and uses them for its own defense.","Its blue side faces up and its silver side down, hiding it from above and below."],"lesson":"Turn a threat into strength. The blue dragon makes its prey's stingers its own.","about":"Glaucus atlanticus (common names include the blue sea dragon, sea swallow, blue angel, blue glaucus, dragon slug, blue dragon, blue sea slug, and blue ocean slug) is a species of sea slug in the family Glaucidae. These sea slugs live in the pelagic zone (open ocean), where they float upside-down by using the surface tension of the water to stay afloat.","description":"Species of mollusc","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Glaucus_atlanticus_1_cropped.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Taro Taylor from Sydney, Australia","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glaucus_atlanticus_1_cropped.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaucus_atlanticus","sci":"Glaucus atlanticus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":391},
{"name":"Lettuce Sea Slug","page":"Elysia_crispata","group":"Mollusc","where":"Shallow, clear reefs of the Caribbean and the tropical western Atlantic","diet":"Algae","facts":["As an adult, it keeps the green parts of the algae it eats, and they make food for it for over a month.","Its ruffled sides look like a leaf of lettuce.","It eats many kinds of algae, unlike its pickier relatives."],"lesson":"Keep what's useful. The lettuce sea slug turns its meal into its own solar panels.","about":"Elysia crispata, common name the lettuce sea slug or lettuce slug, is a large and colorful species of sea slug, a marine gastropod mollusk. The lettuce slug resembles a nudibranch, but it is not closely related to that clade of gastropods; it is classified as a sacoglossan.","description":"Species of gastropod","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Lettuce_Sea_Slug_11-03-2006.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"LASZLO ILYES from Cleveland, Ohio, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lettuce_Sea_Slug_11-03-2006.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elysia_crispata","sci":"Elysia crispata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":392},
{"name":"Pacific Banana Slug","page":"Ariolimax_columbianus","group":"Mollusc","where":"Damp coastal forests from California to Alaska","diet":"Leaves, fungi and dead plants","facts":["It's the second-largest land slug in the world, up to 25 cm (10 inches) long.","It's often bright yellow, but can be green, brown, tan or white.","It helps spread the seeds of forest plants."],"lesson":"Slow can still matter. The banana slug quietly helps plant the forest.","about":"Ariolimax columbianus, the Pacific banana slug, is a species of slug found on the Pacific coast of North America. It is the second-largest species of terrestrial slug in the world, growing up to 25 centimeters (9.8 in) long. An important function of this particular species is the role it plays in seed dispersion.","description":"Species of slug","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Banana_Slug-1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Thomas Schoch","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banana_Slug-1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariolimax_columbianus","sci":"Ariolimax columbianus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":393},
{"name":"Giant African Land Snail","page":"Achatina_fulica","group":"Mollusc","where":"Native to East Africa; invasive in many warm places, including Florida","diet":"A wide range of plants, from crops and flowers to weeds","facts":["A grown snail can be 20 cm (8 inches) long, one of the largest land snails.","It turned up in Miami in 1966 and 2011, and each time took nearly a decade to wipe out.","It eats crops, flowers, native plants, weeds and even dead plants."],"lesson":"Watch what spreads. The giant African land snail is the most common invasive snail in the world.","about":"Lissachatina fulica is a species of large land snail that belongs in the subfamily Achatininae of the family Achatinidae. It is also known as the giant African land snail. It shares the common name \"giant African snail\" with other species of snails such as Achatina achatina and Archachatina marginata.","description":"Species of land snail","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Snail_in_Ubud%2C_Bali%2C_2010_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Snail_in_Ubud%2C_Bali%2C_2010_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Alexander R. Jenner","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snail_in_Ubud,_Bali,_2010_(1).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lissachatina_fulica","sci":"Lissachatina fulica","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":394},
{"name":"Portuguese Man O' War","page":"Portuguese_man_o'_war","group":"Cnidarian","where":"The Atlantic, Indian and Pacific Oceans; winds often drive it onto beaches","diet":"Small fish and shrimp","facts":["It looks like a jellyfish, but it's a siphonophore, a colony of tiny animals working as one.","Its gas-filled float works like a sail.","Its stinging tentacles can trail 30 meters (100 feet)."],"lesson":"Teamwork makes one body. The man o' war is many small animals acting as one.","about":"The Portuguese man o' war (Physalia physalis), also known as the man-of-war, bluebottle, or sometimes called Caravela Portuguesa (a name of Portuguese origin), is a marine hydrozoan found in the Atlantic, Indian, and Pacific oceans. While it is typically considered the only species in its genus, Physalia, and family, Physaliidae, genetic evidence suggests there may be more.","description":"Species of marine cnidarian","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Portuguese_Man-O-War_%28Physalia_physalis%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Image courtesy of Islands in the Sea 2002, NOAA/OER.","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portuguese_Man-O-War_(Physalia_physalis).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_man_o%27_war","sci":"Physalia physalis","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":395},
{"name":"Lion's Mane Jellyfish","page":"Lion's_mane_jellyfish","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Cold waters of the North Atlantic, the Arctic and the North Pacific","diet":"Plankton, fish and other jellyfish","facts":["It's one of the largest known jellyfish.","Its tentacles can be more than 30 meters (100 feet) long.","It's the culprit in a Sherlock Holmes mystery."],"lesson":"Reach far. The biggest lion's mane ever measured had tentacles longer than a blue whale.","about":"The lion's mane jellyfish (Cyanea capillata) is one of the largest known species of jellyfish. Its range is confined to cold, boreal waters of the Arctic, northern Atlantic, and northern Pacific Oceans. It is common in the English Channel, Irish Sea, North Sea, and in western Scandinavian waters south to Kattegat and Øresund.","description":"Species of jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Lion%27s_mane_jellyfish_in_Gullmarn_fjord_at_S%C3%A4mstad_7.jpg/1920px-Lion%27s_mane_jellyfish_in_Gullmarn_fjord_at_S%C3%A4mstad_7.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"W.carter","license":"CC0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion%27s_mane_jellyfish_in_Gullmarn_fjord_at_S%C3%A4mstad_7.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion%27s_mane_jellyfish","sci":"Cyanea capillata","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":396},
{"name":"Australian Box Jellyfish","page":"Chironex_fleckeri","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Coastal waters of northern Australia and the Indo-Pacific","diet":"Shrimp and small fish","facts":["One jellyfish is said to carry enough venom to kill 60 adults.","Its tentacles, up to 3 meters (10 feet) long, carry millions of stinging cells.","It's nicknamed the sea wasp."],"lesson":"Respect what you can't see. A sea wasp's sting comes from millions of tiny darts.","about":"Chironex fleckeri, commonly known as the Australian box jelly and nicknamed the sea wasp, is a species of extremely venomous box jellyfish found in coastal waters from northern Australia and Papua New Guinea to Indonesia, East Timor, Cambodia, Malaysia, Singapore, the Philippines and Vietnam.","description":"Species of jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Avispa_marina_cropped.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Avispa marina.jpg: Guido Gautsch, Toyota, Japan derivative work: Mithril (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avispa_marina_cropped.png"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chironex_fleckeri","sci":"Chironex fleckeri","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":397},
{"name":"Immortal Jellyfish","page":"Turritopsis_dohrnii","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Temperate and tropical seas, first found in the Mediterranean","diet":"Plankton","facts":["It can turn back into a young polyp and start its life over.","That makes it biologically immortal: in theory it never has to die of old age.","It may be spreading around the world in ships' ballast water."],"lesson":"It's never too late to start over. The immortal jellyfish begins again when life gets hard.","about":"Turritopsis dohrnii, commonly known as the immortal jellyfish, is a species of small, biologically immortal Medusozoa (jellyfish) found worldwide in temperate to tropic waters. It is one of the few known cases of an animal capable of completely reverting to a sexually immature, sedentary polyps colonial stage after having reached sexual maturity as a solitary individual.","description":"Species of small, biologically immortal jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Turritopsis_dohrnii.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Bachware","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turritopsis_dohrnii.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turritopsis_dohrnii","sci":"Turritopsis dohrnii","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":398},
{"name":"Staghorn Coral","page":"Staghorn_coral","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Coral reefs of the Caribbean, the Bahamas and Florida","diet":"Food made by algae living in it, plus plankton","facts":["Its thick branches can grow over 2 meters (6.5 feet) tall and look like a stag's antlers.","It's one of the fastest-growing corals in the western Atlantic.","It's critically endangered."],"lesson":"Rebuild what's lost. Staghorn coral grows back fast when it's given a chance.","about":"The staghorn coral (Acropora cervicornis) is a branching, stony coral, within the Order Scleractinia. It is characterized by thick, upright branches which can grow in excess of 2 meters (6.5 ft) in height and resemble the antlers of a stag, hence the name, Staghorn.","description":"Species of coral","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hertshoon.jpg/1920px-Hertshoon.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Albert Kok at Dutch Wikipedia (Original text: albert kok)","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hertshoon.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staghorn_coral","sci":"Acropora cervicornis","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":399},
{"name":"Upside-down Jellyfish","page":"Cassiopea_xamachana","group":"Cnidarian","where":"Shallow mangroves and lagoons of Florida and the Caribbean","diet":"Food made by algae living in it, plus plankton","facts":["It rests upside down on the seabed, its bell working like a suction cup.","Algae living in its tissues make food from sunlight for it.","It surrounds itself with mucus full of tiny stinging bodies called cassiosomes."],"lesson":"Face the light. The upside-down jellyfish turns toward the sun that feeds it.","about":"Cassiopea xamachana, commonly known as the upside-down jellyfish, is a species of jellyfish in the family Cassiopeidae. It is found in warm parts of the western Atlantic Ocean, the Caribbean Sea, and the Gulf of Mexico. It was first described by the American marine biologist Henry Bryant Bigelow in 1892.","description":"Species of jellyfish","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Upside-down_jellyfish_%28Cassiopea_xamachana%29.jpg/1920px-Upside-down_jellyfish_%28Cassiopea_xamachana%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Marco Almbauer","license":"Public domain","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upside-down_jellyfish_(Cassiopea_xamachana).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassiopea_xamachana","sci":"Cassiopea xamachana","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":400},
{"name":"Crown-of-thorns Starfish","page":"Crown-of-thorns_starfish","group":"Echinoderm","where":"Coral reefs of the Indian and Pacific Oceans","diet":"Coral","facts":["It can have up to 21 arms.","It's named for the venomous, thorn-like spines that cover its back.","Its stomach is unusually large for its body."],"lesson":"Balance matters. A few crown-of-thorns are natural; too many can wreck a reef.","about":"The crown-of-thorns starfish (Acanthaster planci) is a large starfish that preys upon hard coral polyps. The crown-of-thorns starfish, frequently abbreviated to COTS, receives its name from venomous, thornlike spines that cover its upper surface, resembling the biblical crown of thorns. It is one of the largest starfish in the world.","description":"Species of starfish","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Acanthaster_planci_460468243_%28closeup%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"John Hepburn","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acanthaster_planci_460468243_(closeup).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown-of-thorns_starfish","sci":"Acanthaster planci","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":401},
{"name":"Sunflower Sea Star","page":"Sunflower_sea_star","group":"Echinoderm","where":"The North Pacific coast, from Alaska to California","diet":"Sea urchins, clams and snails","facts":["It usually has 16 to 24 arms.","It's among the largest sea stars, up to 1 meter (3 feet) across.","Its numbers crashed starting in 2013, from a wasting disease."],"lesson":"Every part of the web matters. As sunflower sea stars vanished, sea urchins boomed and began threatening kelp forests.","about":"Pycnopodia helianthoides, commonly known as the sunflower sea star, is a large sea star found in the northeastern Pacific Ocean. The only species of its genus, it is among the largest sea stars in the world, with a maximum arm span of 1 m (3.3 ft). Adult sunflower sea stars usually have 16 to 24 limbs.","description":"Species of echinoderm","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Pycnopodia_helianthoides_SLO_CA.jpg/1920px-Pycnopodia_helianthoides_SLO_CA.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jerry Kirkhart from Los Osos, Calif.","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pycnopodia_helianthoides_SLO_CA.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_sea_star","sci":"Pycnopodia helianthoides","status":"CR","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":402},
{"name":"Purple Sea Urchin","page":"Strongylocentrotus_purpuratus","group":"Echinoderm","where":"The Pacific coast of North America","diet":"Kelp and algae","facts":["When its predators vanish, it can eat kelp forests down to bare rock.","It can live as long as 70 years.","It lives along the Pacific coast from Mexico to British Columbia."],"lesson":"Too much of anything is a problem. Unchecked urchins can wipe out a whole kelp forest.","about":"Strongylocentrotus purpuratus is a species of sea urchin in the family Strongylocentrotidae, which is commonly known as the purple sea urchin or Pacific purple sea urchin. It lives along the eastern edge of the Pacific Ocean extending from Ensenada, Mexico, to British Columbia, Canada.","description":"Species of sea urchin","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Urchin_%289398869414%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Ed Bierman from CA, usa","license":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urchin_(9398869414).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strongylocentrotus_purpuratus","sci":"Strongylocentrotus purpuratus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":403},
{"name":"Medicinal Leech","page":"Hirudo_medicinalis","group":"Worm","where":"Weedy freshwater pools and ditches of Europe, east into Asia as far as Kazakhstan","diet":"Blood","facts":["Since the 1970s, surgeons have used it again, to help blood flow after operations like reattaching fingers.","Its saliva has hirudin, which stops blood from clotting.","It has three saw-like jaws with about 100 sharp edges for cutting skin."],"lesson":"Old tools can still help. The medicinal leech is still used by surgeons today.","about":"Hirudo medicinalis, or the European medicinal leech, is one of several species of leeches used as medicinal leeches. Other species of Hirudo sometimes also used as medicinal leeches include H. orientalis, H. troctina, and H. verbana. The Asian medicinal leech includes Hirudinaria manillensis, and the North American medicinal leech is Macrobdella decora.","description":"Species of annelid worm","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/European_Medicinal_Leech_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_378486881_on_26_November_2024.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"(c) Георгий Виноградов (Georgy Vinogradov), some rights reserved (CC BY)","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_Medicinal_Leech_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_378486881_on_26_November_2024.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hirudo_medicinalis","sci":"Hirudo medicinalis","status":"NT","mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":404},
{"name":"Giant Tube Worm","page":"Riftia_pachyptila","group":"Worm","where":"Hot vents on the deep floor of the Pacific Ocean","diet":"Food made by bacteria living inside it","facts":["It has no mouth or gut; bacteria inside it make food from chemicals.","It can reach 3 meters (almost 10 feet) long.","Its red plume is full of hemoglobin, like our blood."],"lesson":"Thrive where no one expects. The giant tube worm lives in the dark deep sea, around hot vents full of sulfur chemicals.","about":"Riftia is a monotypic genus of polychaete annelid worms in the Siboglinidae family, which contains the sole species Riftia pachyptila, commonly known as the giant tubeworm or giant beardworm. R. pachyptila is found in association with geologically active regions of the deep sea.","description":"Giant tube worm (species of annelid)","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Campagne_HOT_-_Vers_g%C3%A9ants_%28Riftia_pachyptila%29_%28Ifremer_00530-64223_-_52381%29.jpg/1920px-Campagne_HOT_-_Vers_g%C3%A9ants_%28Riftia_pachyptila%29_%28Ifremer_00530-64223_-_52381%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"null","license":"CC BY 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campagne_HOT_-_Vers_g%C3%A9ants_(Riftia_pachyptila)_(Ifremer_00530-64223_-_52381).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riftia","sci":"Riftia","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":405},
{"name":"Christmas Tree Worm","page":"Spirobranchus_giganteus","group":"Worm","where":"Tropical coral reefs around the world","diet":"Plankton","facts":["Its two spiral plumes look like little Christmas trees.","The plumes catch food and also work as gills.","It plugs its tube with a little lid when it pulls back inside."],"lesson":"Know when to pull back. The Christmas tree worm ducks into its tube and shuts the door.","about":"Spirobranchus giganteus, commonly known as the Christmas tree worm, is a polychaete worm belonging to the family Serpulidae building calcareous tubes. The S. giganteus lives in coral reefs in the Caribbean.","description":"Species of marine tube worm","photo":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Spirobranchus_giganteus_%28Red_and_white_christmas_tree_worm%29.jpg/1920px-Spirobranchus_giganteus_%28Red_and_white_christmas_tree_worm%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Nhobgood Nick Hobgood","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spirobranchus_giganteus_(Red_and_white_christmas_tree_worm).jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirobranchus_giganteus","sci":"Spirobranchus giganteus","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":406},
{"name":"Common Earthworm","page":"Lumbricus_terrestris","group":"Worm","where":"Soil of Europe, and brought to North America","diet":"Dead leaves and soil","facts":["In much of Europe it's the largest native earthworm, 20 to 25 cm (8 to 10 inches) long.","At night it may crawl up to 19 meters (60 feet) from its burrow.","It pulls dead leaves down into the soil."],"lesson":"Humble work feeds the world. Earthworms quietly make the soil rich.","about":"Lumbricus terrestris is a large, reddish worm species thought to be native to Western Europe, now widely distributed around the world (along with several other lumbricids). In some areas where it is an introduced species, some people consider it to be a significant pest for out-competing native worms.","description":"Species of annelid worm","photo":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Regenwurm1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Michael Linnenbach","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regenwurm1.jpg"},"wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumbricus_terrestris","sci":"Lumbricus terrestris","status":null,"mass":null,"length":null,"life":null,"n":407}
